      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.,,</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.,,</p>
        <p>DiAR NFILLIE,</p>
        <p>Khaki has been forging its way ahead lately and has invaded the millinery department. At the moment, toques of khaki cloth or soft felt, trimmed with white strappings or pipings, are having a large sale for everyday wear. They are extremely simple, the material being folded to fit the head, and arranged in loops or loose rosettes in front, with the addition of a couple of smart buckles and a quill or two, perhaps. The same type of toque is also Made to match cloth gowns of various colours, and it certainly looks exceed- ingly neat. Felt hats in pale grey—almost white —are almost invariably popular in early spring, and this year is n 6 exception to the rule. A very pretty style of hat in the lightest possible shade of grey felt has a rather high crown, a wide flexible brim that droops back and front, and is trimmed with scarves of white silk with fringed ends that fall on the hair behind. Auriculas, primulas, and crocusses are less hackneyed for trimming spring millinery than violets, yet the latter seem never out of favour, and not a few of the light-coloured felts are adorned with large wreaths of them simply laid on the brim, and backed by a few folds of chiffon, gauze, or soft silk. Feathers still form a prominent ornament to the chapeaux.</p>
        <p>Blouses are very, pretty just now, the sailo shape prevailing. Ribbon, by-the-bye, is to play an important part in our dress this coming season, and many are the delightful colourings and patterns shown us in ribbon lore to meet this demand. Lace too will be a great feature, and one which all may welcome, it being one of the few items in sartorial wherewithal that adds to the charm of appearance in old and young alike. The varieties of lace now obtaining are immensely numerous, and we are not to be tied down to any particular make on pain of being otherwise demods, so now is the time to dig out all the lace treasuree we may possess and put them on active service.</p>
        <p>Skirts are in an undecided state, the plain tight type having vanished among fashionable folk, while its successor, the pleated skirt, has not found general favour yet. A gown of deep pervanche blue cloth I have lately seen at a dressmaker's had a skirt which was an agreeable compromise be- tween the two styles. It was close-fitting, but not =gracefully so, and the fulness at the top was arranged in small stitched pleats on the hips, but in wider ones at the back. A narrow band of blue panne with slots of steel embroidery at intervals was sewn on the skirt so as to simulate a flounce, and the same trimming was introduced on the revers of the plain close-fitting bodice, which had a tiny scalloped basque, and a vest of cream guipure over satin.</p>
        <p>• There are many small economies which can now be practised and I will give you one or two which I consider suitable. If rubbed gently with bread- crumbs after each time of wearing, gloves will re- main clean much longer than would be the case otherwise. See that buttons are well sewn on and the holes kept neatly mended, and fold them in white tissue-paper when they are not in use. When going to a dance draw a pair of old silk ones over your good ones, to protect them from injury on the way. Old kid shoes that will no longer take a restorer can be renovated by painting them with enamel paint, heels and all, and by sowing on a tiny bow of the same colour. Cheap little fancy chains that have begun to look discoloured and brassy can be dipped again by a jeweller to look like new, and at a small cost, or they will look quite well if painted with one of the gold or silver paints that can be bout anywhere. A nice um- brella handle can be -transferred to half-a-dozen different new sticks, while handkerchiefs torn os the border can be cut smaller and re-hemstitched. Your loving</p>
        <p>JEN 11.</p>
