        <p>readers of The Warrington Examiner.</p>
        <p>scare, for which, once in a way, it must be admitt e that there were very real and substantial grounds for the warning. After that we were told that several cases of typhoid had been traced to the eating of watercresa. Now the "Medical Press and Circular" tells us a similar danger lurks in celery. Some risks it says, must always remain, in the case of all vegetables Which are grown in highly fertilised ground, and eaten in an uncooked Feat e. If this is true it is a disquieting suggestion, especially in view of the approaching season when salads are so I much appr, elated, becatse, if celery and watercrese can take uti typhoid germs in infected material used as a fertiliser, why not lettuces, endive, and other vegetables, which are necessary to make up salad nishee ? If there are any adequate grounds for this latest note of warn- ing, it is clear we must regard all such dishes in future with a considerable amount of suspicion. Few more curious stories have been told in court than that which rcc-ntly came betore Sir Francis Jenne. This related to the will of a Miss Moore, which had been de- vised in an extraordinary way. Suffering from paralysis and without power to write or even speak, it seemed impossible for her to dispose of her property. She how- ever still retained her mental faculties, and the method adopted by the solicitor was to write out on one sec of cards all the items of her considerabie property,and upon another set all the names of her relatives. She was then enabled to indicate by signs, the manner in which she desired to distribute her property, and Sir Francis Jeune triel no difficulty in pronouncing for the validity of the will.</p>
        <p>" brIADGE."</p>
