      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WARRINGTON COMPANY PROMOTION.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON COMPANY PROMOTION.</p>
        <p>Failure of Beaman and Deas Limited.</p>
        <p>At tbe Board of Trade offices, London on Monday rreetings of creditors and shareholders were held under a winding-up order made against Beaman and 11 as, Lkmited. Mr. A. S. Cully, senior assistant registrar, presided. It appears that the company was incorporated in June, 1895, with a nominal capital of £30.000 in 600 shares of £5O each, and the Official Receiver was in- formed that the gentlemen interested in the promotion. were Mresrs. Dees. Beaman, Tunstall, Parkinson, and Griffiths. It was formed as the Beaman and Deas Synoicate, Limited, for the purpose of acquiring from Messrs. Beaman and Deas, of Warrington, their British and foreign patents and patent rights relating to improvements in destructor furnaces so carry en the business of contractors, builders, en- gineers, &amp;c. It was apparently intended that after demonstrating the patents the undertaking should be old to a large company to be formed. In March, 1896, tire capital was increased to £40,000, and in June. 1898. it was further increased to £60,000. All the directors appeared to have held the requisite number of shares, but in several instances the shares were acquired by them as nominees of the vendors, and a question as to their liability might arise. The purchase consideration was £26,300 payable £3OO in cash and £26,000 in shares. The actual amount received by the vendors was £15,000, the balance going to the directors and others interested' in the promction. The Chairman further reported that the purchase prices appeared to have been agreed be- tween the vendors and the directors, there being no valuation. No share prospectus was issued. The work of companies at the cßmmencement was to a certain ex- tent experimental, an 7 the directors stated that the first contracts were undertaken at unremunerative prices. The balance sheet up to November 30',h, 1898, showed a deficiency of £9,634, and this had increased at the date of the winding up order to £37.700. The only working capital the company bad was £4,000, and the sum of £14,000 was raised upon debentures. Ultimately a re- ceiver for debenture holders was appointed by the court. The receiver had obtained an offer for the whole of the underaking for £14.450, book debts to the amount of £9,774 being included in the assets, and an application to the court for leave to sell at the price named was pending. It was proposed that the £14,450 should be paid in cash and partly in shares of another company. The unsecured indebtedness was retureed at £7,453,and and in the event of no better offer being forthcoming than the one that was now pending it did not appear that anything would be available far creditors after satisfying the debenture holders. So far as the OEcial Receiver could ascertain the trading had been carded on at a loss throughout. Mr. D. L. Chalmers, chartered accountant, and the Official Receiver were severally nominated to the effi?e of Pquidator, and the matter was referred to the court f)r decision.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
