        <p>dirsteVe.” Then in a wila, nystericai outntirst, sne cried" Oh, don't go, teve I Don't go You ..,InuAtn't oc I " ne soothed , her tenderly, and pesently she to became more composed. There was Der ich hurrying and scurrying about the platform. Tto train was signalled. In a few moments it wor..kl be in the station. The last words must be spok: n. "You will write often. Steve, won'; you? You must do. I •must feel that you aTe ,tver near to me. I shall be so lonely. I cannot 1 tar to think of it. I must feel that you are near to me, I must think of you always, or else—" She stopped abruptly. She dared not complete the sentence and tell him that she mistrusted herself. Her words conveyed a disturbing theught to her lover, and his agitation betrayed the intensity of his feeling. "You will miss me, Ella. I know if, I feel it, but you must be courageous; be st! Dng for my sake. Remember that lam toiling for 3 ou, solely for you. You will always think lovi: uly of me, Ella ? " A timid, scared look flitted across the girl's face for a moment. "Ella, you will ever think of me, only of me? . . . You will never be anything :ore than civil to that fellow Cartleigh. You will not, Ella, eh? You must not. You are compelled to be civil to him, I know, but nothing more. Beware of him. I cannot trust him, nor can yon. Good- bye, dear, good-bye. When next we meet we shall be united for ever, my girl." The panting engine rushed by the platform and the long line of carriages drew up. For a few moments there was much noise and bustle; excited passengers asking many questions of busy porters, officials rushing hither and thither, trucks rumbling along, doors banging, and, above all, the loud hiss of steam from the throat of the iron horse. Steve entered a third-class compartment, and leaning out of the window, again kissed the tear- stained face and murmured words of comfort. Then with a quick hand-grip they separated as the guard's whistle blew. A last fond look, a last wave of the hand, and Steve was quickly whirled away as the iron monster steamed southward. . Aboard the Castle liner he had much to occupy his attention. He had, in the first place, to see the steward in charge of the third-class cabins„ His luggage had also to be attended to. He spent , much time in inspecting the great ship, his floating home for a period, and marvelled greatly at the sumptuous appearance of the first and second-class saloons, the smoking and music-rooms, and the well- appointed, cosy cabins. In his own part of the ship, / forward, he found a clean, comfortable dining- saloon. on which his cabin opened. Below this deck he explored the steerage quarters. On the following morning as Steve came on deck all was quiet, save for the gentle swish-swish of water. The dock Ei were far behind, and the Arundel Castle was steadily speeding down the Thames. This was a day of days for Steve. It opened out a new life for him, new scenes, new faces, new impressions. Henectorward he realised that he would have a new vo.rld to face entirely different from the little world of Dalesworth, in which until now his whole life head been lived. Throughout the greater part of the day land was in sight. About midnight the vessel dropped anchor in Southampton Water, and here remained for several hours. More passengers came aboard on Sattfrday, and not until noon, after the mails had been received, were preparations made for final departure from England's shores. Then quickly the anchors were drawn up, the Mighty engines revolved the twin- screws astern, and slowly the big ship turned towards the Channel again. Past the Isle of NTight the vessel steamed, and soon the Needles were lost to view. As the night-shadows fell, Stephen Hart leant over the bulwarks and gazed lingeringly at the low line of cliffs now dim and cloud-like. "Saturday night," he mused. "Ella will be sitting at home now with her mother, thinking of me, as I now think of her. 'Tis but a short fort- night since we walked to Daisy Nook and I spoke of my plans, and now—here I am amidst this waste of waters, in this little ship-world, with new faces about me; all strange faces, not one here whom I can call friend or comrade; and Ella—ah she will be terribly lonely, too, but she has friends to cheer her; she has her mother." His ruminations were suddenly disturbed. A strange, uneasy, painful feeling possessed him. His right hand closed tightly, and with his left he gripped his coat nervously. "What is it ?" he asked himself, as a sharp pain seemed to strike through his whole body. "Ah 1 Where was I ? Where was I? I have it. I was thinking of Ella in her loneliness, and I thought—she was not alone! 0 God What is this strange feeling which comes upon me ? " He pressed his hand to his heated brow. "I feel that Ella is not safe, not well, or something Ah ! I know what it means. That scamp Cartleigh will trouble her. He will. I know it. I feel it. But Ella will be faithful to me. She will be true to me. I can trust her. She is not false." The last words he uttered half-aloud, and a Polish Jew, standing near, stared at him wonderingly. "I'm wandering. I'm upset and light-headed," said the tortured young fellow. "I'll stand in the breeze and let it sweep these silly ideas out of my head. 4</p>
        <p>Up on the forecastle deck, where the cool, refreshing breeze toned and exhilarated him, he laughed at his hallucination, as he now regarded it. How foolish, how mean seemed his suspicions and fears. How dare he for one moment suspect that Ella would be false to him? Her love would be as enduring as the stars above him. Though the poles divided them she would be constant and true to him. So he assured himself as he stood there looking out on the black water, his face yet turned towards the unseen land. And as the look-out man in the prow called out his cheery "All's well 1" so Stephen Hart, as he descended to his cabin, gave answer from his inmost being: "All's well !"</p>
        <p>20 d ti in fr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER IV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IV.</p>
        <p>While Stephen Hart voyaged to the new country events most momentous to him were happening in the old country he had left behind. On the Satur- day evening of his departure Ralph Cartleigh sat in the parlour of the Rose and Crown at Dalesworth, his feet on the mantel, a cigar in his mouth, and at his elbow a glass of whisky. He was the only occupant of the room, The hour was yet early for the usual throng of tipplers to visit the hostelry. Cartleigh knew that none would disturb the sanctity of his apartment for at least another hour. "Seven o'clock, he said, as he drew out his watch and held it before him a moment. "I'll wait here until half-past, then if Barrett doesn't turn up I'll go in for another form of entertainment." He puffed vigorously at the cigar, enshrouding himself in a cloud of smoke so dense that the declining sun shining in at the window appeared quite red, as seen through a November fog. "Oh, bother it!" exclaimed Cartleigh at length, as he suddenly dropped his legs to the floor and walked towards the window. "Hanged if I'll wait here for Barrett. It's too fine a night for the Folly. "Ugh! To be stuffed inside a music-hall in the middle of July 1 Not likely; no, not for all the Junos in the world. Barrett reckons the Sisters Sparkle are regular dazzlers, perfect scorchers; Junos, he termed 'em. Why, I know a Juno who, I'll bet, will be on show to-night, and I'll back her against the pick of the Folly crowd. We breed Junos in our weaving-shed. Gad, we do!" He returned to the fire-place, looked at his reflection in the mirror, and smiled approvingly. "I should be a precious fool to go down to Manchester—grimy, slimy Manchester—on 4 fair July night like this, on the off-chance of getting an intro' to the dear, giddy Sparkle damsels, when here, within a stone's-throw, is a maid whose sweet company I can have all to myself. Egad!" he chuckled, "I wonder how that fortune-hunting fellow feels to-night! Home-sick, I suppose, and— love-sick. Love-sick! Bah! As if a clown like him, a big piecer, could love, and above all, could love a girl like that Ella Rayburn. I'll shew him how to love! Ah, she's a stunner! She is,tideed, a regular stunner! Whatever, now, could she see in that benighted big piecer ? Let's see, what did I call him ? "</p>
        <p>He tried to recall his conversation with Den, the warehouseman. "Let me see. Ben was talking about Africa and— Ah I have it! Steve, the Outlander! That's it. Oh, lor ! That name fits the sublime, would-be minder down to the ground. Steve, the Outlander! Sounds quite—ah !—quite heroic. Good title for a hero. Ha! Ha! Think I'll go in for play-Nvriting, be a second Sydney Grundy. I've got a rattling title, and that's more than one-half the difficulty in play-writing, I understand. Steve; the Outlander! The name likes me well. Ah, we'll have a play, my ambi- tious mule-tender! You're not a half-bad mule yourself, my dear absent friend. Yes, we'll have a play, a regular tip-top melodrama—not too mellow but quite dramatic. I'111)e the villain of the piece; Ella, fair Ella Rayburn, shall be the lovely heroine; you. my buck. shall be the hero</p>
        <p>the good young man wno alea, ana, egaa, you shall die! The villain shall be the conqueror in this play, my absent, misguided, fortune-hunting friend. A. fine play it shall be, a regular thriller,</p>
        <p>a tip-topper which shall make the gods howl with delight. Yo, oh! The idea likes me exceedingly well ! It's fine.! It's superb 1" In.his excitement Cartleigh had allowed his cigar to burn out. His glass, too, was quite empty. He rang the bell. 'As usual," he said to the barman, and, the liquor being brought, he took it up, held it to the sunlight, winked at it slyly, then, as the door closed, said aloud:</p>
        <p>"Here's to my mellow drama—very mellow— Steve, the Outlander Confusion on the hero 1 Success to the villain! Long may he wave! The villain still pursues her! " The glass was again empty, and Ralph Cartleigh was as full of good spirits, so he assured the barman, as mortal man could wish to be. "If Barrett comes, tell him I couldn't wait any longer. I've an urgent appointment. Sorry I couldn't wait for him. You'll not forget ? " "No, I'll tell him, sir."</p>
        <p>Ere he stepped from the doorway of the Rose and Cartleigh glanced quickly up and down the .oignway, then, perceiving that there was no one of 'his acquaintance in sight, he walked speedily down the road. He looked very smart and dapper this evening. His stylish, light tweed suit fitted his tall, lithe figure perfectly. Spotless white- linen, tan shoes, and Trilby hat set jauntily on his head, gave him the appearance of a swell fitted more for the promenade of a seaside resort than for the smoky town of Dalesworth. His face was radiant; he seemed particc lay pleased with himself; he even appeared to be IA !as4ll with the folks whom he encountered.</p>
        <p>The district through which he was passing cop:, tamed many dingy-looking ironworks, bald, xtre. looking factories, and long, dreary rows of !mall houses. The surroundings were not idyllic. The light tweed suit seemed quite out of place, but the man within it ,was evidently not out of keeping with his environment.</p>
        <p>Ralph Cartleigh had spent most of his life in Dalesworth. Unlike the other members of his family, he had mingled with the people of the dis- trict, especially with the sporting fraternity, which assembled each Saturday at the local coursing grounds. Apart from these gentry, he had a pro- found contempt for the Dalesworth folk, particu- larly that section who laboured in his father's factory. "How could he respect his father's hands' ?No more," he observed, jocularly, "than I can respect tay father's feet, and 'tis little respect I have for them, or for his revered footsteps. The beaten track may be very well for these beaten hands' of hie but for myself, well, I'll make a track of m: own."</p>
        <p>lany of the workpeople at Fernleigh Mill recog. n ed him as he walked along, but . few were they v,. to nodded familiarly to him, and these few were ignored by the haughty young fellow. After proceeding along the main road for a con- siderable distance, he turned to the left down a by-street which stretched away towards green fields. A group of children with daisy garlands in their hats came along merrily singing a school song. The scent of new-mown hay came with them. "Ah, that is fragrant 1" said Cartleigh, as he passed the little ones. "Sweet and fragrant new- mown hay and daisies. Better than those music- hall' daisies' with straw-coloured hair. If Igo a few yards further and see the daisies and ha; 'n the fields, I shall burst forth into poetry, I'm 8.419 shall. Let's see; June-moon, spoon-loon. spoon,' that's what I've come out for, to spou with Miss June, or to be more correct, Juno."</p>
        <p>He was indeed in a gay humour. He strolled more slowly now as he approached the end of the street.</p>
        <p>"Number one round the corner, facing the fields, is where Miss Rayburn lives, lam told. Ah 1 Sensible girl, sensible mater They at least have more sense than some of the folks, and like to see the green fields. The other green folks prefer to see black, bricky houses and factory walls. Juno has a soul above the common, evidently. She has an eye for the beautiful, for the poetic. That's why I shall impress her; she'll have an eye for me, two eyes in fact; and as for poetry, I flatter myself I can be as romantic as the most fastidious Juno coulVesire. Hellos, here we are 1"</p>
        <p>The street merged into a field-path. To the right, overlooking the meadows was a long row of two storey dwellings. At the door of the first house a stout woman of middle age was standing, gazing out across the fields towards the Old Roman-road. As Ralph Cartleigh was about to go forward, , woman came out of an adjoining house and entered into conversation with the stout matron.</p>
        <p>He stepped back and listened. "Are yo' lookin' for some'dy, Mrs. Rayburn ? l' "No, not pertikler. I was just watchin' our Ella yonder. Poor lass I she feels gradely lonely to-neet." "I'm sure she does," said the first speaker. "When all t' other lasses are off with their lads, she'll feel it keener nor ever to be left alone by hersel'." "Yes, that's just it, Mrs. Merrydew. She wasn't for goin' out at all to-neet, welkin' by hersel', she said; but I persuaded her for to go at last. It's no good mopin' in th' house on a fine fleet like this. See you, yon's her! She's crossin' th' Roman-road, an goin' o'er to'rd th' Nook. That's just th' road 'at her an' Steve went th' last Baturday neet they were out together." "Yes, an' I'll lay owt 'at she'll walk o'er every inch of th' way thinkin' of him an meitherin about him. Aye, well, lads will be lads, an' lasses will be lasses. And having delivered herself of this sage observation, Mrs. Merrydew seemed dig. posed to end the conversation, turning to re-enter tier domicile. But Mrs. Rayburn detained her. . "Yes, as you say, Mrs. Merrydew, lads and lasses will be as 'they, are, which is only human nature; an' if they'd only get their courtin' days over an' done with, I shouldn't mind; but when they go dilly-dallyin' o'er th' business for years, as our Ella an' Steve Hart has done, an' then don't seem to know just what they re'lly think of one another, an' one goes away on a gold-huntin', wildgoose chase, an' t'other stops at home an' frets a bit, when all th' time I don't believe she thinks all that much about him—well, when it gets to that pitch, I think lads an' lasses ought for t' be learnt to cpurt as we used to court : quick, an' get it done with." Widow Rayburn was quite exhausted by her lengthy speech, and panted and gasped for breath. In her excitement she had pitched her voice so high that the listener who stood at the strear-end heard distinctly every word without straining for- ward to catch her speech, as he had previously done. He crept a little nearer still, keeping well out of observation. When he first learnt that Tbar Rayburn had gone unattended for a stroll, he Vic; about to follow her; but the conversation of the two women proved too powerful a magnet, and he remained in th b hope that he might glean further information concerning the girl and her absent lover. "Yes. courtin' were diff'rent in our time," re- sumed Mrs. Merrydew. "It were soon begun, an' soon ended. But young folks nowadays like to stretch it out, an' make it last o'er a long time.; an', perhaps, if we were young again, we should happen do the same. Courtin' days is th' best days, I reckon. Chaps are rare an' diff'rent after they're wed; an', I dar' say, they'll say women are, too." "Yes, it's much as you say, Mrs. Merrydew," said the widow. "Do's not as I want to hurry our Ella into wed life, or get her courtin' days over so soon. In fact, I'd rather she didn't hurry at all with th' chap she's taken up with. Steve's not a bad fellow, you must know, Mrs. Merrydew; but I don't think he's good enough for our Ella. I I , don't want to see her slavin' after she's wed, like a lot of th' lasses does, goin,' to th' fact'ry again, an' sich like; an' yet, you see, being wed to any- ' one like Steve there's nowt else for it but that." "But if he gets a fortin' abroad, Mrs. Rayburn, how will that be ? " "Oh, that'll be right enough, if he does get one; t I've my doubts. It would have looked better him if he'd stopped here, an' made the best of Ms work; stopped here wheer he'd a bin company rm• our Ella. But he'd sooner go gallivantin' away, .eeminly. I don't see much love of a lass in that sort o' work." , "But he could no' mak' much at his work here, could he? He wur only a big piecer, I reckon? " "Aye, that were just it. He ought to have bin a minder, havin' piecers workin! for him; but they wouldn't give him a pair of wheels. He reckoned ffe'd not bin fairly dealt with in this matter; but I take no notice o' that. If be bin fit to look after a pair of mules, he'd a bin a full-fledged minder now—that's my opinion. Our Ella herself, while she doesn't think Steve's bin fairly treated, iloesn't blame young Mestur Cartleigh for owt." The interested listener smiled as he heard this 1 statement. It was gratifying to learn that Ella and her mother took such a view of the matter' laid oarticularly so to know that Ella did mot hold</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>An advertisement has been recently appearing in the papers, appealing to the women, wives and mcthers,and housekeepers of this country, to buy only British and Colonial goods. Many will no doubt Sympathise with the object in view, which we are informed, is to avenge the foul lies and insults heaped upon our people and troops in South Africa by foreigners. Above all we are to avoid everything made in France, Germany, and Russia, for, by buying goods from these countries, it is stated we are helping to pay for their huge armies, and navies. _ .</p>
        <p>_ We are urged not to travel on the Contient, nor to drink French or German wines, or indeed to have any- thing to do with foreigners. This may be all very well, but it seems very much like patriotism run mad. Tbe advertiser, whoever he may be, would appear to be very much like tbe person who said there should be only one .anguage in the world, and that English. If we insisted upon every nation speaking English, we should then be saved the troublesome taak of having to learn foreign languages. The Duke of Orleans is said to have expressed his ap- proval of the gross caricatures of the Queen,which have appeared in some of the Paris papers, and, unless the Prince can disprove the assertion, he should be stigma- tised in this country as a fit and proper person to avoid. His father, the late Comte de Paris, was respected as the worthy representative of a family which reigned for centuries over France, but his son has become asso- ciated with the rabid crew of Royalises,lmperialists,and Nationalists, who keep France in a ferment, and who would embroil her in a war with this country, or in any other disaster from which they would look to reap some advantage for themselves out of the chaos. The Duke is not allowed to set foot on the soil of France. Like his father he has long enjeyed the hospi- tality of this country in which he was horn, and his in- sults to the Queen are particularly discreditable to him. Her Majesty has shown every possible kindness to the -Orleans family, but it is to be hoped that the present Pretender will in future seek an asylum in some other country, which he may be lees disposed to revile and in- sult. The fourth and final volume of" C. H Spurgeon's Autobiography" contains many portraits end other illustrations, and is probably the moat interesting of the series. It deals more particularly with Spurgeon's home life, his holidays, ansi experiences, in -which we get a better picture of the man, so distinct from the preacher. Many stories have been told about Spurgeoe, most of them pure inventions, which are often tacked on to some orlcither great man's name, in order- to give them colour. Spurgeon was especially a victim in this res- pect, but there never was any doubt aboct his keen humour, of which the present volume affords ample aad authentic evidence. Among many others, an instance of this was afforded when he fell down a marble staircase. On that occasion Mr. Spurgeon knocked out one or two of his teeth, and, rolling over and over, some money fell from his pocket into the Wellington boots he was wearing. Getting up much shaken and danPged, he at once spoke of the accident as " painless dentistry with money to boot." There are many such instances of Mr. Spurgeon's humorous and witty Eayings,in his interesting biography. The late Mr. Moody appears to have been of the same opinion as Luther who said that "the Devil can stand anything but gocd musie, that makes him roar." Mr Moody. though not so great a man as Mr. Spurgeou,wa. scarcely less remarkable AF a famous evangelist, and ha of course, always made singing a prominent feature ofe his services. Many ministers who often have to lament empty pews, might improve matters by following, as far as possible, his advice. Good singing, ho said ,"helps to build up an audience, even if you do preach a dry Sermon. If you have sineing that reaches the heart, it will fil the church every time." There are still many people who ref ard a musical service as inconsistent in a place of worship, but that was not Mr. Moody'er view, and there is no doubt that it was to this cause that much of his popularity as a preacher was due. Buttons are much en evidence this spring, and many of the latest specituens are fascinating ti a degree. These in onyx are delightful, while the cameo variety are ex- ceedingly beautiful. Artistically arranged in groups, the effect upon a simple gown, is all the heart of woman could desire. OtlYx, it may interest many of my readers to kndw is a variety of amorphous silica, remarkable on account of its being coloured in well-defined t ands of two, or occasionally mere, contrasted tints. The ancients appear to have obtained this stone from Arabia, and possibly also from India, and tt ough rare in Britain, a few specimens have been met with in the Isle of Syke, and Perthshire, and others at the Giant's Causeway, in Ireland. As in the case of agates, it is possible to im- prove inferior specimens artificially. The tcque' has widened consisisrably and now des- cribes a high semi-circle, each side dipping almost to the ears. This shape is infinitely more becoming to the generality of women, than the smaller variety of teque, we bad with us so long. Fruit is appearing on much of the millinery of the moment, hut M 419 arranged by a master hand—is a failure. Ostrich feathers have vanished for the time being, but will rr-appear ere long. The medical journals are never satisfied unless-they are constantly alarming the public over some new danger to lite, or health. Some time ago,it was the typhoid oyster</p>
      </div>
