      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>(copyright.) STEVE, THE OLILANDER.</head>
        <p>(copyright.) STEVE, THE OLILANDER.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR LA.YCOCK,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Young Oaffer," "Seaside Shtches,"</p>
        <p>"Lancashire Sketches," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Steve Hart, a factory operative, in love with Ella Ray- burn, another factory hand, is compelled by the maliciousness of young Cartleigh, the factory manager, to leave his post. He determines to go to South Africa. Cartleigli intends, meanwhile, to win Ella from him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER 111.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 111.</p>
        <p>Ralph Cartleigh was the second son of James Cartleigh, mill-owner and J.P. A wilful, spoilt lad in his younger days, he grew up to manhood's estate • - more headstrong, more • petulant, more masterful. Latterly he had become almost estranged from his family owing to his loose, dis- reputable habits. He was a confirmed drinker, a secret gambler, and hopeless profligate, and, as such, was looked upon with horror by his kindred. Cartleigh, senior, had mapped out a commercial career for both of his sons, Henry and Ralph, but the elder of his offspring, early manifesting a ,desire for the ministry, was allowed to follow his bent after a few years • of mill-life. He was succeeded by his younger brother, who, for a time, assumed sole control over Fernleigh Mill. Then, as the management grew yuc owing to Ralph's neglect and carelessness, 'James Cartleigh once again directed the affairs of the factory, although his son was nominally manager. Amongst the majority of the workpeople Ralph was disliked and feared. He was vacillating and humorsome, particularly °after his drinking bouts, and at such times his presence in the mill was the -cause of considerable concern to overlookers and operatives alike. The girls in the weaving shed, however, did not regard "the young naestur " altogether as a bad sort, although the quieter ones distrusted him and fought shy of his patronage. We have seen that Ella Rayburn was rather favourably impressed with him. To her he was never overbearing or masterful. Nor did he, in any marked degree, treat her as an inferior as he did others. His manner was smooth and courteous, his speech kindly, and at times most flattering. Ella's workmates chaffed her much concerning tte young manager, even hinted that in these studied attentions he sought to win a warm place in her affections. This the weaver lass stoutly and indignantly repudiated. "He is nothing to me," she said. "You are merely jealous because he doesn't pay much atten- tion to you." "He thinks better of you than he does of Steve," said one of the girls. "Perhaps he thinks she thinks more of him than she does of Steve," said another. "What do you mean ? " cried the girl, turning on her taunting neighbours with flashing eyes. "Oh, nothing," the last speaker replied, laughing, and at once became deeply engrossed in her work. This incident awakened new thoughts in Ella Rayburn's mind. She had not previously attached any significance to the fact that Ralph Cartleigh singled her out for special attention. She had never suspected that he sought to occupy the place in her affections that Steve held. "What can it mean ? " she asked herself. "What can they mean by such remarks? If I thought that Mr. Ralph had wronged Steve and had been the cause of him not getting the pair of wheels I would leave this place to-morrow and have nothing more to do with him." But after much deliberation, during which her own vanity and not Steve's well-being was most considered, she disposed of the matter to her com- plete satisfaction. "Let them say what they like, I don't care. Mr. Ralph is nothing to me, and I'll let them see it." Nevertheless, on the afternoon of Wednesday, when the young manager came throrigh the weaving-shed and turned aside to her, she blushed deeply, and, as their eyes met, her confusion was remarked by all in her vicinity. Seeing that they were being curiously observed by the others, Cart- leigh asked Ella to accompany him to his private Office. "Your overlooker tells me that ,you wish to stay away from work for a few days," he said, as she entered the room. "Yes, sir. I—l'm going to—" She hesitated in her confusion. -</p>
        <p>"Not ton wedding, eh?" "Oh, no." She laughed and became more natural in speech and manner. "Oh, no. No such thing. I'm going to see a friend off who is going abroad." "Ah 1 I see," Ralph Cartleigh remarked, as if he had heard the news for the first time. "A friend of yours, eh?"</p>
        <p>"Yes." "Your—er—Your friend Hart, I suppose?" "Yes," she answered briefly, reddening per- ceptibly. "Well, yes, you can go, of course. The best of friends must part, eh ? You'll feel quite lonely when your friend has gone." He gave her a meaning look, and, abashed, she averted her head. "I'm sure you will feel lonely," he said. "I shall have mother." "Ah, to be sure. But a, mother even cannot always take the place of a friend." Ella Rayburn was becoming more disconcerted each moment. She longed to fly back again to her looms. "I must get back now, Mr. Cartleigh. Thank you, very much," and she turned towards the 'loon "One moment," said the yonng man, and he closed the door quietly. "As your friend is leaving you perhaps you won't mind me calling to see you, miss Rayburn ? " ? No. Oh, I don't know," stammered the</p>
        <p>girl. "I can and will be a-very devoted friend, Miss Rayburn," he proceeded, in his smooth, oily fashion. "I don't think it would be right to Steve," said Ella, plucking up courage and striving, though vainly, to feel indignant at this impudent proposal. "I shouldn't like to--" , "Oh, don't mistake my meaning, please," he said, in mock-apologetic tones. "I don't wish to. —er—supersede your friend.' I would call to see you—and your mother, occasionally, you know. I'm sure your, friend' wouldn't mind that." "Ola, no, I don't think so," the girl replied, absenrly, scarce knowing what she was saying. "But I must be going now, Mr. Cartleigh." She hurried back to her looms hot and flurried, and the tongues of her companions vere soon all awag. It was patent to everyone, save the two people most concerned, that Ralph Cartleigh was intriguing to win Ella Rayburn. "Steve ought to know what's going on," said one of the girls. "If he knew 'at Ella was flirting with Mestur Ralph, he'd happen not be in such a hurry to go abroad ; he'd stop at home. He ought to be told, that's what I say." But it was a delicate matter to broach, and there was none willing to act as tale-bearer. "He'll perhaps know all about it when it's too late," they said. So Steve was left in ignorance of what was going forward between his sweetheart, Ella, and the factory-master's son. Early on Thursday morning he bade adieu to his friends and acquaintances, looked for the last time on smoky Dalesworth with its snug rows of cottages and mighty factories with towering chimneys, then, with a heavy heart yet a bright hope, he sallied forth to Manchester. Ella Rayburn accompanied him, and on the plat- form of Victoria Station they had their last chat together. Ella was agitated and tearful, talking and sobbing by tarns. Steve, more 'calm and collected, spoke chePringly of the future, and dwelt on the joy of thelr next reunion, when he would return, if not a multi-millionaire, with sufficient money at least to ensure them possession of most of the comforts and many of the luxuries of life. "The time will soon pass over," he said, "and then—well, ever afterwards we shall be happy together, never more shall we part. We'll soon have you out of the mill, then you shall be as fine a lady na any in the land." His sanguine, hopeful speech failed to rally the girl. She was apparently inconsolable. "There is nothing to fear, my girl, for either of us," he continued. "All will be well with us if we are but true to each other, as we shall be, eh, dear ? " This roused his sobbing companion a little. "Yes, Steve," she faltered, "we will be true; but. oh, I wish now that you were not going away.</p>
      </div>
