      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NO BLOW NO GO.</head>
        <p>NO BLOW NO GO.</p>
        <p>No wind. Morning after morning the two great single-stickers were towed out of harbour in hope ta begin the race for which all the world was waiting. But there was no wind. Old shellbacks looked ever)? way through their hollowed hands, and the weather sharps scoured sea and land for any breeze that might be coaxed to visit Sandy Book. No use. The very air. had gone stale. Misty, muggio motionless—that Was tiae atmosphere—and yachtsmen and spectators prayed or cured each man after tlin kir d. Miles of excursion boats, rolling and btaggering un their loads, formed a lane, in the shore end of which lar the Shamrock and the Columbia—a pair of big eenite swans asleep on the smooth water. The preparations wore perfect. Not a thing had bees forgotten that would help to mike this the sporting Event of centuries, in a rushing, far-and-away, hand-over-65V5 boom-banging, topsail-splitting style. Avast my heartiee ! Yankee ahoy ! Britons strike home ! What's the matter with the galent lot or you? " No wind—no wind—that's all." And ennegh. No b:ory, no go ; that's the lavz for Failing craft. And no strength, no work ; that', the law for you, f°.r me, and the rest of the jackles—afloat or on laee Where shall we pick up power when keels are glued ta the brine or legs and hands refuse to obey orders ? "In the early part of 1893," testifies a witneso, health broke down under me, I was weak and oat sorts. What ailed me I oidn't know. To save ray ljf I couldn't set a name to it. After eating I had so rune' pain I was afraid to put food in my mouth. Yet neeeV sity obliged .; but for bents subsequently my chest sell as though my paur, scanty dinner had actually lodge". half way down. " I lost strength and ambition. I was like the paints ship upon the painted ocfan that the poet tells of. 14° tr?atment helped me until I tried Mother Seigel's Syrol3; After takieg this mediciree a few daes I pulled myself., Ogether, so to speak. All the pain vanished. j CON' eat a jolly good dinner and it agreed with me. " I continued with this splendid medicine and Wilf/ seen as strong and well as ever. You may publish what I say if yeti. like. Everybody should know Mott Seigel's Syrup. It puts lire into the dull and the feeble."‘ Thomas Norman, 186, Jarrom Street, Leicester. June 6, 1899.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>MR. TOM BOOTH ROYD'S AN N UA'</head>
        <p>MR. TOM BOOTH ROYD'S AN N UA'</p>
        <p>CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The Assembly Room at the Conservative Club fr„a well filled on Monday evening or., the occasion at; third annual concert given by Mr. Torn B.g.throyd. programme very appropriately opened with the Pro, tormance of the National Anthem by the banjo. 111&amp;11*d. iine and guitar band of which M. Tom BoothroY.., M. 1.0 M. is the capable conductor. The baud is Ocr posed entirely of pupils of Mr. Boothroyd and iLblet performance on Monday evening was highly credittu • to instrumentalists and conductor alike. One of tlia most enjoyable items was the fantasie on "Home Sllv7, Home" arranged by the conductor, and the clever Pe:e formance of the solo part by Muss Nolland, the leader 1 the mandolines. Miss Nclland was also succissful Ist.efr in the evening in her interpretation of "Contemplazio°%, (Menozzi). Mr. B€othroyd displayed his ability 0..: vis.linist by his fine execution of Leonard's "Souvenir Heyde and Ei ns t's "Elegie." The audience showed.tbelii appreciation in an unmistakable manner. The Voc!'6 part of tbe concert was well sustained by Miss Ltyul Bramble (soprano), Mr. Sam Horton (tenor) and Dar; Libbis N. Burch (baritone). Miss Bramble's two sovg:, were "When the heart is young" and '.For all eternityr, and the scored a distinct success in both. In his isitis3 effort Mr. Burch sang "The Deathless but he was heard to better advantage "Long ago in A'cala." Mr. Sam Horton's pure toot tenor voice was well suited tc his two songs "$1 D.eams " and " &amp;cause of Thee." In response to the continued applause which followed this latter song Die, complied with an encore and further pleased 03: audience by his interpretation of "Come into the Maud." The duties of accompanist wore tasteft3,446 discharged by Mrs Briers. Mr Mattis Chesbil. supplied the humour of the evening and evoked Nat% of laughter by his inimitable musicial sketches. members of Mr. Tom Boothroyd's Banjo, Mandoline alio Guitar Band are as follows :—First Mandolines; Nolland, A Smith, F Jordan (Earlestown),l Broombea_;: Second Mandolines: Miss M Henderson and Miss rel:• berton ; Violins: H Butcher, Matter J H Mandola : Miss P Cheswortb ; Guitar: Miss M worth; Bass Guitar : Mr. J Henderson ; Banjos E Grimsbaw, Mr. E Davies, and Mr. H Sinclair. doctor, Mr. Tom Boothroyd, M.I.U.M.</p>
