        <p>DEPARTURE OF LOOAL VOLUNTEERS. THURSDAY'S "SEND OFF." Unprecedented Enthusiasm. Nothing could live exceeded the heertiness and the enenelasm wih wnich Werricgtoa parted with Ler " ciezeia seldiers " who Cu Friday emberktel on the Av ondale Castle for the Cape. There was some misunderstanding as to the time of th-ir departure and consequently largs nembeis b •gan to asseinlYe in toe streets through wnicn tee toe " ordered South" were to pass, at an unnecessarily early hour. As a m atter of fact the in n die not leave ()reed Berrecka ouol alt me:night, but even at ten o'clock people Legaa to congregate on the route of the procession. Ben the volunteer contingents who had been quartered at o:ferd Barracks made their departure together,oue compaoycon- sisen4 of the Liverpool spectal service company (wheel includes volunteers from Southport), and tile oeh,r • tee South L oneashiies drawn from Warrington, St. flee ns, %View s and Neaten. The sleuth Lan2aseires nutnesred 116 and the Liverpeols 119. It was in the former f course that most of the int, rSt centre J, as the m nity ,of the men forming ths C mop any were. W ere ngt toreeSielse. Diaing the di y ..iiair634 of people horn 11. step 01, S. Liefeus, Widues,Newrose and even Souteport flecked into the town. espesially large numbers coming fr in Li yeepool. The striagency of the rules respectine the entry of civilians into the Barracks was Senlle'ethat relaxed, and relatives and friends of the volunteers were permitted to enter the barracks and converse with these whom they wished in the Volunteers' quarters. '[hi s privilege, needless to say, was largely Wren advan- tage of. At his o'clock all the visitors had to leave the , Barracks, and for the greeter portion of th: time from then until midnight, the msn were b :sily employed in attencling to their kits. At 10 o'clock supper was served. During ths afterneon MN. Ridew ie. wife of tee Colenel of the Ist Vei. Soeth Lancaster? Regieneet (P W.V.) visited the Barracks, and on behalf of the ladies of Warrington presented each man with a Balaclava helmet, choler,* belt, despatch book, handker- deers and other useful articles• Adjutant Ronne also forwarded each of the Warrington Volue- teers a small present accompanying which was an illuminated card baring the portraits of ',ere. Roberts and Kitchener, with the following words :—"From y our adjutant as a little token of eis regard for you wishing you the best of luck and a happy titre in South Aff.ca, where he is sure you will assist to add to the goe.i name of your regiment and battalion." The men on toe other hand remembered thope,who during their stay at the Barracks,had trained them. They were the first to acknowledge toe excellence of the training and the benefit they derived from it. A _ew days ago they pre- seated to Serga-Major Deli-nage a timepiece, which bore a silver plate with the inscription :—" Pres -need to Serge- Major W. D. Image by the members:lA the Active Service Company of the Ist and 2a-1 Veluntesr Battalions S ,ute , Lane osiers Regiment on their depertute for South Africa on February 22ad. 1900." A marble clock was pre- sented to Sergeant Sweeney, the Gymnasium Instructor; and Colour-Sergeant Nicoolas and C 'our-Sereea--t Nice:Trail each received a handsome silver-mounted pipe land half a pound of tobacco. The men fell in on th equate at the B .rracks a' 12 o'clock and they undoubtedty looked as fins a lot of men as any captain cou'd wish to lead. Their stay in the barracks has effected a marked improvement in their physique and the men looked as fit as the proverbial fiddle tor the work which lies before them. Tee pro- spect of leaving tee Bireacks came as a relief to the men eho were getting somewhat tired of a monoton tus Birrack life. We might here state that the Ist South Lancashire Company is under the command et Captain F. M. Appleton the other officers being Lieutenants Lswis and R .bsori and the Liverpool Company is in cnarge of Major J, J. Thomas, the other officers being Lientenents Kyrke-Smith and Kenyon. In the meantime the people in the streets had been wiling away the time by singing patriotic songs, and here and there some rough horse play was indulged in. Considerable amusement was caused by an effigy of Kruger which was suspended by the neck from a ripe across Orford-lane. Some of the crowd commenced to throw stones and other missiles at it and it was though advisable to remove it in order to prevent any destroc tion of windows in the vicmity. I aeother strest an effigy of Kruger was cattle" about,which hl suspended from the neck a card bearing the words " Pity me for I am blind." Youngsters bearing torches paraded the streets in martial order and effected to play the role of the volunteers whom the spectators were anxiously lawaiting. By the time the men left the Barracks the ' streets presented a lively appearance and one could scarcely believe that Friday morning had dawned. Every point along the route where a good view was obtainable was occupied, some of the most eager of the crowd haying got on the roofs of houses and on the tops of hoardings. The proceseion was headed by a large body of men holding aloft flaming torches and Union Jacks and singing patriotic songs. Two bands, the Volunteer and the Borough, played military airs, ants the scene was not one that will readily be forgotten. When the "gentlemen in kharki " came into view, the music was almost drowned in cheers, and the dense throne made the progress of the men most difficult, some of them were seized and borne triumphantly on ths shoulders of the friends whom they were leaving behind, Captain Appleton being one of those who came in for this distinction. At the entrance to the station an exceedingly large crowd had gathered,and the men with difficulty made their way to the platform. Here thee was still greater trouble before they could reach the railway carriages, the platform being filled from end to end. The Liverpool men stepped on to the platform first, and were greeted with vociferous cheers, but when the South Lancashires appeared tea cheering became louder and louder. Many of the men were carried shoulder high along the platform. The Mayer, the Town Clerk, members of the Corporation, and other prominent townsmen witnessed the proceed- ings. The Mayor mounted a waggon on the platform with the intention of addressing the men but was unable to do so owing to the bustle and confusion which prevailed. Is the men were entraining the Volunteer Band struck up several popular tunes 'Deluding the "The Absent- minded beggar" aad the men were assiste in the train by officers of the corps. Amongst the officers preseat were Co'oeel Ridgway, Vajor Roome, Lieutenent G Crosfie'd, Quartermaster Potter, Sergeant-Major Rawlin- Ben, and others. As the train steamed slowly out of the station cheers were raised, hats were waved in the air, and the baud played "Auld Lang Sync," the enthusiasm continuing until the train was lost to view. The crowd then dispersed at about 1 30. The behaviour of the spectators though boisterous slid not lead to any serious disturb ince or accidents. A Con- tingent of police under the directions of Superintendent Monaghan and Chief Inspector Phillips deserve credit for the services they rendered in helping to maintain order. ,A word c.f pr lige is also due to Mr. Cooke, the staionmaster, and his asistantE. On Board the Avondale. The Mayor of Warrington received Me under men- tieued telegram at 2.40 p.m. on Friday. It was handed in at Southampton at 1 50. It reads as follows :—" The Mayor of Warrington.—Ml safe on board ; good bye to you and all in Warrington. Captain Appleton." Tobacco for the Volunteers. The Transvaal Relief Committee purchased for the Warrington Volunteers £2O worth of tobacco and cigars which were put on beard the Avondale Castle duty free. One portion of it has been placed at the disposal of the men during the voyage. One parcel contained 70.ba of " Prairie flower " mixture, half a gross of piper, for the men, and for the officers three boxes of cigars, contain- ing 50 each and 1,000 " Navy cut" cigarettes. The second parcel for use after arrival at the Cape contains 701bs of" Black Cavendish." and 351bs of "Bright Cavendish "for the men, and three boxes cif cigars for the officers.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Intelligence. In the Battalion Orders of the First Volunteer Battalion, Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) there are the following noti- fications :—ln order to encourage and assist those members of the Battalion who are desirous of attain- ing a higher efficiency in the duties of a soldier than can be afforded by the ordinary evening drills before camp, the Commanding Officer has decided to form a class of trained volunteers, commencing on the sth March, to enable them to do so. In addi- tion to the ordinary drill this class will be instruc- ted in physical drill with arms, bayonet exercise, duties of markers and guides, and finally field training, 68 far as can be arranged locally. A special certificate will be issued to each member of the class at the end of the course and he will be termed a first class volunteer, and priority of selec- tion for promotion will be accorded by the Com- manding Officer to those privates holding the cer- tificate. It is hoped that a large number of trained volunteers will give their names to the Sergeant Major, on or before the Ist March, for this class,the only conditions being that they must engage to attend drills regularly on Mondays. Wednesdays and Fridays.—The Commanding Officer having approved of the following promotions they will take efftct from this date :—Colour-Sergeant J Wilcocks to be Quarter Master Sergeant, vice Quarter Master Sergeant Potter promoted; Private G. Cade, and Private T. W. Payton, to be Lance- Corporals.</p>
