        <p>TIT EXA MCC FRI DAT, MARCH 2 1900. CLERGY DAUGHTER'S SCHOOL. -TEA MEETINGS AT WARRINGTON.'</p>
        <p>St Barnabas' Church. evrn eeg annual tea mectin; eon. oil, 'emcee • t h b•ve erne cls w held re th- scheol nec-ira, Ir- e.:eistr,re. At s o'clockc about 250 sat down room, Lan the tee es oeine pras•ae ,erer toy la ly to a e,piLal `-,fl cener ,e•eri el. Atter tea the room vc-ta members of e , ecing, interspersed with songs, Sea, was c e tree and d, .niduighr. indulged in till Presbyterian Church. St. John', ,ting in namection with St. John's Toe aneual tea me. Winwiek-stre -t, was held in the Presbyterian Churca, :jv,11,:t(t)11. , r tree t, n t, )ni t,tr'nTs,::' The tn AwtearerEll:4:Ce reel- which c . which was net so large as i. 30, the fonewine laoies lentas,i s ttienagwaate pt.ialret ktaebni eosf , a..,,ntlioitronßtoirslee w;ci nte, l ui resr3ullhe.'lil orew, whiler . McLarty, Barlow, Walker, 131 Aftrr tea an adjournment was ma. ntertainment. Mr. the room was being cleared for the nchester, preaded James Rus;ell, of D dsbury, near Ma. an appropriate over the entertainment and d diverea swech. • The Rev. J. Warnock, made a statemene to the po. Rion of the ceurtan He sale work as a congregation dicing the year neo,t respects bean a repetition of what had bet. in previous years. There had be 'n a fair aver. additions to the rnembersleip, which hewever, did more thin make up for .he rernora!a by death and ate csael. The number of deaths hal been greater theJ had been for some time. Soon after their last auntie) meetirg they had to mourn the death ot the late Mr. Thomts Dinwiddie, who had le-en a tnanaeer for many years. and at the Isegineine of the present month they lost. their !enior elder, Mr. John M'Do well. As to fie- anees they commenced the year with ae adverte balance of less than £2 and after paying all expenses they be- gan the present year with a balance of practically the came amount. The most •noteworthy event of the year had ben the effort to raise funds for the cleaning of ttid church, and that effort hid In:a with much encourage merit. Up to the present time they had rais^d the sum of LH!, and he need not say that their thanks were doe to many friends both in the congregation and outside, for ther generous help. Of tile total amount raised, up- wards oi £l6 came from the proceeds of the lecture given lest month in the Parr Hall. If they ceull see their way to rai,e another £lOO the work of restoring the church would be proce•ded with at onca He did not say that that amount would cover everything but the deficit we old not be difficult to deal with. Before sitting down he would like to thank ell present for their kind- ness in coming that evening. They all knew that it was , not a very propitious evening and that made them all ' the mere pleased to see their friend Mr. Russell who had come from Manchester to ssat them (App'ause ) An interesting pro:frame t was submitted, opening with a pianoforte eel by Mr. Jories who al,e) acted as accompanist. Mrs Wii;iams sang " When the children are asleep wita SUCOMs and later in the evening gave " When th heart is young" for which she was vieorousle recalled. Mr. Ire- hod was heard to advantage in " Mary " and had the honour of an encore. A similar eomnlintent was paid to him fer his rendition of " The fight of Ages, " but he was unable to respond, having to catch a train. Miss Lonedin cleverly .•,cut.4l 4, couple of pianoforte solos and Mr. Russell sang " Queen ef the Earth." Sergesnt Seepherri and Mr. Hunter coneributed Scotch &amp;meg. The humorous items of the eveuing were provided by Mr. E. A. Sanders, who created roars of laughter with his recitations. Mr. Hawthorne recited " The translation of Elijah " with effect. At the close, en the propedtiort of Mr HAWTHORNE, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chaiemareand all who had centrinuted to the success of the gathering. The singing of the Doxology terminated tne proceedings. The children's tea and prize distribution took pace on Weeluesday evening.</p>
        <p>with iegard th t their had in done x2e ot ,er</p>
        <p>Cairo Street Chapel.</p>
        <p>Despite the upfavourable weather on Monday evening the attendance at the annual conereg4tional ga^hering in connection with Cairo-street Chapel' was very satis- factory. Tea was served to ale or, 100 persons and at the conclusion an eetertairenent was provided in the schoolroom. The proceedings were presided over by Mr J H Knowles. He was accompanied on the platform by the Rev W H Drummond, Rev J M Lloyd Thomas, (Linear]) Mr Thomas Birched', J.P., and Mr H Knowles. Toe CHAIRMAN expressed nis pleasure at meting 411(;i1 a large company considering the state of the weather, and said it was pleasant to see the young people assembled on such an occasion. The only pity V7.-Is that thnir faces were not oftener seen in chapel. To him it had always been an exceeding delight to attend the services, and he wanderei that their young people had not tn-lre pleasure in attending. When he looked back upon his iifo there were a few things that pleased him, and the firet -.vas that he had always had the habit of attending church. He believed that the interest he had taken in religious mattare had been one of the greatest tlessiegs of his life. Be also looke i back with pleasure upon the happy time he had spent in that school, first as a scholar and then as a teacner. There was nothing gave him greater pleaeure than to see young people taking an intereet in tee school and chapel. He felt then that the labours of those who had gone before had not been in vain. They had a glorious religion, and they must let it infleence their own persenal life. They must let God control their hearts and lives. Wherever they saw a gogd man and good woman thre they saw God manifestisg himself. (Applause). The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said it was only natural that they should have a goee grumble at the weather, but he thoueht there were reasons why they should take a hopeful view of the situation. (Hear, hear.) He was sorry that certain members of their fellowship ceuld not be present. Mr.and Mrs. Long were away,and a similar reason was responsible for the absence of their good hind from Walt ne Hall, Mr and Mrs Monks. They titled that night at the close of another year of their work as a church. As they looked back upon the twelve months he thought they might say that there was no- thing that had been very conspicuous, nothing which stood out as very remarakaele in the incidents of the year, except perhaps that they might remember with special gratitule and pleasure that Sunday morning when they listened to Mr Stopford Brooke in the old chapel. It had been a year of steady and quiet work, and he though': that these uneventful years of history of which was reile.rd to tell %ere perhaps the best and mod fruitful years in the life of a church. He sometimes wondered whether everyone connected with them quite realised the amount of good solid Christian work chat was done in that place week by week. They had their services morning and evening ; they hai their monthly children's service in the chapel and several societies which displayed the greatest activity. One or two stood out very prominently. The Childr, n's Society had been ceoducted with great success and industry by a number of young people connected with tee scnools. Another society which did a great amount of good was the Mother's Meeting. It was a thoroughly good bit of Christian work, largely because it was in the direction of cheerfulness. They were indebted very much to Miss Houghton, for her really splendid faithfulness in that and in all good works. (Applause.) There was also the Lads' Club, which was very successfully worked and was a thorough good pieee of work. Then there was the Religious Fellowship Society whose meetings had been most helpful to those who attended. At the beginning of the year Mr. Hill and Mr. Pemberton retired from their position as superinten- tendentsof the Sunday school, and Mr. H. Knowles was appointed the new superintendent. They heartily thanked Mr Hill and Mr Pemberton for the good work they had done in the school, and cordially welcomed the new Superintendent. (Applause ) Interesting as was all that activity, to the mind of tie Chris- tion mieieter the central and absorbing feature of his life was the preaching of-thee gospel. It was what a Christian minister existed for. The longer he was engaged es a minister, the more convinced he became that a church could not be successful unless the heart and supreme interest of the people was in its wership. Admitting that he bad opened him- self to the criticism of his people, he said his aim bad been to lay stress upon the great living essential things of Christianity and to trouble himself very little with 'mocking other people's houses down. Fie heard of a gentleman saying the other day that he (Mr Drummond) had not preached about the war. Be had done that on more than one occasion,and he thought he had never had the slightest desire to conceal his own position on the unpopular side of the coatroversy. The negotiations which led to it and the war itself had been to him almost a constant nightmare, but he felt that he could nut help them most by strong partisan statements in tho pulpit. He had. been most anxious to keep a Christian keeling living and quick among them, and he hoped he had been able to keep befo.e their eyes some of the great principles of national righteousness,allowing them then as re,ponsible men and women to apply those principles for themselves and draw their own conclusions always keeping them in the closest harmony with Chris- tian principles, (Applause.) He had been with them seven years, and he hoped they would tolerate him a little longer, judging him charitably as they had done in the past. (Applause) During the evening, the Rev. J. M. LLOYD Thomes also addressed the meeting. Sones were contributed by Mrs. Muir and Miss Allen in a praiseworthy manner. Mr. liebbiugton delighted the audience with his brilliant execution on the violin and was deservedly encored. Mr. Harrison was else very euccresful in his pianoforte solos. A pleasant gathering concluded with the usual vote , of:thanks. h</p>
        <p>Failure of a Warrington Builder.</p>
        <p>A receiving order has besu ;nad a in the Warring- ton County Court, on the debtor's own petition, against Thomas Worslay, who carries :on business as a builder at 125, Foster-street, Warrington. He has bteu adjudicated bankrupt.</p>
