      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Entertainment and Prize Distribu-</head>
        <p>Entertainment and Prize Distribu-</p>
        <p>tion</p>
        <p>The a inual priz distributioe aid ente-tainment (post- ponel trom Jhristmas) ia ceneestiee with the a bove school toek place oe Monday afternoon in the preeeees of a large number of friends of the pupils and m :ny local ladii,s and gentlemen. The fits", p trt of the programme coesieted of a series of tab eaux, nemely (1) " Midsummer Night's Dearn :" Titania, M Fietcher; Oneron, K Kennedy ; Bottom, B Lund ; fairies,E Harry, R Nicholas, D Barris, G Crane, B Reed, D Bich Jones ; elves, I Garnrtt, E Farmer, G Peters, G Gess,R Me ceell, E O'Brien. (2) " Alice in Woaderland ": Alice, I WU- cockson; Kieg of Hearts, C 0 Garnett ; Queen ot Hearts, A Coulton ; Knave of Heerts, I Schofield ; knee hatteeL Farmer ; white rabbit, D Norris ; dormoue,, G Wilson; Duchess's cook, M Wright ; jurors, G Bradford, D Rea- mers, C Wright. D Trott, S Wagentreiber, M Salt. (3) " Snow White ": Snow White, R Garnet ; prince, M Parcival ; dwarfs, N Thomas, G Harris, M Hick- Hog, M Clayton, N Price, C Hat ficht, P Ttmperley. (4) "Dream of Fair Women ": lielen of Troy, B Healey ; I ehigenia, T Willing:on; Cleopatra, el Fletcher ; Fair Rosamond, E Vaughan ; Jepintheles dangliter, H Ash; Margaret Roper, E Fish; Joan of Arc, X TnatolienQueen Eleanor,M H Mitch ; Poet, M Cotton. (5) "Britannia," Britannia, C Colsto . ; Canada, M Cotton ; Africa, I SehOfield; India, Ceulton ; Auetralie L Farmer. The most effective tableaux were "Strose White" and "The Dream of Fair Women." The Reseroie (the Rev. Can-on Willis), at the conclusicee of the musical part of the programme' said he had much plessnre it introducing an old friered, namely the ex- president, of the school, Mr. Joynson, who had very kindly hotieented to distribute the prizes.. The President for the yet? was Sir l'ancis Powell, who was unable to be present, but Mr. Joynson was a great friend and benefactor of the school and had kindle come to their assistance. (Applause.) Mr. JOYNSON then distsibuted the certificates, an ex- planatory statement being made by Miss Kennedv ,as to the prizes. Subsequently the books were given away. Tne following is the prize list : CAMBRIDGE HIGHER LOCAL CERTIFICATE M L Flood, Group C (mathematics), class 111. ; Group A (English literature), Class I. (distineeished in English language and literature and in early Elirtise) ; (+cello H (history), Class II; Group B. (Latin, Greek and Freneh), Class I (distinguished in Latin), M E Young, Group A (English literature), Cass I : Group C (mathematics), Class 111 ; Group B (French and German), Class I. (distinguishecl in German). B J S Cetton, Group H (history), Class I. (distin- guished in Enelish history); Group A (English literature) Class I ; Group B (French and Germau), Class 11. G 'I aylor, Group A (English literature), C!ael 11. distinguished in early English); Group C (mathematics), Class 111 ; Group B (French), Class 111. P.C. Kennedy, Group A (English literature), Class III; Group R (relieieus knowledge), Class 111 ; Group 13 (Latin), Class OXFORD LOCAL CERFIFICATES 1899. Snmento—Class 111., passed, M Lamb, C F Atiamson, R Scott, E F Taylor. JUNIOR.—Pas,ed, A M Coulton, D E Norris, M L Phillips, G F V Temoeriey, D V Ternoerlee, H tat Wright. C C Wright ; passed over age, C M S Cotton, E S Gib- bon. PRELIMINARY —Passed, T Wilcockson ; passed over age, C M Collinson, N M H Fletcher, E H J Gardiner, M asl Percival. LIST OF PRIZES AWARDED ON THE RESULTS OF THE MIDSUMMER EXAMINATION, 1899. Scripture (first and second Scripture prizes given by Rev Canon and Mrs Willis; third and fourth prizes eiven by Mrs Beamont).—C M Holditeh, G A Vaughan, B S J Cotton, H fat Thatcher, E E Vaughan. Old Testament : E H J Gardiner. Literature (all given hy Mrs Sharp).—M Lamb, G A Vaughan, H Ash, B M W Ashworth, BJ S Cotton, R Wright, I M Schofield, M G Phillips. English Essay.—C M Holditch. English Composition.—C M Collinsen. English History (first prize given by Mrs Jones).--C M EP:Witch, L W C Willington, E F Taylor. Geography (all given by Mrs. Stanton).—E S Gibbon,. C H Collinson, N M H Fletcher. French (first prize given by Mrs Jones) —B J S Cot- ton, B Becbey, H G Tayler, M Lamb (translation), C M S Cotton, M G Phillips, E E Vaughan, E H J Gardiner, H G Harris. German (Hamilton Memorial prizes).—B J S Cotton, L W 0 Willineton, B Hedley (translation). Latin.—B Hedley, M Lamb, LW C Willingten, Ii M Weight, C C Wright, M G Phillips (grammar), Neste Thomas, L B Lund. Classics.—F C Kennedy. Arithmetic (Ist, 2nd, and 3rd prizes given be Mrs. Henry Gre,nall).—B Hadley, BJ S Cotton, M Lamb, N M H Fletcher, tat M Percival, L B Lund, G Birch Jones, G A Peters, G Wilson. Euclid (given by Mrs. Penrhyn).—C M llolditch, A M Coulton. Having distributed the prizes Mr. JOYNSON, after re- ferring in terms of praise to toe excellent musical enter- tainment which had been given, and with regret to the absence of Sir Francis Powell, whom he described as "an educational expert," said the Ectiool was in a high state of efficiency; indeed, it was second to none. He appealed to Miss K-nnedy to say whether that was no or cot. (Laughter a Well, at any rate, she could not contradict eim.The question which arose was' Seould the education be continued in that building ?" It was a most difficult one to solve, and he was in favour, if is could be man- aged, of getting into more pleasant surroundings, although he must say that the question was surrounded with difficulties, as Canon Willis and himself knew very well. Several meeting had been held to consider the subject, and though it would be very painful to Canon Wilts, as Chaplain, to hive to say " drood-bye " to that building he was sure be would willingly sacrifice his own feelings if he could assist the institution by a removal, as he had said, into more pleasant surroundings—where there would he prettier walks and more fields and trees to see. The speaker concluded by paying a handsome compliment to Miss Kennedy. The Rector having proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Joynson for their attendance and services, an adjournment-was made to the Library for tea, and this brought the proceedings of the day to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WEAVER'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>A WEAVER'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Chronic Kidney Disease Cured After Years o•</p>
        <p>Agony.</p>
        <p>Till He Used Dodd; Kidney Pills Nothing</p>
        <p>Cured Him.</p>
        <p>Then His Cure Was Rapid And Thorough TIIH very nature of the factory operatives' employ- ment is a great strain on the system, as owing .o the long hours in a heated atmosphere the workers are rendered very susceptible at this season to tha much colder tem- perature on leaving work. Pence many of the mill hands are victims to chronic ailments. A typicil iilus- tration of this has recently come to light by the remark- able cure of the serious case which is reported by a Blackburn weaver, who for many years has suffered great agony from kidney disease. The weaver in question is Mr. T. Lawson, who resides at 30, Daisy-street, Daisyfield, Blackburn. On being visited by a representative of the "Black- burn Standard," Mr. Lawson readily gave particulars as to his illness and cure. "For nearly 20 years I have been a sufferer from kidney disease, which produced tnrible pains in the back, which from time to time used to double me up. I saw several doctors, but though I got temporary relief the attack returned periodically. I lost a deal of work through my illness. besides suffering fearful agony." " I started being as bad as ever again a few rnontl4B ago," interrupted Mr. Lawson," and I had to stop off work a week last Saturday. I thought I was in for an- other long illness, but fortunately I took some D Ad's Kidney ?ills, and was delighted to find the pains leaving sae. I now feel all right ; they have ma&amp; a new man of me. I felt that I wanted to die wheu the pains were on, but now I am quite willing to live a tit Luger." Both Mr. Lawson and his wife are certain that Dodd's Kidney Pills - -</p>
        <p>alone effected the cure, because no other medicine had been used. Mr. Lawson is 54 years of age,and has been e staunch teetotaller for several years. He is ell-known in the town, and he is on whose word may be relied upon. He is convinced that Dodd's Kidney Pills have effected a val cure of a deep-seatsd dissase in himself, and he is anxious that others should hmsr of the benefit he has re- ceivod from Dodd's Kidney Pills. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 2s. 91. per box or six boxes for 13s. 9d., of all Chemists ; or sent post free on receipt of price by the DODDS MEDICINE Co., 23, F..irringdon Avenue, London, B.C. See that you get D-o-d-d-'s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Death of the Rev. B. B. Prescott.</head>
        <p>Death of the Rev. B. B. Prescott.</p>
        <p>We regret to chronicle the death of the Rev. B. B. Prescott who was for many years vicar of Christ Church, Latchford, which took place as the result of an operation performed at Carlisle. His deatl• was announced to the congregation at Christ Church on Sunday evening by the Rev. F. A. Screeton, (vicar). The deceased gentleman had long been in a deli3ate state of health, and, although be did not resign the living until nearly twelve months ago, being succeeded by Mr. Screeton, many years have elapsed sines be took any active part in parochial matters. It may be remembered that his brother, the Rev. J. M. Prescott, who at one time officiated as curate at Christ Church, died from the effects of a bicycle accident.</p>
      </div>
