        <p>- - Toe monthly rneetinl! of ,he WarrLigton Rural Di trict Gmacil was held at 13-wsey CoanV)ers on Wednesd iy afternoon, Mr. Jishua Bad presiding. There were also present, Messrs J H mgllt3n, J Dignum, IV C Brook s, J Johnson, T Halton, R Forsha w and H Thornton, with Dr. S,phtin (medical offi:er),Mesirs F rfoot,(lnspec- bor), B S Newc ombe, W Wright (assistant surveyors) J C Sutton (..V.rk) and S Sutton (as.atant clerk). THE LATE MR. J THOMISOIi. The following annou3uenc Od the di-ath of Mr. John Thompson, the Council's Surveyor, app arei on the a;y,enua paper :—" It is with profound regret that we, Surveyor's Assists,nts,have to report the painfully sudden death of Mr. John Thompson, the Council's Surveyor, which occurred arty on Friday morning the 16 h inst. One of us had ben staying with him at his house for some Mt° time ard was with him when he was seized with the illness from wh'cti he died. Pendinz instruc- tions from the Council, we are,under the direction of the Clerk, carrying on the ordinary routine work of the office. Anything extraordinary we are dolerring.—B. S. NEWCOmBE, WILLILIA WRIGHT." The CHAIRMAN Said that betore proceeding with the business he could not help but refer to the loss that uouncil, and the districts that it represented had sus- tained by the very sad and sudden death of Mr Thomp- son. It would be idle for him to try and speak of his qualities to them, they knew them so well, out for him- self he could say that head watched them for the last 16 years, and he wa3 sure Mr. Thpmpson's work was a cry lasting testimony to the very great good he had e. In conclusion he m9ved that a int of condolesc: 'warded to bis relatives. louuscroN seconded. Ha thought that the ino- onditi na of toe roads at the present time was 'a due to Mr. Thonapson's extraordin try</p>
