        <p>the prebible This was agreed tee ,4 FOR T„ UAL? TEAR. The CeTE'TREK E6STIMhaliMe. I,rainagesttbune of year ending at geeeral expenditure dit. ry to preparation of precepts Michaelmas next, preemite ,I 1 be based upon rateable upon cverseers which wroa .09F1 of the current half year. values as they stood at the le Council for approval and and would he laid before Vele, 'larch 28 h. At th 3 close sienaturo at their meeting cum . March 1899, after of the half year ending e treasurer held a payment or all liabilities, Va led of £BOO. This balance in favour of the Coese f one penny in the surplus had accumulated from mike ,ea the rule of the pound made in recent years. It had ba. meet abnormal Council to reserve a sufficient fund to . expense they outlay and particolarly to meet the heave ble treatmeot were liable to incur for provision of hespata a within the in the event of outbreak of infectious diseatea. would at district. In the ordiaary course, the Commit 'l9, have Lady Day 1899 and again at Michaelmas', I&amp; of one I called for parish contributions at the usual Far.* urer's penny in the pound and the balance in the trees dto hands would at Lady pay, 1900, have inciter! he probably £l,OOO. It became, however, the duty Of Council to take over at Lady Day, 1899, the werl. and liaoilitiee of the H hway Brd. Credit was certeinly due to that Board for the exceptionally geo 1 condition of the roads and for the I very satisfactory execution of other works by Mr. Ttoompson, the surveyor, whose reeeat sudden decease was much regretted. In respect, however, of finance, the condition was impecunious, no balance being trans- ferred to the credit of the Council. They became re- sponsible for all outstanding liabilities for the comple- tion of unfinished works in hand, and for the execution of unfinished works decided upon. In respect of plant and unused materials stored within the district the Council equitably compensated the Leigh Rural District Council to the extent of the value of their interest therein. It was, however, necessary in these circum- stances to add to the usual ore penny call, fivepence for highway and road purposes. At Michaelmas the coun- cil reduced the call for the current half year to fonrpence (a penny and threepence) ; the total of calls thus being tenpenc in the pound for the year. The claims upon the Council in respect of the above several road matters had had the effect of exhausting the £BOO surplus. It was estimated that at the close of the present half year a probable balance of £550 would be in the band; of the treasurer. On the other hand the following liabilities were to be met :—Meree wages, £150; team labour, £120; sundry accounts for the quarter, £490 ; making a total of £770. The amount for wages was estimated to the end of April. It would be seen from that statement that the Highway Board, to execute the work they undertook and to recur e a 'reasonable working Wane, required at least one penny in the pound more than they called for. To remedy the financial condition it was desirable that the council limit for a short period any important new work other than maintenance of roads that might be absolutely necessary. It was also &amp;sir- ' able that they carried out their rule to pay off outstand- ing debts at the end of each quarter, and to avoid high- way and road assets being in arrears. A'ter considera- tion of estimated receipts from other sources, he (the Clerk) was hopeful that a call equal with that mede for the corresponding half of last year would meet all libjli- ties within the next half year, and would improve their financial position, and he recommended aceorclinely. The estimates for special expenses were of the ordinary kind, and precepts would be prepared accordingly for submission at their meeting on March2Bth. The estimate was adopted. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SIPHTON reportei that during the months' there had been 18 births and 24 deaths. The death rate was equal to 'A per thousand against 14, the average ef the past three months. and 18, the correaponding naonth of last year. Influenza was the muss of five deaths.</p>
