      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LONGFORD DEPOT.</head>
        <p>LONGFORD DEPOT.</p>
        <p>With regard to tice—alleied---n-uisance at Longford Dept, the Doctor said that as requested by the Council he met Mr. Deas, the previous day and inspected the works. New and elaborate machinery was being put down,and Mr. Deas explained the working of it and bow he thought to abate the nuisance. Be (the doctor) thought it would be wise for the Council to wait and see if the result was satmfaatory. The report was adopted. INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR reported that during the past month nothing:had been done in sanitary matters on account of the severe weather and the ground being covered with snow.—Mrßobert LOIVP informs him that with reference to:the water supply to Dr Murray's house, Penketh, the Corporation water would be put on as soon as the weather was favourable.—The Clerk had handed him a letter received from Mr Owen Llewellyn, H.M. Chief In- spector under the Canal Boats Act asking for further particulars with reference to the prosecution mentioned in the annual return to the Local Government Board and these had been given.—He had to report that Mr Albert Payne had allowed one of his new houses to be occupied , without first obtaining the Council's certificate to do so rendering him liable to a penalty of £lO. He had written to apologise for his act. The report was adopted, the apology of Mr Payne beiDg accepted. .rerki THE MANURE AT PENILETH, The CLERK said that the notices served as to the de- positing of manure at Penketh had been complied with and the manure that was lying there at the time the resolution was paesqd had been removed. Now, hov, - ever, another deposit bad come and the owners asked the Council not to press for the removal of the manure within 14 days, as it was too short a time. Mr. KERPOOT said they wanted the Council to rescind the resolution. It was ultimately decided that the original resolution should stand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BURTGNWOOD WATER SUPPLY</head>
        <p>BURTGNWOOD WATER SUPPLY</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk, who was accompanied by Mr Dees, esplained what had been done with regard to the above question. He said that the Corporation had done all that they could to get the supply. It would net be diffi- cult for them to carry the mains from Winwick, as the distance was too long. They had been trying to get the supply from the St. Helens Corporation, but they would not supply the water at leas than 6,1 per 1,000 gallons. The estimated consumption of water was 1,697.000 eal- lons, which at 63 per 1,000 gallose would cost £42 88 7td. Toe cest of the maim in order to connect the houses with the Collins Green supply would lie £.360. They (the Warrington Corporation) asked the Council to pay 4 per cent. of the cost of laying the mains. The matter had been bronght before the Water Committee of the St. Helens Corporation, hut they felt unable to reduce the price from 6d. per 1,000 gallons. Mr. Forshaw Then we can't get -St. Helena' water under 6d? The Town Clerk: It is impracticable at present. Continuing, the Town Clerk said that if they went to the St. Helens' mains the cost would be £2l 10s.insteed of 214 Bs. Mr Forshaw sail the water from Collins Green entered the mina that went to St. Helens. Out the St. Helens people d,d not use it except in ease of emergency. They</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>INFLUENZA SUBDUED IN 24 HOURS. Death-rate Reduced.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA SUBDUED IN 24 HOURS. Death-rate Reduced.</p>
        <p>During the epidemic of Influe-iza wlitich raged through- out North America last year,VExi;a LiGurxiNG Couon Culls reduced the death-rate to such an extent that physicians never hesitated to prescribe it. Clinical tests prove it to be of unsurpassed excellence for the rapid cure of Influenzi, Bronchitis, Asthma and Coughs. It is a god-send to the afflicted, Ask for VICNO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Price is. ltd. Sold by all Chemists and Co-operative Stores everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>RuNgOßr'\-1 NOTES.</head>
        <p>RuNgOßr'\-1 NOTES.</p>
        <p>CORRESPONDENT.] There have been ties, when the spending of a sum of money, however small, would have been the subject of con,iderahle discussion by the Board of Guardians. At their laat meeting, however, a sum of £ll5, for prpposed laundry improvements at the Workhouse, was recommended to be spent by the Howe Committee, and agreed to without comment. This is either a sign that the Rouse Committee have the tull confidence of the Board, as a com- mittee to be trusted for its thoroughness; or a sign that the BoArd;of Guardians is passing indiffer- ent to the conduct of its finance. This money will of course, have to come out of current rate; NO it is to be hoped the surplus on the estimate will be large enough to include</p>
        <p>The Rural District Council has decided to in- crease the salaries of its surveyors from £l4O to £l7O a year ; and also to purchase a steam-roller, which in all probability will cost from £4OO to £6OO. Considering the work acconiplished by the surveyors in the past, and the thoroughness with which they discliarge their duties at present, the increase adJrded them is well merited (especially so in face of the recent action of the Local Govern- ment Board, which practically prohibits any remuneration for extra services.) With regard to the steam roller, there can be little doubt but that the investment will be an economical one, and one that will repay the Council. Toey must, however, put the roller under the care of a competent roan, and not " spoil the ship for a uenn'orth of tar " as is done in an adjacent district. Goad tools in the hands of good men will do wonders, but the condi- tions brought about by an adverse policy will never be anything but unsatisfactory.</p>
        <p>The question of the Weston water supply, as reported upon by Mr. James Farrington, inspector to the Rural District Council, gives us a clear view of the situation. Mr. Guest and other inhabitants of Weston say it is bad ; and Mr. F. Wiswall (chairman of the Runcorn Urban District Coancil), practically admits it to be so; and owns to the liability of the Runc)rn Urban District Council to remedy it. Now tbese facts are admitted, let us see whether Runcorn will rise to its responsibilities and get 'Utters riet-d.</p>
        <p>I understand that Captain George P Ashton, Lien-</p>
        <p>'enant W G Linaker, and about 86 men of the Run- rn Volunteers, volunteered for Foreign Service, CO- vlonday last. Runcorn can, therefore, be more °B lastly proud of her "eitiz-n soldiers.' for it is than ‘ry town of its siz,that can furnish an almost not eirs. , company—(fully officered)—capable, and complerts., I defend its crmntry. Tie Volunteer motto defitne‘3 should now be rendered 'uthenc.a. nt' defiance ! w.'ll stand to our guns " `4 Defence or</p>
        <p>is the last, verse of a parody com- posed b a The following. in the street," which is becoming 3 " man . Incorn : very popular, in •land.'s muter, o'er all the land and "When you say that Rag sea. to France an 4 Germany. Tis a statement very vexiivg. ing. They've played us lots of The 80-rs have b-3en tail tviist. dirty tricks, whiikers soon, by Runcorn's But there'll be a raid on Kruger's gallant six. he victory will score, They will face the savage Boer, and L. I will leave them a They will teach them a lessors, an.</p>
        <p>-blessing. So that when they come home once more. -tied their tears. The girls the pretty drars. will then have a of the Runcorn And with joy they'll smile, to see the style Volunteers. Chorus</p>
        <p>They'll take their stand., behind the hand With gun in hand, they look so grand. The cheering crowd will roar, as it never did be( orwe• "Hurrah For th,- Volunteer Corps :"</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Runcorn contrary to expectations, got defeated at St. Helens, on Saturday, by a try to a dropped goal.</p>
        <p>How did it come about? Well some say that the principal factor contributing to Runcorn's downfall was the Wednesday match with Salford, and from the way Runcorn played anyone could see they were a long way off form.</p>
        <p>St. Helens played a better game than they did at Runcorn ; but they are not a finished football team, and would not have won on Saturday, but for the one mistake of the veteran Houghton. It was not their good play which brlught about the defeat of the chainpions,but it was the bad play of the latter.</p>
        <p>On the whole, the match was a keen one, and at times very e% citing to watch. Possibly, St Helens never tried harder; but, certainly, not once this season, save at Stockport, have the Linnets presen- ted so poor an attack. Their defence, however was perfect. and I for one will not blame the great "Samuel" for letting that one try past him, for truly he has made very few mistakes, and is still the best fall-back in the Northern Union.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday, we visit our neighbours across the</p>
        <p>"silver -streak," and do not expect an easy victory. We are going to play for all we are worth, how- ever, and will want a lot of beating. In other words we expect to win, hut realise the difficulties of the situation in a fair estimate of our opponents ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>The annual church iermens were preached on Sunday by the Rev. J. M. Lloyd Thomas, of Liscard. In the morning the choir sang the anthem, " I have surely built thee a house" (B :cce), and in the evening " Judge rae. of God" (Mend eissobn). The offertories were for the chapqt funds. Gospel Hall.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night Messrs. Wagstaffe and Bullough of Ashton-in-Makerfirld prea;,•hed here. The Love ot nnd in giving his only begotten son war dealt upotA. Etfct are always produced by a cause. Why God should love a world of sinners and give his son to die for thena is bevond our comprehension. He locked down from Heaven and saw all gore astray, nothing in man to meet with God's approval, Jesus came as God's representative ! He proved that by healing those who were ificurable by man. Then He suffred as the sinners' substitute. He was the One who could, would and did accomplish the work wkereby sinners can be fitted for the presence of God. He now offers eternal lite to those who accept Christ as their Saviour, but to die wi'hlut Christ is to be for ever separated from God.</p>
        <p>Independent Order of Rechabites.—Union Tent.</p>
        <p>The annual social in connection with the above was held in the Mission Hall, Academy-street, on Monday evening, February 26th, at 7.30 p.m., when about seventy members and friends sat down to an excellent repast. After this was over a concert was held, when solos were given by Messrs G Gough and S Hassall ; cornet solos by Nir J 31aaw and reci- tations by Miss B Hands, all of which were ren- dered in excellent st3le. Da ring a break in the proceedings the secretary, Mr S Fernley, gave the annual report, from which it WRS seen that the tent and district are in a growing condition both nu- merically and financially. Y.M.C.A. Debating Society. On Monday night the above held one of its most , successful meetings. Mr S Fay read a very able and interesting paper on " Re-union of Christendom." There was a good attendance of members. After giving a rapid view of the; early church and point- ing where dessension had crept gradually in their midst by superstition and idolatry, culminating in the reformation and the subsequent multiplying of s.scts, Mr Fay, briefly and yet strikingly pointsid to the aims and desires of en ristians for re-union and pleaded eloquently for individual effort in that direction. A brief discussion followed and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr B. Fay, for his able and instructive paper. The paper next Monday also by Mr Fay, is " Divine origin of the Scriptures."</p>
        <p>WHOLESALE AGFNTS.---F. A. JitSKINS. 199 PADOL. LANE, Warrington. J. BUN, 91, BUTTER-MARS STREET, Warrington.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE LINES AMBULANCE COMPETITION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Success of tile Warrington Central</head>
        <p>Success of tile Warrington Central</p>
        <p>Team.</p>
        <p>• The seventt. annual cotnpe Rion took placc o Fr;day at Liverpool Central. Mr. D M ,Idrum, the general manager, presided, and amon:st those present were : Mrs Meldrum, Dr Wright, Captain Waring (Warrington Csrps), Messrs. J Young, A Manners, Li Mason, E 0 Lloyd (hon secretary), Izl. Squads each consisting of five men, were present from Hulkisson, Brunswick, Manchester, Warrington Centro), Glazebrook, Warring- ton Engineers, Southpoit, Warringt,.;n Stores. D. F D B Pim of Barrowford, acted as judge. The result was announced, as follows :—First prize. Warrington Central, 319 marks; speond, G;az‘hrook, 307 marks; third, Warrington Stores, 278. (Maximum marks 4001.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ma!drum presented the priz-s. On the recom- mendation of Dr Pim, the following men were chosen to represent the Cheshire Lines Comaaittee in the competie tion for the Railway Ambulance Challenge Shield, open to all railways in England, and presoweri by the St. IJohn Ambulance Assecktion, namely, A Fryer, H A Roberts, H T Williams, G Greavev, J Fogg, H they having obtained the highest individual marks. The final for ,the shield takes place in London. Votes of thanks to Dr. Pim, Mr. and Mrs. Meldrum and Mr. E. 0. Lloyd terminated the proceedings. This being the third time in succession that the War- rington Central team have won the First Prize Challenge Cup, it DOW betr.mes their property, and it speaks very highly for the efficiency of the various members of the staff who have competed from time to time, two of whom (Ho'loway ar.ol Malkin) are now serving in South Africa. A word of praise is also due to their indefatig- able leader (Mr. II T Williams), who has led tbe various teams at ten competitions, in which they h4ve won five first prized and two thirds. The following is this year's T Williams (leader), J Fogg, A Fryer, H A Roberts, and J Croasdale. It may also be mentioned that this is the second time the Stores team have won the third prize, and have also held the first prize one year (1896). The team is as follows-0 Greaves (leader), Hacket, Welfair, T Buckley, and Crozier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Miss Pollie Houghton (aged 13), of Deresbury, a pupil of Mrs. Hubi Newcombe, Green Bahk,Penketh, was awarded the silver medal and badge in the competition for solo pianoforte playing at the recent Widnes Eisteddfod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Spiritualism.</head>
        <p>Spiritualism.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, Mrs. Porter delivered two addresses —in the afternoon on "Homes in the spirit world," and in the evening on Spiritualism and its critics." At each meeting she gave a few clairvoyant delinea- tions. On Monday evening, after the opening ser- vices, clairvoyance and pyschometry were given. The Theft from a Warrington Draper's. At the Liverpool Intermediate Sessions. on Tues- day, Frank Barnton (22), engineer, pleaded guilty to stealing two woollen shirts, two cycle aweater€, and a silk muffler, on February 9th, the Property of William James Dunn, Warrington. There were several previous convictions against the prisoner or felony,and be was now sentenced to six months' hord labour.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Man Killed at Darwen; At the Blatkburn Infirmary on Monday. an in- quest was held on the body of Edward Boyle (37), foreman concreter, of 60, Garibildi-street. Warring- ton, who died from injuries received at Darwen on. Thursday. The deceased was employed by the Manchester Phenix Fireproof Flooring Company on the new stores Which are being erected on the Green at Darwen for the Provident Co-operative Society, and under his dir.ction a jibhand crane. was being moved. Deceased and two .rnore men were behind the centre part supporting it with their hands, when by some means it overbalanced, and Boyle was knocked to the floor, and fri's head striking a piece of girder, his 'hull wee fractured. 'He was medically atteaded, and afterwards- re- moved to his lodgings, but becoming worse was conveyed to the infirmary,where he died on Friday. —A verdict of" Accidental death was returned.</p>
        <p>Narrow escape of a Cheshire Lines Express. On Monday morning an accident which might have resulted very seriously occurred at the Cheshire LinEB Railway Station, Irlam. The 10.30 a.m. train from Wigan shunted as usual into the siding, and the driver without noticing the signal, which was against him,atternpted to come out. The points being closed prevented him, and the engine ran into the end blocks, which were totally de- molished, and thence on to the tail-end of the plat. form. Immediately an express dashed past, just escaping colliding with the wreckage. Fortunately, no one was seriously hurt, though the driver was cut about the face. A break-down gang was sent down, and the engine and coaches were righted during the day. If the coaches bad been thrown off the metals on to the permanent way there would certainly have been a big smash-up.</p>
        <p>Social Gathering at Wycliffe.</p>
        <p>The teachers and bider scholars of Wycliffe Sun- day School spent a very enjoyable social evening on Tuesday. About one hundred persons responded to the invitation and they were accommodated inn') Upper schoolroom which had been tastefully decor- ated for the oeca.ion. The early part of the pro- ceedings was so arrancmfl rirrAir a tete a tete conversation, and in 1 t—r, • • ;light refresh- rn-nts Were handed r: und. !'u afterwards indulged in, Mr. George Brown sk•fa- torily discharging the duties of master of cere- monies. Several vocal and other items were agreeably , interspersed. Mr H M Ashton and Mr E A Sanders delighted the audience with humorous recitation,' and readings, and on were contributed by Mr W H Wallington and Mr William Brown. A charade taken part in by Mr Ashton, Mr Payton, Miss Knowles, and Mr and Mrs Witts wda also a pleasing diversion. A vote of thanks having been heartily accorded those who had assisted in the arrange- ments, the gathering concluded with the singing of ,Auld Lang Syne."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Independent Order of Good Tern plars.</head>
        <p>Independent Order of Good Tern plars.</p>
        <p>The quarterly session of the TernpPrance Mission Lodge oi the above Order was held in the Temper- ance Mission Room, Academy street on Wednesday. BroJ W Roberts, 1,D., opened the lodge. One can- did,,ate was re-admittevl and four others were in- itiated into the Order. Bro A Francis, C.Se.cretary,</p>
        <p>towas present as a visitor and ok the chair. Tile' election of officers wa,4 then proceeded with and resulted as follows :—C.T , Bro J W Roberts; Sister Nurse; Secretary, Bro C J .Sheath ; F Secre- tary, Bro G Roscoe; chaplain, Sister M Fletcher; marshal', Bro J G Twigden ; deputy marshal', Sister Sheath, jun ; treasur.er, Bro C Nurse ; guard, Bro P 0-Brien ; sentinel. Sic ter Sheath, son; assis- tant secretary, Sister Pldcknert ; P C.T., Bro F Bet- ties. The officers were nktalle -1 by Bro A Francis, 11. D., by Bro Roberts as insrallinA marshal'. Bro Roberts, T then delivered a brief address, C. ~ thanking the members for trvointing him. Bro Francis was thanked for his eff,,rts on behalf of the lodge, on the motion of Bro O'Brien, seconded by Bro. aurse. Bro Francis suitably r.-sponded. The digests of the district lodge (March 3rd) and No. 1 Convention (March 10th) were read and accepted. Bro C J Sheath and Sister Nurss were appointed as D.L. representatives. Bro J W Roberts was re- commended as Ledge Deputy. The lodge was closed by Bro Roberts, C.T.</p>
