        <p>COUNTY OF CHESTER. WILD BIRDS PROTECTION ACTS, 1880, 1894, AND 1396. NOTICE is Hereby Given, that the following is a copy of an ORDER which has been made by the Right Honourable Sir Matthew White Ridley, Baronet, M.P., Secretary of State for the Home Department viz:— WILD BIRDS (ENGLAND). The Wiid Birds Protection (County of Chester) Order, 1898, Dated 15th February, 1898. In pursuance of the power; conferred upon me by the Wild Birds Protection Act, 1880 to 1898, and upon appli- cation by the County Council of Chester, I hereby make the following Order, which shall apply to the whole of the County of Chester : _</p>
        <p>1. Th's Order may be cited as "The Wild Birds Pro- tection (County of Chester) Order, 1898." 2. The time during which the killing and taking of wild birds, or any of them, is prohibited by the Wild Birds Protection Aet, 1880 (43 ano 44 Vic., cap 35), diall be exteaded in the County of Chester so as to be from the Ist day ot March to the 12th day of August in each yeAr•</p>
        <p>-3, The Act of 1880, as extended by Section 2of this Order, shall apply within the whole of the County of Chester to the Kestrel and Heron as if those species were included in the Schedule of that Act. 4, The taking or lelling of the following species of birds, namely :—King Fisher,Great Crested Grebe,Heron, Goldfinch and Owl, shall be prohibited within the whole of the County of Chester during the whole of that period of the year to which the protection of wild birds under the Act of 1880 (as extended by Section 2 of this Order) does not extend.</p>
        <p>5. The taking or destroying of the Ecgs of the Owl, the Bittern, the Kingfisher. the Kestrel, the Heron, the Sheldrake,the Dunlin, the Blackheaded Gull,the Common Tern, and the Oyster Catcher, shall be prohibited in the whole of the County of Chester. And I make the following further Order which shall apply within the portion of the Hundred of Wirral eolnured pink on the maps deposited in the Home Office and in the Office of the County Council, and embracing the low-lying lanes an sandkills situate between the estuaries of the Rivers Dee and Mersey of which the boundaries shall commence to the south at a point of the main highway of the County of Chester, 200 yards to the West of the Dock Cottages near the " Docks " Railway Station of the Wirral Railway, run along the main highway to Bidston Village, and then turn in a northerly direction and run to Bidston Railway Station of the same Rail- way, then turn in a westerly direction and run along the Wirral Railway until it reaches the Meols Station of such railway, then run along the high- way near to the Meals Railway Station in a northerly direction to the Irish Sea, then on the north along the margin of high-water mark of the Irish Sea to a point at New Brighton where the Yellow Noses join the said Irish Sea' and then run in a southerly direction to " Warren Drive " or road, and along "Warren Drive" to the meet northerly house and grounds on the west side of such drive then along the western boundary of the houses and premises rituate along the western side of such drive or road to a point 100 yards or fica from Grove-road, then turning in a westerly direction along the northern boundaries of the houses and premises in Grove-read, Wallasey, until it reaches the Wirral Railway,then turning in a southerly direction along the Wirral Railway until it reaches the Wallasey Marsh, then along the edge of the marsh in a southerly direction until it reaches the Poulton Halfpenny Bridge, then turning in a westerly direction on the edge of the said marsh, until it reaches along the edge of tbe marsh the main highway leading from Poulton to Meole, such parts within the aforeaaid boundaries as are market garden lands and are not coloured in the said maps are excepted from the operation of this Order. 6. The Act of 1880, as extended by Section 2of this Order, shall apply within the above-named area to the following species of birds : Red-backed Shrike. Whinchat, Spotted Fly-catcher, Sedge Warbler, Blackcap Warbler,Grasshopper Warbler, Wood Warbler, Garden Warbler, Mistle Thrush. Common Bunting, Reed Banting,Goldfinch, Wheatesr, Chiffchaff, Golden-crested Wren, Yellow Wagtail, Pied Wagtail, Water Wagtail, Skylark. and Titlark, as if they were in- cluded in the Schedule to the Act of 1880.</p>
        <p>T. The taking or destroying of the Eggs of all Wild Birds shall he prohibited within the above-named area for a period of five years from the date of this Order. 8. The taking or killing of ALL WILD BIRDS shall be prohibited within the above-named area during the whole of that period of the year to whish the protection of wilst birds under the Act of 1880 as extended by Section 2 of this order does not extend.</p>
        <p>9. The taking or killing of the Gull and the Tern- is prohibited within the Administrative County of Ches- ter during the whole of that period of !hie year to which the protection of wild birds under the Wild Birds Pro tection Act. 1830 (as extended by Section 2 of this Order) does not extend.</p>
        <p>10. f hereby exempt from the operation of paragraph number 9 of this Order so m Lich ci the County of Ches- ter nq do's NOT lie within the Parishes of PUDDINGTON WALLASEY BURTON LOWER BEBINGTON NESS BROMBOROUGH NESTON-C 1.7 Ml' A R K - EASTFIAM GATE INCE 800 TON GREAT STANNEY</p>
        <p>NETHERPOOL FRODSRAII LORDSHIP WHITBY WESTON</p>
        <p>S CA.NLOIN GAYTON</p>
        <p>RUNCORN HALTON</p>
        <p>DES WALL-CUM - 0 L Li- NORTON</p>
        <p>FIELD MOORE THURSresTor; ACTON GRANGE CALDY WALTON INFERIOR BOYLAKE-CUM-WEST KIRBY. including therein so much of the cAuaries of the Rivers Dee and Mersey as lies within the Administrative County of Chester.</p>
        <p>11. The Orders of the 10th July, 1895, and the Ist March, 1897, are hereby repealed. Given under my hand at Whitehall, this 15th day of February, 1898.</p>
        <p>M. W. RIDLEY. One of Her Majesty's Principal Secretaries of State.</p>
        <p>PENALTIES. Any perFcn acrins in contravention of the above- mer.tiom-d Order renders himself liable to the Penalties mtutioned in the said Acts. REGINALD POTTS. Clerk of the Cheshire County Council. aaester, 6th February, 1900.</p>
