      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>I_AAA ) "-114. rr 'll\lll</p>
        <p>MARCH 2ND, 1900</p>
        <p>STIRRING events elsewhere "With any Party." should not hide from us the fact that the several Labour parties in this c euntry have come together and decided to form a distinct Party in Parliament, ready to co-operate "with anY Party" promoting legislation favourable to " the direct interests of Labour." We presume that any body of politicians in the House of Com- tunns, desiring to promote special interests, must act in this independent fashion. There can be no ground of complaint on this head. We believe that in nine cases out of ten this co-operation will be secured for the Liberal party, and we shall not complain if, on the tenth case, the opponents of Liberalism are able to obtain the Labour vote, Whenever it may be formed. A Labour party, constituted on the lines now advocated, would not be wholly composed of men of one class, and that Will be a gain. There are many politicians,young and old/who are not working-men in the ordinary sense, but who are still in complete lympathy with the wags-earners, and who can contribute ideas, tactics and Votes to any independent organisation. Thus, out of Parliament and in it, we are agreed that an advance is being made, and that there shall be an end of divisions, jealousies, and recriminations. The interests of the wage-earning classes are pro- fessedly, made the subj act of florid declarations at times of excitement and during a general elec- tion. Vague statements otten suffice to capture a large nunaber of votes, and very little practical Zd.lt is visible when the Parliament comes to an d A little more discrimination is wanted in etereatning what candidates shall receive support, and whether the cause is better served by a special candidate or by throwing an overwhelming influence into the scale in favour of a non-Labour candidate when other parties are evenly baleuced. Eeperienee will have convinced electors in many cases that they reade grave mistakes in 1895, from the effects of Which they have been suflering ever since. Many Of these blunders will be rectified another time, and hence it may be to the interest of some per- -8 Ons to promote working-class divisions. Party Politicians are often compelled to do their best in this direction, but the opportunities favourable to such tactics must now be lessened, and there will, consequently, be more chance for political honesty and straightforwardness. In the present Parlia- ment there are some M.P.'s who have done as much for Labour as woald have been accomplished 13Y purely direct representatives. We should be earrY to see any attack made upon them. On the Other hand,there are M.P.'s who have received a large *limber of working-class votes, who have done nothing whatever to entitle them to a continuance of such favours. They will be missing from the Xt Parliament. A general clarification of ideas taking place. It was the condition-precedent of "aitY, and it has resulted in the triumph of coin- -111)11 sense. Constituencies will be the better for What.il s going on. With the end of the conference there will be a smart survey, and we ellen hear of e Selections of candidates for the next General 'legion. Let full justice be done to existing Lllernbers, who have done well, and there will be a e"tter spirit in being. Cara will be required in not salbarking in too many contests, and a good :legion of candidates must be made. The return 41 a distinct Labour party will be viewed with pYrinPathy and goodwill by all the true friends of i3gess, and now 'that it will also include men of 'tilled and of the Liberal professions,- as well as Workers, it will command the respect of its qualities.</p>
        <p>LORD DURHAM may be Weak Government, A Large Majority, a man of erratic im- pulse, a headstrong Dh°litician, a shifty patriet,and a poor prophet. But hell a man of influence in the North of England, v'etiri a fearless exponent of his own personal con- al ;14e,rts, and he may he saying what other people pr-e `itlinkiner. if the language he employs is LiarlY hvi's own. We remember that his original quarrel With the Government was over the Work- too Act. in his opinion, it went tar) but others haw) had to complain that it h Pretentious, incomplete, ill-constructed, and 's led to more litigation than :my enactment of recent times. Nobody seems to have been very grateful for it, unless it be the lawyers, and it will fOt 'nlY have to be amended but enlarged, before .I'B authors can be held to have redeemed the Drcmlises they made c • to have settled the I:3bleihs they :prevente.l others from handling ith success, or to have rendered smooth and ellitaY the duo administration of the law. Lord (TttllAl+i will probably be more in agreement With the good sense of the country when he deals With the general features of the political situation. The Government has, in Lord Dust- AMs °PiniOn, "neglected its duties, thrown away its Opportunities and ignored the temper of the nation." We can unreservedly subscribe to these animadversions. At the present moment they are tetamed in office by a snbservient and weak</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>majority, which visits with obloquy any strong spirit, and resents criticism, as if it were either heresy or treachery. It is quite true that the Cabinet is weak. Individual 11/4.nisters are weak, the head of the Government is weak, its policy has often been weak to the point of contempt, and it has used the nation's resources to reward and enrich classes for whose support it is indebted to its present position. There can be no question as to the strength of the majority which sustains it. This majority has rendered it careless of outside opinion, and indifferent to the best interests of the country. It has been so sure ef its power in the House of Commons that it has not always paid proper respect to financial and political traditions. It has landed us in a great war for which it was inadequately prepared, and it conducted the negotiations which led up to the war as if it thought war improbable and a policy of bluff the best that a nation like our own ought to pursue. Under any system of shorter Parliaments it would probably have been replaced, but the worst of a strong majority is that it pre- serves a Government in power long after it has ceased to be in touch with the country,and makes its Ministers more disposed to be disrespectful than patriotic, riotous in the exercise of pa wer rather than attentive to the arguments and claims of minorities. It has been a misfortune that the Government has been a Coalition, supported by two parties, because it has had no consistent and steady policy;and it has been weak when the coun- try required it to be strong, as in ecclesiastical affairs, and strong when it ought to have been wary and prudent as in conducting the negotations with the Transvaal. But have Lord DURHAM and his friends any legitimate ground of personal com- plaint? They consented to the Coalition, they abandoned the old Whig principles which belong. to steady Liberalism, and can only produce enerva- tion in Canservatism. They surrendered principle for power, and the power has been displayed in a series of see-saws, in trying to please all men and in failing to retain the confidence of any. It is the fashion to say that party Government is fail- ing. This gibe is untrue. What is failing is the Coalition, and the old wisiorn of the country is expressed in the phrase, "England does not love Coalitions."</p>
        <p>Having accomplished his primary purpose in relieving Kimberley,Lord Roberts, with the genius of a great commander, followed up his advantage in such a fashion as to strike a death-blow at the resistance of the Free State forces. Having once drawn the cordon round General Cronje and his men, the astute British Commander gradually shelled his foe into submission rather than lose many lives by storming the position.Cronje capitu- lated at day-break on Tuesday, and by 10 o'clock the same morning the civilised world was ringing with the news. In this country the jubilation was great, and many were the expressions of joy that the Boer leader so famed for his " slimness " and brilliant fighting qualities was at last a prisoner in our hands. The national elation was enhanced by the re- markable coincidence that the surrender of the enemy to the number of over 4,000, took place on the anniversary of " Majuba," which may thus be considered—by those who harbour vengeance—to have been fully avenged. Lord Roberts himself pointed out this significant fact, and in the light of later particulars, it looks as if he had, with an eye for the dramatic compelled the submission of his foe on so auspicious a day. Crenje's defeat and capture are net things to gloat over however. These are merely incidents of a reassuring nature in a difficult campaign, but a campain that can have only one end—the end one foresees. It is not too late to again express our admiration for the heroic defence made by General Cronje and the dauntless soldiers of the soil under him.</p>
        <p>People had only just ceased to shout "Bravo Bobs l" when the great and glorious news of Lady- smith's relief came in an official despatch. The cry at once changed to "Bravo Buller !" and joyousness unprecedented was everywhere apparent. March Ist will be as memor- able a day as the anniversary of " Majuba," for never, we think, has an event of national importance been so earnestly prayed for and anxiously awaited as the relief of beleagured Ladysmith. Sir George White and his gallant men have made an heroic and success- ful struggle against tremendous odds for over three months, enduring privation, bombardment and fierce attack from a determined foe in a manner which has more than sustained the reputation of Britons for pluck. Many of the Continental news- papers laud Cronje to the skies, attributing to him Spartan courage, but the fulsome praise would be better bestowed on the defenders of Ladysmith. What must have been their feelings during the past few weeks, when effort after effort on the part of Sir Redvers Buller to relieve the town was repulsed by the Boors? How heart-breaking the anxiety with deliverance so near and yet so far? The guns of the relief force were continually heard, nay the men in khaki were actually seen, but the cordon could not be broken. Then, all at once, Sir Redvers emulates the feat of kis chief at Paardeberg and Lord Dun- donald's cavalry division dashes in by a rapid flank movement. The enemy retreat, and the town is relieved. Well may the nation shout, "Bravo Buller," and sober old towns like War- rington become suddenly gay with flags and bunt- ing, and hilarious with au overjoyed community.</p>
        <p>A suggestion, quite Ruskinian ic its character, has been made that a society should be formed to supply freely architects' designs for cottages. The thought is prompted by the fear that villages are becoming ugly. The new Washington Irving writing a sketchbook will, it appears, have a sorry tale for American cousins. No Goldsmith will describe another Sweet Auburn, not because the spirit of poetry will die, hut because the material on which to descant will no longer exist. The cheap and nasty,so long associated with Suburbia, is beingireproduced by the small cottage builder in the hamlets. That is what we are to gather from the proposal fo: a society made by Mr. Marshall in the pages of " Macmillan." The remedy, however, needs to be a far deeper one than can be found by architects. However rude and often incon- venient were the labourers' dwellings in the past, they were erected by squires who had sufficient money to give them substance, and nature did the rest. They were not picturesque a 3 initio, and, be the truth told, they were generally insanitary. To give the cottages of to-day substance means money, and no craftiness of design will obviate the difficulty. The buildings will be mean, becauee the owner is perforce mean. The villages IRck their pieturesqueness because the young with spare energy to cultivate the garden and train the creepers fly to the towns, and only the feeble in body and tke aged are left. What is wanted is to find profitable occupation on the land, to quicken country existence, and to clean from the minds of the people the mirage of town attractions; for, after all, town existence for the poor, if a living is to be got in the country, is generally less healthy and enjoyable.</p>
        <p>Some of the French newspapers have been un- able to resist tho temptation which the approach of the Paris Exhibition causes. It is notorious that the restaurants aLd hotels will largely increase their charges ; the most famous will probably be able to double there at the height of the season, and other prices will be to scale. There is a suggestion, indeed, that the theatres, being certain of a larger number of applicants for tickets than they can provide for, should also rake in ex- tra gold. Now, there are many French people living abroad with slender means for whom a visit to the capital at this juncture would be a particular source of pleasure. It will be a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 2, 1900. IRUNCORN AND WIDNES,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 2, 1900. IRUNCORN AND WIDNES,</p>
        <p>time for renewing old acqnaintaneeships, and gathering up the scattered threads of friend- ship. That is, if it can be done. The thought has induced at least one French journal to arrange a scheme of board and lodging per week for its abonn?s. There you have the newspaper editor combining with his literary and journalistic func- tions those ot a licensed victualler. The tenden- cies of newspapers to go into trade outside their immediate province has been seen in England in the past few years. The most reputable of their number have already descended to book selling, and furniture selling in the mild form of bookcases. It is to be hoped they will keep this commerce within strict limits. It would be too terrible to buy opinion and a glass of beer with the same ticket. We must watch our weakness, before the next London Exhibition comes.</p>
        <p>The Warrington newsboy is an individual with whom we are all more or less familiar. Usually a denizen of ratter-tatterdom he haunt' the streets at all hours of the night endeavouring to dispose of his damp news sheets. Occasionally he dis- tinguishes himself by the unintelligibility of his street cries. At times like the present he jumps to conclusions as to the meaning of certain large letters on his "contents" bill, which are decidedly wide of the mark. But for that he cares little so long as he can command a ready sale. The war has proved a veritable copper-mine for these lads of the gutter, and they are not at all disposed to join in any demand for its early termina- tion. Last week some of the lads made a departure from their usual war-cry, with surprising results. An ex-M tyor of the town had passed away and they wer3 given instructions by a news-agent to duly announce the fact as they paraded the streets. His consternation may be better imagined than described when on going into the streets a little later ho heard the newsboys cry- ' • 4.6 mg Death of the Nest Mayor."</p>
        <p>THE MISSING RUNCORN Z'CHOONER. News has been received at Runcorn of the safety of the schooner Constance Mary (Captain J Deakin. Runcorn) bound for Charlestown with a cargo of rock salt from the Ship Canal, Runcorn ; the vessel having reached its destination the very morning it was reported missing. FARN WORTH HARRIERS. WIDNES. The team which will most probably represent the above barriers at the National Championship Meet- ing next Saturday is the following :—F Andrews, E 'Broome, P Brady, J Cadman. M Fallon, P Flynn, A Haynes, M Handley, J fiosker, WLI Jones, W Lightfoot, R Radcliffe, and J Willis. ALLEGED THEFT OF BOOTS AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday, before Mr. F Wiswall, Leonard 0 Sergeant, a young boy,of Wellington-street, was charged in custody with the larceny of a pair of boots valued at 133. the property of his father William Sargeent.—Evidence of the theft was given by the prisoner's mother, and of apprehension by Sergeent Gregory.—Prisoner was remanded on bail to Monday next. FREE LIBRARY LECTURE AT WIDNES. The fourth lecture of the series for this session in connection with the Widnes Free Library Com- mittee was delivered in the Technical Schools last Friday evening by the Rev C E Stocks, M.A., vicar of Widnes. There was a very fair attendance, pre- sided over by Mr A E Calvert. Under the guidance of the rev, lecturer the assembly spent a most enjoy- able time, he giving a graphic acceunt of" A Day in Cambridge." The limelight illustrations were of great beauty, and added much to the interest of the lecture.—Mr. Jones, of Farnwerth, proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer.--Nlr James Hough secon- ded, and the motion was unanimously carried. DEPARTURE OP Mn. W. COOK FOR SOUTH AFRICA. Last Monday evening, Mr. W Cook, lately in the employ of Mr. Weston, chemist, of Runcorn, left Runcorn Station by the 5.30 p.m. train for London, from whence he proceeded to Smith Africa on Wed- nesday. Mr. Cooke is going to Pietermaritzburg as a ehemist, under the firm of Messrs. Turner. A large number of friends assembled at the station to see him off and as the train utoved out bade him hearty " Good luck." Mr. Cook was the recipient of quite a number of presents from his Runcorn triends, not the least among them being a camera, the gift of Mr. Weston. Mr. Cook had been secre- tary of the St. Michael's Sunday School for some time, and on Sunday afternoon the teaching staff presented him with a writing desk as a memento of his connection with the school. WORKMEN'S CATHOLIC BALL AT WIDNES. On Monday evening the above annual ball was held in the Widnes Drill Hall. The spacious build- ing presented a very pretty appearance. Mr. J. Gar Kiddie had kindly lent the committee scenery, and this was very effectively arranged. The com- pany began to assemble at 8.30, and soon they were zealously engaged in the terpsichorean art to the strains of Mr. J. Gillon's band. Councillor Tom Laws provided refreshments, his catering giving great satisfaction. The decorations had been effi- ciently carried out by a committee consisting of Messrs Quinn. Fish wick, Bishop, Flannery,William- son, Ford, and J Gerrard. In connection with the ball there was a large committee, the chairman of which was Mr M Wailing; vice-chairman, Mr J Campbell; with Mr P Flannery, secretary; and Mr M'Ford, treasurer. FUNERAL OF A RUNCORN SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHER. The mortal remains of Ellen Palin Woedward, who died after a short illness, were interred in the Runcorn Cemetery on Sunday last. Deceased's un- expected death caused much sorrow in the neigh- bourhood of Union-street,where she had gained the esteem of all. Though but 17 years of age, she occupied a somewhat prominent position in Camden Sunday School, being closely associated with the sewing class. Previous to the cortege starting the mourners sang, the well-known hymn commencing "A few more years shall roll." The coffin was covered with floral tributes from her relatives and friends some being of most exquisite design, and were contributed by Mr A Palin,‘Er and Mrs Mellor, C Hulse, Mr Acton, Si Britland, S Bazley, H T Dodd, Mrs Wood ward,Mr and Mrs J Falls (London), Mr and Mrs J Lee Palma, Mr and Mrs Leath wood, "Her three friends," "Sisters and brothers." The sewing class and Sunday school class also forwarded wreaths. Before the coffin was consigned to its last resting place the mourners—among whom were R Davies, 0 Blythe, J Bazley, P Leach, W Ackerley, Horrocks, 0 N Norton, C Millinger, Rev JA B Malvern, R Bazley, A Patin, W Palin, J L Patin, C Leathwood, and Mellor—sang "The Roll Call." De- ceased's Sunday school class and sewing class were well represented. The Rev. JA B Malvern offici- ated. ALLEGED THEFT BY A LODGER AT WIDNES. At the Widnes Police Ccurt, on Monday mereing, before Alderman J. W. Wareing (in the chair) and Major Lewis, a young man named Ed win Harris, no fixed abode, was charged with stealing a silver watch chain, value 155., the property of William Jennings, 23, Luton-street, on the 21st inst.—Sarah I Jennings, wife of prosecutor, said that on February 21st the prisoner came to her house, and asked for lodgings. He said be came from Allerton, and was working on the railway at the signals between Hale- bank and Allerton. In consequence of that, she allowed him to stay. Prisoner was supplied with food by witness, and retired at eleven o'clock About 330 the next day witness left the house, leaving prisoner in the place with her sister. She saw the watch chain (produced) safe on the kitchen mantle shelf when she went out. Aboat six o'clock she returned and found that the prisoner had gone She missed the chain an straightway wens to Sir Houghton's pawnshop, a little girl having told her that the prisoner had gone to that establishment. Here she found that the chain had been pawned, and consequently gave information to the police. The prisoner had not paid her anything for his board.—Harry Morris, assistant to Mr Houghton, said that on the 21st prisoner came to the shop about 5.30. He pledged the silver watch chain for ss. In answer to witness's question as to whom it belonged prisoner replied that it was his own. He ' afterwards handed over the chain to P,C Bamford. —Prisoner, who had a bad record was committed for trial at the County intermediate Quarter Ses- sions. FUNERAL OF MRS. LEADBETTER, RUNCORN. Amidst manifestations of profound regret, the mortal remaine; of Mrs. Jessie Leadbetter, whose death we briefly recorded last week, were laid to rest in Runcorn Cemetery. The position the de- ceased occupied amongst the community worship- ping at St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, was one of more than ordinary moment, and prior to being laid in its last resting place the deceased's body was taken into the church, where the first portion of the burial service was impressively conducted by the Rev. C. W. L. Christien. In a few well-chosen remarks the rev, gentleman also reviewed the career of their beloved sister Leadbetter, whom owing to the ripe age she had attained they might well call mother. Born in Scotland, she was converted to God in that country, when some 20 years of age. She then joined that church (St. Paul's), and had been a con- sistent member for over 70 summers. It might not be given to many of those preeent to have it said of theta when they came to the end of their lives that they had been as devout and consistent as their departed sister. She came to Runcorn in 1853 and was married shortly afterwards and had been a widow for the past. 34 rare. Her long life was devoted to Christ for she had been a class leader amongst them for more than 40 years, and at one time of life had two classes. During that time hundreds of pupils had passed through,those classes. and received advice and holy influence that would influence their lives for many days. For 20 years of her life Mrs. Leadbecter was engaged in Sunday school work. One of the great features of her char- acter was her foundation in religion. She never missed a day at church that she could help. She put Gad first and all private concerns nenst be neg- lected, before the Church of God must be ' left. She loved to do the work that God gave her. She was loved by many and they should all be thankful that God hsd spared her so long to I go on with the good work. The rev. gentleman then described the last scene: He was saying to her as she lay in bed " The Lord will never forsake von," and she replied "He is always with me." From that they coull all gather consolation and comfort —The funeral cortege then re-formed, and proceeded to the cemetery. The Rev. C. W. L. I Christien also officiated at the graveside.—The mourners were :—Mr David Leadbetter (son) ; Messrs J Rocker and R Mcores (sons-in-law),Messrsl J L and F J Hoeker and Masters Douglas and Alec Moores (grandsons). Mr J Campbell, Mr George Mackie, Mr Ben Smith (Liverpool), and Mr J Har- rison (Liverpool). The bearers were: Messrs D Biebrown. T White, T Leach, T Caygle,J Weedal,and G Leadstone. A number of floral wreaths and tri- butes of respect from relatives and friends were re- ceived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EYESIGaT RTSTORED</head>
        <p>EYESIGaT RTSTORED</p>
        <p>in many cases that have been given up as hopeless. and WITHOUT an OPERATION. The success of Mr. MELLOR, the Eve Specialist, has been remarkable. See Book of Cures. free by post. Gold Medals have been received for Restoring Sight to the Blind. Nurnerom LOC.iLL CA.SES of PERFECT CURES. Consultations Free..</p>
        <p>Note the Address:-286, OXFORD-ST., MAN,CHESTER</p>
        <p>Hours :-9 to 12, 2t05,7 to 9. Cl9sed Fridays &amp; Suadays</p>
        <p>TESTIMO NI IL9.</p>
        <p>47, Hopwood-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mellor, Dear Sir,—l write to thank you for the mar- vellous cure you have made of my eyesight; previous to being recommended to you I had been a patient to the Man- chester Bye Hospital for over 2 years, and was discharged incurable, my left eye was completely blind, but aftr receiv- ing your treatment for about 7 months, my eyesight is restored, and can see better than ever I coald. I shall be pleased to recommend your treatment to everybody that needs a cure. I am, yours sincerely, Hiss R. A. HARDING.</p>
        <p>No. 11, Eldon-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mellor. Dear Sir.—Allow me to tender my sincere thanks to you for the great benefit I received whilst under your treatment with my eyes. I was recommended tq you by a little boy at the station. I came, and alter three (3) visits was soon able to see again. You are at liberty to do with this as you think propor. Yours truly, THOMAS F R ANGLETON. 1. Howley-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Sir.—l write to thank yon for the good you have done for my eyes &amp;ter so sbort a treatment (3 weeks.) The change isr truly remarkable. With pleasure I shall commend you treatment an answer any inquiry. Again thanking you. I remain, yours sincerely, BEN' IiA.LL.</p>
        <p>FEES for TREATMENT are Withia Reach of ALL. Write for my BOOK OF' CURES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortn!fzh'lly meeting of till above Board was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday last. There were present :—Messrs J Moseley Williams (chairman), A It Norman, T Reynolds, the Rural District Councillors, with Mr George F Ashton (clerk), Mr W E Rough (as- sistant clerk), arid Mr Joseph Cunnah (master.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>GUARDIANS AND THE SALE or DRINK TO CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS AND THE SALE or DRINK TO CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Cheshire Baud of Hope 'Temper- ance Union, in a lengthy circular, respecting the sale of drink to children, on behalf of the 1,500 societies in all parts of Lancashire and Chef;hire, with over 250,000 members which they represented, respectfully appealed to the Board to memorialise the House of Commons and Government in favour of 3 Bill, which Mr. Robinssn Souttar, of Dumfriesshire, bad introduced to the House of Commons during the present Parliamentary Session. On the motion of Admiral Jones-Parry, seconded by Mr. T. Wright,it was unanimously decided to memorial- ise the House of Commons in favour of the Bill. I' OR LAW CONVERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles proposed that the Chairman (Mr. Moseley Williams) and Mr. T. Darlington, with the clerk (Mr. George F. Ashton) be appointed as representatives to the Poor Law Conference, to be held in London, on the 29th and 30th of March next. ShoulJ. either of those gentlemen b 3 unable to go, he would propose that Mr. Chadwick be appointed in reserve. He (Mr. Knowles) had been to the Conference last vear,and felt that every Board ot Guardians should send representatives to the meeting. Mr. Stokes seconded, and also testified to the great advantage guardians obtained by attending the confer- ences, and added they should send different delegates every time. The resolution was unanimously agreed to. FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright reported the payments in out-relief were :—Runcorn £80; Frodsharn £24 ; Rudworth £24. Theca!! for the County rate had been received, the amount being £3.429 21. 9d.; made up as follows : County rate, £2,524 8s 111 ; conetahulary general £172 is ; constabulary local £732 123 10d. For the correspond- ing half of last year the items were :—County rate, £2,465 Ms ; canatabnlary general. £l6B Os 9d ; con- stabulary local,' £643 19s 8d ; total, £3.277 17a. Mr. Chadwick seconded, and the financial report was unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CONQUEST OF TEE BOERS,</head>
        <p>CONQUEST OF TEE BOERS,</p>
        <p>The Chairman read a message received at the foot of a business telegram to the Clerk " That Cronje had sur- rendered,' an intimation which was received with acclamation. Mr Stokes remarked that the news was more welcome because that day was the anniversary of Majaba Hill. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>IMPROVEMENTS IN THE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>IMPROVEMENTS IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The 11-mse CommittAe minutes were passed on the motion of M. A. R Norman, and contained a recom- mendation for the 'pending of £ll5 for laundry improvements. Mr. Norman observed that the com- mittee had fully considered the item anlwere convinced that it was an absolutely necessary expenditure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Police Inspection at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Police Inspection at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The annual inspection of the Warrington Police Force took place in the Drill Hall on \I onday after- noon. There were on parade the Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot), Superintendent Monaghan ,Chief Inspector Phillips, Inspectors Rowbotham and Taylor. Sergeants Evans, nollingwort h, Dean and Bell, Detectives Barlow,Meacock and Foster and 41 police constables. Sir Herbert Croft inspected the men, and there were present the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Alderman Fairclour,h, and Councillor Heaton (members of the Watch Commit;;ee), the Town Clerk (Nir J Lyon Whittle), and Dr It :..tnith (police surgeon). The men's kits having been in- spected. the Chief Constable and inspector Phillips put the constables through several movements, which were faultlessly exectited. The Inspector afterwards drove to the Police Station, and having inspected the men on duty there, examined the books, offices and cells.</p>
        <p>A Warring-ton Man Drowned at Seq.</p>
        <p>News has been received by Mr. Samuel Dunn, 54, Baxter-street, Warrington of the loss of his son. James Dunn, aged 20 years, who last week fell overboard of the s.s. Lucy, belonging to Messrs. Joseph Monks and Co., steamship owners, Warring- ton. The deceased was fireman on the steamer, which was sailing from Liverpool to Ulverston. When off Shell Wharf,Fleetwood,early in the morn- ing of the 14th inst. it is supposed that he was tak- ing down a lamp, and fell overboard, as the lamp is missing. His father with whom much sympathy is expressed is very anxious tliat the lyidy should be recovered, and is willing to give a reward to the person finding it. The deceased was sft. 9in. in height, of fair complexion, slender build, with slight scar on left side of neck. He was attired in brown trousers, woollen drawers (nearly new),dark brown stockings, woollen shirt (nearly new), one boot and one shoe on, and he had some cash in trona</p>
        <p>CLES.-13 A RGAINS.-1,500 hines, new &amp; second-hand, from each ; agents wanted ; wholesalo free any address. Warrelow Ipany, Weston-super-Mare.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER TILE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS Ix LANCAS RM.—Advt. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS A.,.-13 SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STEREL—Advt.</p>
        <p>)e rs pocket. C roase. list -.A Corn,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COVIIT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COVIIT.</p>
        <p>Jade Gwynn e Jatp.es, preNided at the Runcorn County Court last Tuesday. There was no business of public interest. Fort.)-two ordinary summonses, four default cases, and fourteen judgment sum- monses were heard. THEFT AT FRODSHA3I.</p>
        <p>William Ainsvvorth,a boy was brought before the Frodsham magistrates on Wednesday, charged with stealing an overcoat belonging to John Burgess of Frodsham on the 10th February. Both lived with the mother-in-law ot Burgess. The coat was missed from behind the door on the 10th ult., and it was proved that the prisoner pledged the article with Mr. Imison of ituneorn, for five shillings. The prisoner was sentenced to four years' detention in a reformatory.</p>
        <p>SAIL6E KILLED AT WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night John Shotton. aged 21 years, fireman un board the steamship Dungonell, of Glasgow, was killed at Weston Point. The ves- sel was moored in one of the docks unloading a cargo from Valerie. The deceased was walking along the Quay side and not noticing an embay- meat in the wall where there are some steps, he fall and alighted with his head on the steps. Re was promptly taken from the water and Dr. Delaney was called in, but he succumbed to his injuries in a short time. The deceased was a native of Sun- derland. Yesterday afternoon Mr R Dobson, coroner, held an inquest on the body and a ver- dict of accidental death was recorded. A PONY TRANSACTION.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at Frodsham Petty Sessions. James Britland, a cripple, of Connah's Quay, was charged with cruelty to a pony.—On the 6th Febraary P.S. Young found a pony in a field at Danham-o'tb-Hill. Its bones almost protruded through the skin, and when the Sergeant put his hands on its hind quar- ters it fell down.—E, T. Charming, an inspector of the R.S.P.C.A.. said the pony was in a terribly emaciated condition, and staggered like a drunken man. It ate some gruel ravenously.—The defendant said.he had exchanged his donkey and cart with Harry Ackerley, coal dealer, Runcorn, for a pony which was represented as four years old. The exchange took place at Newtown, Frodsham, but before defendant had an opportunity of examining the pony Ackerley's by drove off with the donkey sad cart. It took him three hours to get the pony to Dunham-o'th-Hill. Nit. Winston allowed hitt.. to put the animal in his field. He afterwards wrote and asked Mr. Winston to give it some hay and water.—Mr. Winston gave corroborative evidence, and said the pony was 25 years old.—The Bench said they recognised that defen lant had been taken in with the pony., but he should not have exposed it in a field.—A fine of 103 and eo3ts was impo.ed OBSTRUCTING THE ITELSBY STATIONMASTER.</p>
        <p>At Frodsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday before Messrs. C Reynolds and A. Thomas, William Jones, of Newtown, Frodsham was charged with obstruct- George Thomas. stationmaster, Helshy, while in the execution of his duty. The defendant pleaded guilty. On the 3rd February the defendant went to the booking office at Heleby Station and demanded a ticket for Frodshamodating he had no money but be would pay later. The stationmaster refused a ticket on these terms, and the defendant became abusive, made for the train, but was prevented boarding the train by the stationmaster. A struggle ensued, in which the stationmaster was thrown to the ground. The defendant informed the Bench that he thought a traveller could have n ticket without money as he had seen correspondence in a newspaper to that effect. --Mr. Reynolds said ha mast have known better than that and was fined Al and costs or 14 days. MERSEY MISSION CONCERT,</p>
        <p>The weekly series of concerts ,jziY-en at the Mersey Mission were continued on Tuesday last, when Miss , Clara Withington's concert party had charge of the programme. The proceedings were enlivened with patriotic songs, and then hearty cheers were given to commemorate Lord Roberts' success in South Africa. The items on the programme included :-- Pianoforte solo, Miss Rodd; song, "Within a mile of Edinburgh town" Miss Joh nson;piccolo solo,"Chriet- mas party," Mr J Mack; recitation, "The young dressmaker," Master A Tread well (encored); song. "Grandmother's chair" (encore "Gin a body") Miss; Stone: duet, "Gipsy Countess" (in character) Misses Stringer and Taylor; song, 'The fisherrnam and hi. child," Miss Ada Stan way; comic " A. little bit off , the top" (encore "One at a time"), Mr A. Grounds; recitation, "Toe King of the Rose," Miss F Jones; tableaux,"The song that will live for ever"(encored) Miss N Douglas ; song, "By the fountain," Mr E Roberts; this was particularly well received and Mr Roberts obliged with" Scenes that are brightest;" mandoline and piano duet, Misses E Treadwell, and C Firman ; song,"The dear home song,"Miss Burton; duet, "A.8.C." (encored) Miss Stringer and Taylor; comic song, " Delaney's chicken," Mr A Grounds. The efficient accompanist was Mr W Grounds, organist of St. Michaels Church. The chair was ably filled by Mr Reid. The usual vote of thanks having been accorded, the proceedings t!,rminated with the sineinq of "God save the Queen" A FILOBSHAM PUBLICAN FINZB. James Worrell, landlord of the Bridge Inn. Fend- sham, was chargeu with selling drink to a drunken ! person named Joseph Stanley, on the 10th February ! —Mr Giles (Cheater), prosecuted for the police and ! Mr A Browne (Warrington),defended. The evidence showed that P.C. Dickens; entered the hotel, and amongst other men in the tap room was Stanley, with a glass of hot rum beffore him. S-Anley was sick, and fell on the form, bat subsequently drank up the rum. When the beastly state of Stanley was pointed out to defendant, he replied that he had been upset by his misses and had not had time to give proper attention to the house,—After leaving the house Stanley fell down in the road. Corrobor- ative 4!) Ovid(nee was given by Sergeant Clays. who said me days after toe offence the defendant as ked him to make it light for him.—James Riddings, postmaster, Froisham Brilge, said Stanley was un- doubtedly drunk —For the defence, Mr. Browne stated that the defendant had been in the police force for 14 years, and had kept a public house in Warrington for 20 years. without any record against him. All Stanley had in the house was two glasses of rum, but he had a drink of beer with another casaomer, and this made him sick and drunk. It was not conceivable that a man like the defendant I would jeopardise his livelihood for a paltry three- pennyworth of rum. The defendant and a fellow Iworkman named William Davies gave evidence. ' The defendant was fined .£1 and cests 12s GC The Bench refused to allow the fee of the prosecuting advocate,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THR YOUNG MR'N'S CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION.— We re- gret to Pave to reelrd the disbandment of this snciety. The Associßtien, which was condect.d on nn-sectarien lines, had its headquarters at the old Ship Canal Mission Room at Wilderspool, where raeetings were held every Sunday afternoon. The work was started and carried on by Mr. John Hallows—a well-known Stockton Heath resident—Resisted by a committee of eleven members, with Mr JS Miller as peeretary and Mr J T Goodliffe as treasurer. Commencing in January 1898,with a member- ship of 80, tne growth of the association Fenn surpassed all expectations, and, by the end of the year, the num- ber of members had reached 90, there beine at that time a small balance of money in hand.—Unfertunately, the new year (1899) did not bring in its train the prosperity which had attended the preview' twelve month's, and from this time, from various Causes, there was a gradual falling off in the membership, until, in June, it became evident that the state of the fends would not pe!mit of the acseciation being worked longer on a sound financial basis. This being so, it was resolved to disoose ot the furniture and other properties and wind the Rearming ,n up.—A sale ha. been recently effected and the arnoent realized,-10 Of 91—has been generonsiy handed over by tlie committee as a donation to the Warrington In- firmary and Diepeesary.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAILURES.—ACCOrdiIIe to "Kemp's Mer- cantile: Gazette." the number of new bankruptcy prn- ceedines in England and Wales azotted during the week ending February 24th, was 89 The number in the eorrespondinq week of hie year was 74, showing an increase of 15, being a net decrease in 1900, to date, of 14.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Boomnoyp. of 134, Brider-street, Warrington is now offering his large stnelr of pianos and organs at greatly reduced urines during his clearance sale for one month only and has on vie pr, the latest automatic. elsctrio piano (penny in the slot). This electric piano has been taken up by most nf the leading hotel preprietere throughout the country. Everyone is invited to call and hear life marvellous instrument, a most ingenious and sensational inv.ntinn. Mr. Boothrovd is else show in a large variety of penny in the slot polyphone.</p>
        <p>• T.INSRED COlf FOUNT" ' for :Coughs and Colds, Asthma and Bronchitis. Of Chemists only.—Advt. USEFUL PRESBNTS of every description, See Win- dow.—J. S. GIRLAtin Ironmonger. Bridge-st.—Advt. THE MEDICAL PROFESSION are extensively recom- mending the moderate use of Port Wine in all cases o dfbility. especially after Influenzt. INVALID POET of exceptional quality. tkoronghly matured and guarantee by W. a A.. GTLBET, can be obtained of their 3,000 Agerls at 2s. 6d. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
