      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was hel dat the Workhouse, Dutto list Tuesday. Mr. Joseph Knowles presided,and there were also present t—Admiral Jones Parry, Messrs J Mclctyre. T Wright, J B Chad- wick, C E Linaker, T B octer, E Clarke, G Pining, W Fletcher, J Stokes, C Boylance, T Darlington. P Gleave, T Lewis, J Whitlow, T Antwiss, Joseph Nickson, with Mr George F Ashton (clerk)„ Mr W E Hough (assistant clerk), Mt sirs J Swinton and W Diggle (surveyors), Mr J Farrington (inspector), and Dr J Adams (medical officer).</p>
        <p>THE NURSING STAFF.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote approvint, of the payment by the Guardians of the sum of 13s 9d to Nurse E. L. Lowe, late at the Workhouse, to enable her to defray the expenses incurred by her under the cir- cumstance mentiooel in their letter. They also reques- ted that when the Guardians had appointed a nurse in the room of E.L. Lowe, the particulars of appointment be communicated to the Board. Tao Clerk intimated that would be done. ADOPTION OF CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The Chairman in the absence of Mr. C. E. Linaker trinved that all the parental rights and powers over Elizabeth Ann and Margaret Antrobus, at present in- mates of the workhouse, be vtsted in the Guqrdians of the Runcorn Union until they shall respectively attain thP age of 18 years. This was unanimously agreed to. NEW MEMBER FOR STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rovlance memed " that Mr. John Fairhurst,parish councillor for the parish of Stockton Heath be placed upon the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee." Mr. Pilling seconded,and the motion was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE SURVEYORS AND THEIR SALARIES. AN INCREASE.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'A Baxter reported that the Highways Committee had met thAt day and considered the questions of the surveyors' salaries, and the purchase of a steam roller. They after due consideration recommended that the surveyors' salaries be increased by £3O a year, making Elio. This was conditional, for at the end of each Year, the Council was also to take into ronsideration any special work which the surveyors might do for them during the twelva months and include in the next year's salary sufficient remuneration for that also. Of course, all each expenses for special work would Jai' charged to the parish for wham they were done. That course was recommended because of the order of the Local Govern- ment Board which prevented them giving the surveyors extra remuneration save in the form of salary. The committee also recommended the purchase of a steam roller, and deputed a sub-committee of five to make the necessary inquiries and adopt the best means they could for the purchase of a suitable staam roller. He proposed that the report b. adopted. _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright, secon led, and added as a result of the c'erk's enquiries respecting the advantages obtained by councils from the use of se Lm rollers, they almost all responded that the expenses were reduced by it and the roads were keot in far better order. Admiral Parry questioned whether his resolution was affected.bv. the decision of the Committee. . _ _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Linaker repliei it was not,and the only extra work the furveyors would be paid for was in connection with the Parochial Committees.</p>
        <p>Admiral Parry : All right as long as it is clearly understood.</p>
        <p>The renorrwas then unanimously adopted. Subsuquently the surveyors were made acquainted with:the decision and thankel the C•iuncil obs2rvlng they would co6tioue to discharge their duties as in the pass:.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : 0 ! No! We want more wr,rk for more pay. (L tughtPr.)</p>
        <p>A9IITON. DUTTON, AND PRESTON o.lll' HILL WATER .SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. C E Linaker submitted the • report of the Com- mittee which had met that morning to consider the estimate of the Clerk for the above water supply. The scheme was for the supply of certain property situate between the Workhouse and what he would call the turnpike road, with sufficient water. The expenditure would be about £725 64 44 for cost of works, and an annual cost of £ll6 83 11. Towards this a revenue of £9B 53 61, which would I2ave a ci.,ficiencr of £lB 2% 7d, which if it were borne by the three parishes interested would mean a rate of about one-fifth of a penny in the £ for the first year and this would be re-laced each year as the principal was repaid. The committee decided that they were willing to adopt the scheme suegPsted. sub- ject:to that portion affecting the supply of,the Workhouse being considerel by the House Cemmittee. Mr Roylance questionel whether it was necessary to have a supply tor _the house.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker and others point.d out that there was no surplus supply at the Workhouse. and in a case of fire such a supply would be very advantageous. Mr. Wright snonded, and the report was adopted sub- ject to the condition named by Mr Liner.</p>
        <p>WESTON ANT) ITS WATER SUPPLY. WATER NOT FIGURES REQUIRED. Mr. James Farrinetou, inspector, submitted the fol- lowing highly` interesting report upon the Weston water supply :—" In accordance with a resolution n,ssed at a meeting of the Council held on Tuesday,Jan. 30th. 1900, a deputation from the Parish Council, Wee- toe, cansistine of Messrs A G Guest, J Collier, GEI Garner, D and J Bennett, and W Dunbavin, also J Far- rington, inspector, as representative of the Rural Die. trial Council, attended a meeting of a committee of the Urban District Council of Runcorn, ',mid under the are- sidenay of Mr. J.Pritcherd, Os Thursday. February 22gd, in reference to the intermittent and indifferent supply of water provided and supplied to the township of Westen by the raid Urban District Council. —The report of the Inspector of Nuisances upon the subject was read, after which Mr. Guest, in the unavoidable absence of the Rev Canon Wood (the chairman of the Parish Council).srated that he knew from his own personal experience and ob- servation that the report was entirely correct, and he added that the water supnlied was at times quite unfit for domestic purposes. The other members of the de- putation, also spoke in the same strain, Mr. D. Basnett adding that he had that morn- ing on his way to the meeting called at certain premises and found that the water was off.— The Chairman, in a brief speech, said that extensive alterations and improvements had been made at the Water Works at a considerable cost for the benefit of Weston exclusively.—Mr. Wilding, the water engineer, submittei the daily record charts shewing the depth of the water in the reservoir, together with the number of consecutive hours of pumping ; and he stated that the water in the reservoir was kept much higher than it was in the Water Company's time.—Mr. Guest replied in a very kindly and lucid manner that he could not accept the statement made by the Chairman, that the altera- tions and improvements had been made at the Water Works for the benefit of Weston exclusively ; and as to the record charts, they were right enough in their place, but they did not show that Weston had a proper supply. It was of no importance to the residents in Welton to know what height the water in the reaervoir was. What they wanted was the water at Weston, so that they Phenld not be called upon to pay for that which they did not get. At this point Mr. Guest read a letter from a resident which I had been handed to him, in which it was I stated that the writer's premises had been without water I for the past three weeks. In conele.sion, Mr. Guest strongly urged the committee to recommend the Urban District Conne'l to adopt some means whereby Weston could have.a eatisfactory water sunplv.—Mr. F. Wiswall, and several other members of the Committee acknow- ledged the liability of the Council and said that it was a serious question; and one that could not be obviated ; something must he done with a view to an improvement being effected.—The qeestions of the waste of water ; the possibility of the mains being somewhat corroded : and the service pipes tieing defective, wee, d'e when the inspector seated that there was er:rheut doubt a waste of water, which was, to a great extent, due to the taps being left open whilst- the occupiers were away, in order to make sure of a supply upon their return, in the event of the water coming on during their absence. After due cons;deration, it was agreed that an examination of the mains and service pines be made by Mr.Wildineethe water engineer,and Mr.Farrington,the inspectoreiccom- panied by such members rf the deputation as thought proper to attend ; and that the cost of the examina- tion of the mains he borne by the Urban District Coun- cil, and the cost of the examination of the service pipes —where found defective—he charged to the property owners ; ale() that the result of such examination be re- ported to a future joint meting, of the committee of the deputation.—Mr, Guest !thanked the committee for the very kind and courteous manner in which the deputa- tion tied been received, after which they retired. On the motion of Mr. J. B. Chadwick, seconded by Mr. T. Wriet, it was unanimously decided to forward a copy of the report to the Westosn Parish Council.</p>
        <p>DISEASES OF THE 'MM.—Mr. William Mellor, of 286, " 9.dBiisneealeaS orfepthnetartykenwaitsha- Oxford-street, Manchost'r. successful specialist for curing rut operatinn. Since he c-Inmenced practice he has had over 27,000 successful cases, and during the last four Fears •in Manchester. this eminent sprcialist has cured over 4,000 people. To avoid the danger and suffering of operations so as not to risk the lives of patients, and to do no harm, have bops) his aims, and he has the satisfaction f knowino that his treatment for dis,ases of the eve have produced in most cases a complee cure, but in all cases saved the patient from becoming blind. As will ne seen from an advettiaernont on our fifth p.,ge scveral testimonials are publishei from Warrington patients.</p>
