        <p>4</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, snd the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease, and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man Who imagines he had only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Com- plaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of In- iluenza. whose after effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant Scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has sever since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages Sind its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Prev, ntative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym ilvans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken ins time have often proved effectual in warding off attackr of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating thei Severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you Stiffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Headache, accompanied by difficulty of breath- ing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest under end between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness ef the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bit- ters. By common consent Patients everywhere admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an at- tack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The hest way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilvm Evens' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness and Lan- gone, with greater sumps than any other known Remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indiges- tion. Sleeplemvs, Low Spirits. Nervoueness, and Loss of Appetite, Gwiiym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands un- equalled. It is sold in blttles 2; 9,l•and 4s 61., by all Chemists. and Patent Medicine Vendors, or direct car- riage tree, I r the above prices, by the- '-',onrietors : Quinine Bit ere Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the camc, "Gwilym Evers" on Label Stamp, and Bott!e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>SCENE AT THE MODEL LODGING 11811 SE.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday, before Mr Birchall and other magistrates, John Preston, who appeared in the dock minus his coat and vest, was charred with making use of threats towards Inn Davies OD Sunday morning and with doing wilful damage to the Model Lodging-house, Friars'-green, to therextent of 3.ss.—He pleaded not guilty.--Ann Davies, the com- plainant, said that she was the wife of Tnomas Davies. of 17, Friara'-green, who,was the propriet ,r of the Model Lodging-house. The prisoner acted as her deputy in look nA after the lodging-house. On Sunday morning ebe went to church leaving him in charge of the lodging- house. When she returned ehe wanted to get into the lodging-house, but found the doors locked. She asked prisoner to let her in, tu't got no answer. Liter on the pri,cn .r presented himself at the cop of tha gate with an axe (produced), in his hand, and said " If you Come over this gate I will cut your head off" She informed the police and they obtained a ladder, got into the pre- mises and apprehended the prisoner, who had broken doors and tables with the axe doing damage to the ex- tent of 1.5 i. Tbe prisoner " was supposed never to take drink, but he had been drinking on Saturday night.— Georgo Lyons said he was in the lodging-house at seven oclordt on Sunday morning when the prlssner give him a shilling and askelihim to aseist him to wash the pots and do other work. Preston then picked up an axe and commenced to barricade the doors. knocked the hinges off Vie doors, and did a great deal of damage.—P.C. Bruen said he and P.C. Ryiatt went to the "Model" on Sunday morning and found iron spikes protruding through the doors. A large bar had been placed across the gate. They saw a crowd outside the building, and were told that the prisoner was in possession of a revolver. A ladder was procured, and they and a number of men by this means got into the yard and the ace into the lodging-house and secured t'Ae prisoner, who was in a very excited state. He had barricaded the doors and had done a great deal of damage inside the house. He said he w ould no: let Mr and Mrs Davies in for 24 hours.— Prisoner now prcceeded to make a long statement and asked the Bench to send the case for tri tl because it was a very actions one. He also made charges against the landlady, who, he said, owed him money. He had, therefore, made up his mind that he would fasten her and her husband out. He was manager of the house and not the deputy, and he would fight the matter out. Let then do their bre and their worst.—The Chairman eanreqfpd the opinion that the privoner was insane.— Toe Cilief Constable; He was coherent yesterday and not as excited as he is this morning. If he is sent to gaol his condition of mind Y;111 be inquired into.—Pri- goner was committed to orison for a month, and the Chairman told him that his condition of mind would be nvired into —Prisoner: lam sound enough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY —Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Wright, Councillor Smethurst. hr. Gomel!. and Mr. Hepherd. MILITIA. A BeENT EEB.—Thomas Kelly, la.t from Wigan. was charged with being an absentee from the Kings Liverpoal (Miliria) Regiment, stationed at Fulwoed Barracks. Preston, since May, 1899.—Prisoner surrendered himself at the Police station at 8 o'clock the previous night, and told P.C, Marsh he was an absentee from the above regiment. He wa remanded to await an escort.—William Lancaster was Charged with being an absentee from the same regiment since the previous day,—P.O. Delahav said that he found the prislaner sleeping in a hay loft at 3 o'clock that morning in company with another man. He said he left Preston on Thursday and got drunk and did not intend to go back.— Prisoner was remanded to await an escort. and the officer was allowed the usual reward. CRUELTY TO A Honex.—William Partington, 17, Portland- street, was charged with illtreating a horse by working it whilst in an unfit state on the 15th February.—He pleaded guilty.—P.C. Gibbs said he saw the defendant driving a grey horse attached to an empty lurry. The horse appeared to be lame so he stopped it and examined it. He found a wound under the collar on the eft-side shoulder about the size of a 2s piece. It appeared to be an old wound.—Defendant said did not know that the horse was in that condition when he took it out. He was fined in the costs (69). WIFE ASSLULT —4llfrel. Hodgkinson, 76, Church-street. was fined 10s. for assaulting his wi-e Ala Hodgkinson on the 19th inst. LIVELY CENES AT BANK QUAY—CHEERING FOR KRUGER.— Lawrence McGeer and Margaret McGee'', Fox-street, were summoned for assaulting Ambrose McFarlan end Ann McFarlan, also of Fox street on the 18 h inst. There was a cross-summons against the McFarlans for assault.—Ambrose Maarten said that. at 5 o'clock on the evening of last Sun- day. he was going home for his tea from Crosfield'a works when he saw Mrs. McGeer. She " gave a scornful look at him and sent forth vile language." He passed on and said nothing and she turned round and struck him a violent blow in the 'sleuth. Her husband then came up and handed her a basket and a bottle. Mrs. MeGeer flourithed the bottle about his (complainant's) head but her husband pre- vented her from hitting him. He returned to work srear'ing under the insult" ani at 8 o'clock went out for impose% When he got to the work's gate he met his wife and took the supper from her. They exchanged a few words when Mrs. McGeer again came up and struck him a blow in the face. He put up his hauls in self-derence but rece vest several teows. Mrs McGeer then went to the midd;e of the street and looked for something to throw at him but could not find anything. She th.n took a bottle from a basket containing her husband's supper and struck him on the Lead with it. After that she went for her husband and he assaulted him (the complainant). His wife was also struck.—Mrs McFarlan gave evidence in support and added that McGeers were pro-Boers and Mrs McGeer was cheering for Kruger. Lawrence McGee: then gave evidenee and said that McFarlan challenged him to R4ht.—Mrs McGeer said MeV:lrian thumped her in the heed and kicked her The magistrates dismissed the case against the McFarlans and fines the McGeers Sc and costs (16s) each. WIFE DESERTION -George Harding. 132, Chapel-lane, Wigan was summoned for deserting his wife Ann Harding, 37, West-street. Warrington, on the 12th inst.—Mr Browne prosecuted and stated the facts of the case.—A separation or ter was made, defendant t I pay 5s a week towards the maintenance of his wife.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Alderman Wright. Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs. Geddes and</p>
        <p>_ S Wright. rinwrrirre'rforr. Lydia 13.clief t, 'a young woman living isr Blackhurst•street, Was summoned for causing an obstruction In Rylande-street with a handcart, on the 21st February.— P.B ',Smith stated that on the evening in question the hand- cart was in the street for half an hour.—The Chief Constable said that Mr. Potter, of the Royal Court Theatre. had com- plained very sorely of the obstruction caused by girls selling oranges in Rylands-street near the doors of the theatre. They took no notice whatever of frequent warnings.—A fine of is. was imposed and the defendant was given to understand that if she committed the offence again she would be more severely dealt with.</p>
        <p>WREN FEELING. ..LIVERISH REMEMBER that CARTER'S LITTLE LIVER PILLS '"l'ouclf* the Liver. They absolutely cure Sick Headache, Bilious. Torpid Liver, Indigestion, Constipation, Sallow Skin, Dizzines, Furred Tongue. Small pill, small Price, small dose, purely vegetable, forty in a phial. Dose Oike at :Night. Is. lid. of all Chemists. Be sure they are CARTER'S. •</p>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL TEETH FOR ALL WHO USE (sillily 012 the tooth brush a few drops of SOZODONT, tile pleasantest dentifrice in the world. Cleanses the teeth az/ Epees between thew as nothing else will. Sound and pearly white teeth, rosy lips, and fragrant bseath essiered. Ask for SOZODONT. 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Geddes (in the chair). Alderman Sutton and !dr. Birchen. OVER THE BLRELCIES WALL.—Hdith Jones and Elizabeth Hickley. young women, were charged that they., being sus- picious persons, were found in a room at Orford Barracks. with intent to commit a felony at two o'clock the previous afternoon.—They pleaded guilty —Sergeant Ball from the Barracks, said that at the time named he had occasion to go into a room. vacated the previous day, and found the prisoners hiding behind the tables. They could not give any satisfactory account of thernielves, and he. knowing that several articles, including blankets and boots, had lately been missed, sent for the police. lie thought that the prisoners must have scaled the wall in order to get into the Barracks.— Superintendent Monaghan said that Jones had been before the court cn three occasions. and Buckley had made two appearances.—They were both committ d to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions. TUESDAY.—Befora Messrs J C.Parr (in the chair) and Henry</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions. TUESDAY.—Befora Messrs J C.Parr (in the chair) and Henry</p>
        <p>Meld.</p>
        <p>COMPLAINING oF THE ViIII4HEY —George Welab', Stock- ton Heath, was summoned for being drunk and disorderly 'and refusing to quit the licensed premises of William Coppack, at Stockton Heath, on junuaty 3Elth, when requested to do so. Browne appeared for the complain- ant, and stated that oo the Saturday evening previous to the Tuesday in question the defendant went to the house, complained of the whiskey,and interfered with the company, which consisted of several respectable tradespeople. He was told to leave the house and not to go there again. On the evening of Tuesday. January 30th. he again called, and said he had come for an explanation for having been ordered out on the Saturday. His client refused to answer him, and refused to serve him. When requested to leave defendant refused, and ma e use of foul langu sge. Mr. Coppack Could not put him out, and when the police arrive I he refused, and was fercibly ejected. He now pleaded not guilty, and said that he had a perfect right to go into the house This was wrong. No person, except travellers, hen any right there whatever without the permission of the landlord. It w. 5 like a private house.—William Coppack, licensee of the Red Lion Inn,Stockton Heath and P.S. Borne gave corrobora- tive evidence.—Defendant stated that on the Saturday even- ing be called at the house and asked for a small whisky, and he was served with a mixture of whisky and gin. lie objected to this, and the landlord called him a monkey. On the Tuesday evening following be went to the house to apologise, and the landlord seized him by the neck, and tried to eject him, but he resisted.—He was fined in the coats, and Mr. Browne was granted the advocate's fee. RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL PROBECUTIoN.—James Parr, farmer. of Hatton, was summoned to show cause why an order should not be made upon him to have his trees pruned or lopped In such a manner that the roadway might not be prejudiced by the shade, and also why his hedge- should not be cut, pruned. or splashed, so that the sun and wind might not be exclude from the said roadwity.—Mr. Browne appeared for thffdefendant. and ,pleaded not guilty.—John Swinton. surveyor for the Runcorn Rural District Council, stated that the complaint had come through the Parish Coun- cil, who had written to the Rural District Council requesting them to cot the hedges and tress. The branches very often knocked off the hats of persons travelling in vehicles. After short discussion it was decided to adjourn the case for 14 days to the Magistrates' Clerk's office, Winwick-street, War- rington. In the meantime one of the magistrates would visit the spot and decide what should be done in the matter. ALLOWING COLTS To STRA.Y.—William Owen, Walton, was summonea for allowing a colt to stray On the highway on February 21th.—P.O. Lee proved the ease, and defendant. who had been cautioned previously, was fined is and costs, lames Shepherd, of Waren, was fined 51 for a similar offence committed on the same date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MoNnAY.—Before fd!sers. A. Thomas (presiding), and</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MoNnAY.—Before fd!sers. A. Thomas (presiding), and</p>
        <p>A. R. Norman.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISoRDERLY.—George Barnes. Lunt's-row. Weston. was fined 5s for bting drunk and disorderly .—Mary Ann Atherton, Mill-streeb, who pleaded guilty to a similar offence was fined 10s. Doss AT LARGE.-138ward Bowden, Trentham-street, and George Richards. butcher. 47. Widnes-road, Sims-cross. Widnes, each had to pay 4s. 6d. costs for allowing their dogs too be at large, —Benign&amp; Kyff, for using obscene language, in her residence at Top Locks, with such vehem- ence as to be heard outside was fined 10s.—The hearing of a similar charge against liar husband, John Kyff, was adjourned to the neat court. ARMARS OF MAINTENANCE. —William Ellis, Mill Brow. Runcorn, was summoned by the police for the recovery of £2 21. 6d. and the costs of the summons.—Sergeant Gill. Divisional Clerkowor t that th*laim was for arrears in respect of an order made by the Runcorn Bench in February 1899, upon the defendant William Ellis to contribute is 6d a week towards the cost of maintaining his son who was detained in the Macclesfield Industrial Sahool. There were 60 weeks owing (24 10s) but they only claimed at present for the recovery of 6 months arrears. He (Sergeant Gill) had made i 'rivalries and found defendant was in the employ of Wiggs , Works as a labourer, and during the last few weeks his I average earnings were 216 s id, He had four children, one 20 years of age, and the other three had left school Defendant. who was represented by his wife, was ordered' to pay in a month. WHAT THE POLICE HAVE TO CON rEND AGAINST.—Joseph Bushell. Milt Brow, pleaded guilty to a charge of drunken- ness and disorderly conduct. ,3oosmitted by him in High-st. on Saturday last.—Censtable Grogory said on the occasion he • was standing outside the shop of Whinyates when defendant came up to him and said " You are only in civilian clothes now, and are no better than any other man." He also said that "ho was not afraid ef him ; it was only the clothes he was afraid of." Witness tried to persuade him to go home, but he would not and subsequently tried to assault him with a can he had in his left hand and also to strike him with the right. Persuasion failing witness was obliged to close with the defendant, and he becoming very violent, the constable blew his wnistle and Constable Carrington came to his as- sistance Defendant was then locked up.—Fined 10s and 5s Costs. or 14 days in default. A TRAMP Oar ERED To LEIVE.—WiIIiaM Smith, of St. Helens, was charged in custody with begging in Nelson-st., on Sunday afternoon —Prisoner who appeared greatly atilicted with St. Vitus dance, was ordered to leave the town at once. 0 .pTURE OF A YORKSHIRE THIEF. —Sarah A.nn Billington, who was charged on a warrant with stealing goods of the valise of 5s from the dwelling house of Sarah Ann CI rke, South-street, Goole, Yorkshire, was ordered to be de- tained until handed over to The Goole police.—Evidence of apprehension of prisoner with the stolen pro _:erty in her pos- session was given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ENGLISH ACTORS IN SOUTH</head>
        <p>ENGLISH ACTORS IN SOUTH</p>
        <p>AFRICA.</p>
        <p>Disagreeable Experience of Two Artists.</p>
        <p>Wurr.sr on my usual weekly " round " of the variety palacts I happened (writes the chief reporter of the " Weekly Dispatch ") to meet Mr. and Mrs. Casson, the well-known sketch performers, who told ma a very interesting story of their via!: to South Africa. It was really from Mrs. Casson that I heard most of the story. " It is some time now," she said," since my husband and I were touring through the provincem witls " Hans tbe Boatman," and after that we made an excellent contract with the Empire Music Hill, at Johaunesburg. We had little knowledge of what was in st)re far us. Our first experience was</p>
        <p>A SMITH AFRICAN DUST-ST3RM. Without the least warning, whilst walking alang the street, you suddenly find yourself in a kind of whirl- pool of dust. It enters your ears, your mouth and eyes, and almcsl blinds you. The sensation h horrible in the extreme, and, to pers.oee of a nervous temperament. most dangerous. It was Walter who suffered most from this, and gradually I became aware of the fact that he was getting in a very bad state of health. The food there does not deserve to be called by the name ; the beef is sameth:ng like frie I oilcloth ; as for vegetables, one hardly ever hears of such luxuries, and therefore you can quite understand that I had serious misgivines when I found my husband suffering acutely from indi- mtion. It became impossible for Walter to IrAep a single thing down that be ate, apd the excruciating pain he suffered no one knows but hunsalf. Be grew thin, and bed a terribly haggard expression, caused by that dreadful feeling that comes tn those who suffer from in- digestion, of having sonr,thing heavy upon the chest. This was generally followed by a terrible headache and a dull pain right acro,s the forehead, foloweci by severe heartburn. His rest was disturbed at night, and fre- quently he had violent palpitation of the heart. We sought medical advice, but with little or no result,and I got really alarmed, for he began to lose flesh terribly. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WORRY AND ANXIBIT</head>
        <p>WORRY AND ANXIBIT</p>
        <p>14. ht '</p>
        <p>in my ease cartein,y brought about my own sefferinee from very severe nervous debiliry.and I began to wonder whether we should ever reach England alive. I happened one day, however, to go to one of our trunks that had been packed by pr afeasional friends before I lett England. It contaieee, among other things, a number of boxea of Dr.Williairs' pink pills for pale people,and I remembered having red somewhere in the eewspepar that the had cured a person who suffered acutely from indigestion, and, although Walter emiled when I suggested it, I ebyermined that he should try the pills, and to de so myself. To be truthful, I hadn't much belief in them, but a couple of days later, havieg taken the nine as directed, I certainly felt better and so did Walter. We went on taking them, and before the week had finished we were distinctly better. My husband could sleep better at night ; his fend did him more good ; tbe palpitation of the heart was cured ; and the next we, k the • lump of lead on his cheat ' tiad gole, and he was able to get about. The nasty cold perspira- tion that he had, left him, and in less than a fortuight we were very nearly well. We continued the pills on the voyage home, and when we landed in Eaela.nd we never felt better or stronger in all our lives. Occasion- ally, when a rrformaoce is ever-exacting, and we suffer at all, Waiter remarks, We had better try our old friend,' and in a very short time the pills put us in condition again. I know I have told you a lone story. Every word of it is true, and you can tell the world, througO'the press, that I am convinced that, had it not been for Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, neither of us would have been here to tell the tale.' " The nervousness and worry mentioned by Mrs. Casson —whose narrative (the" Weekly Dispatch adds) was confirmed by several friends—often accompany and sometimes cause acute ard chronic indigestion. Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, which are obtain- able everywhere, and may be a (flied upon as genuine if the package bears Dr. William" name, have, of course, a well established reputation for the cure of digestive ailments. Their value as a nerve tonic is not less notable and can be quickly appreciated by anyone in need of a tonic she will give them a trial. Their effect is prompt and well marked, and they have cured the most acute nervous dieeasel, such as epileptic fits, paralysis, loco- motor ataxy, and premature decay of monly health. They are equally efficasious in building up the blood,and have cured ansemit, consumption, rheumatism.scrofula, and other diseases that spring from vitiated or im- poverished blood.</p>
