      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Rochdale Hornets. A Defeat Avenged. These teams me:: foe the secoa I time this season at Wilderspool on S ttueday. in b-atriful weetner. and in the presence of a crowd numbering about 4,000. Both teams were fully reoreeected As the Hornets defeated Warriagton at Rochdale by 7 points to 5 early in the season, the home nem were nar.urally anxious to avenge themselves, and no!;withstandng their exertions on Wednesday against Oldham, they turned out smartly. Mr. Moore (of Oldtsem) was the referee and toe teams lined up as follows : Rochdale Hornets :—Rigg full back ;Martin, Daeies Platt, Wightman, threequerters; Allen and Kruger, half backs ; Chadwick, Langloorn, Wood, Wilcox, Hodgkin- son, Gillilands, Harrison and Aehworth. forwards. Warrington: Hallam, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Jones, and Hnckenhall, threequarters ; Bate and Burgess half backs ; Taylor, Morrison, Baardnaan, Eden, Antro- bus, Mereweather, Scholtze, and Swift, forwards. Jones started for Warrington, Martin returning. A free kick to Rochdale caused Isher wood to save in the home half. Jones returned, and Platt mulled at centre. Then Bate, Burgess, Isherwood and Fish passed beauti- fully the latter racing behind the posts- Fish landed a goal easily, to the delight of the home crowd. Harrison dribbled away for the Hornets but Jones marked cleverly. Allen and Martin got away but the latter stopped in touch when sprinting clear. Good punting by Jones kept the Hornets on the defensive. Antrobus neared and Jones kicked a fine goal at the second attempt, the first having been spoiled by a charge. Warrington showed excellent form in all depart- ments. Martin, repelling an attack by a neat mark. Burgess thought of dropping a goal, but failed to kick the ball—a ratier essential feature. He was to the fore a moment later with a short punt and tackle of Rigg. Fists received again and made a won- derful dodgy run nearly to the line. Hockenhall should have scored a moment later from Jones' opening but he was not up in time. Warrington continually attacked. Hockenh all dribbled to the line, Harrison saving from Fish. Mereweather was off for some time with injuries; but Warrington held their own. Half time, Warrington two goals one try (7 points) ; Rochdale Hornets, nil. Harrison restarted for Rochdale, Warrington having to take the 'field withoat Mereweather, whose leg was in- jured. Rochdale got going and Martin dropped the ball when a yard from the line. Tne escape Was a narrow one for Warrington and round them up. Wightman dodged clear and ran to Hellam who brought him down giaadly. aah tale con-inued to at- tack, getting possession often at the scrimmage. Fish was tackled near the goal by Martin. Jones inter- (tented and dashed off, bat wis upset. Mere w -ether returned amid cheers. A se-amble in the pool at the top end brought forth roars of laughter. Witehtman almost scored from a cross kick, Hoikenhall just stopping him in time. Warrington then attacked, Hockenhail trying to drop a goal, but failing. A heavy downpour of rain now came on and this made play rather scrambling, the ball being difficult to hold. Isherwood played a very squad game and was of in- valuable service to his side. Fine passing by Warrington gave Fish possession, and he rounded Martin splendidly only to be shoved in toacti by Allen. Thl Hornets were driven to the line, Rigg kicking clear. Warringtoe were isooa back again, and Rachdale had Kruger carried off hurt. Burgess passed to Jones near the line, and the latter scored, Fish failing at goal. Hallam was tackled near the home line, after a poor attempt to relieve. Kruger returned to the scene of action near the finish, and just at the same moment Burgess went off with a strained knee. Rigg failed by a trifle to drop a goal, and then the finish came, the score reading: Warrington, 2 goals, 3 tries (10 points); Reale dale Hornets, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Runcorn Beaten for the Second Time. A Great Game at St. Helens.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Beaten for the Second Time. A Great Game at St. Helens.</p>
        <p>More than considerable interest was manifested in the fixture, between Sc. Helens and Runcorn, which was played off at the former place last Saturday, as could be seen from the fact that over 1,000 spectators accorn• Dallied the visitors, and swelled the number contributed by St. Helens and district to something like 8,000. A i far as Runcorn was concerned a victory was confidently looked for, and by the way the conclusion was justified from the display of St. Helens at Runcorn, on the Saturday previous. To be a lit'de previous, however, the conclusion was not realised and in the result Run- corn were defeated by 3 points to 2 poinas. The teams were as follows :—Sc. Helens : Foulkes, back ; Hunter, Barnes, Whitley', Doherty, threequerters; Boyle ane Appleton, halves ; Winstanley, erescott, Thompson, Bri re, Mooney, Dale, Rennie and Green. Ruocern : Houghton, back ; C and J Butterworth, D Jones, T Wilkinson,threequarters ; Faulkner and Jolley, halves ; Walker, Farmer, Gayter, Reece, Hughes, Light- burn, Taylor and Darlington. Barnes commenced. Wilkinson returned and Farmer tackling keenly gained considerab:e ground. Tbel game was remarkably keen both teams playing well. St. Helens followed up finely and tackled Jolley. From a penalty Barnes tried a drop but was very wide of the pasts. Runcorn then attacked spiritedly end maintained a good „lace in the bomb territory. SI. Haloes' passing was good. Jolley and Faulkner pas,-d out grandly and Jimmy had a chance to drop but failel miserably. Gayter held a rush finely. Houghton saved but was roughly mauled whilst on the ground. Another beautiful sprint on the St. Helens men's part was cheered,Doberty being especially fine.Runcorn made off spiritedly. Jolley eeicked up and passed to Faulkner and he fed Charlie Butter- worth, and with a fine effort the latter nearly succeeded in scoring. Hougliton saved with a mark near the posts shortly afterwards, and seat into touch. This he re- peated. A five yards scrummage r,sulted and Runcorn's chances locked bad ; but the seige was raised C Butter- worth being prominent. St. Helens then held the advantage and pressed. Appleton had a splendid chance to drop bet Wilkinson charged down. Rencorn then rushed away, but a good mark was awarded the Saints. Houghton, Jolley, and Jones were fine on;the defence, and averted almost certain disaster. Runcorn were starting off with a pas- sing movement, but Butterworth's pass out was charged and Whitley got away. Houghton magnificently saved a certain try., The Saints still assumed the aggressive and were at times very dangerous, Houghton being twice called upon to kick or touch dead. Runcorn had la chance, but the Saints rushed away, and Houghton fumbling had to fall upon tie,. ball to save. Seeping up ' the great pace at which the game started, St. Helens and Runcorn treated the crowd to a great game. The sun breaking out from a bank of cloud aided a further ad- vantage to the Saints. Briers feeding Appleton. that player put in a good run and only Houghton could save. Hunter added to the excitement with a good drop. Play was transferred by huge kicking from end to end. Far- mer gained ground, but Hunter replied equally as well. A passing move by Faulkner and the two Butterworths was responded to by another from Boyle and Briers. Jolley was slightly hurt but resumed. Still pressing. chance after chanee fell to St. Helens. Foulkes was then called upon and responded coolly. Attacking again it fell upon Jones to defee d, and he did it fully up to expectation. J. Butterworth then made things certain by a further effort and was deservedly applauded. Half- time : Runcorn, mil; St. Helens, nil. The interval was taken up with a highly amusing in- cident, which illustrated that even tbe dogs of St. Helens fully enter into the opirit of football. A little dog entered the enclosure, and accomplished some fine dribbling finally dribbling the ball out of touch. Farmer recommenced and at once Runcorn assumed the ageressive. Foulkes kicked mut, and upon the reame of the ball which had been on a visit over the stands, a further advantage accrued. Whilst attempting a catch F. Gay ter was laid out, and from the free kick Farmer sent into touch. Both teams then tried footwork. The next point of interest was a fine mark on the run from which Jolley secured considerable ground. Foulkts' back play was however too good, and no score resulted. Hunter made a bravo attempt and only for Faulkner securing his jersey there would have been a race for the Runcorn line. Foulkes further im- proved the conditions and Houghton RAI tackled in his own half. Then a brilliant attempt by the whole of the Saint; pack was made ae scoring, and once again the superb defence of the visitors alone saved them. Fonlkes, Barnes, and Hunter were dangerous, but C. Butterworth pushed the latter into touch. Carrying all before them Runcorn forwards made inroads upon Foulkes, but when dangerous Lightfoot was unfortun- ately hurt, and a cessation of play resultel. Lightfoot could not resume. Jolley picked up from the scrum and dropped a beautiful goal. Excitement was intense. Shortly after Lightfoot resumed, and was instruments in causing St. Helens to retire, from a strong position they held on the Runcorn left. Whitley was cautioned for tackling Faulkner. Jolley was the next laid out, and after marking too. Then things began to bum. The Saints played finely and Houghton, Jolley, and Butter- worth were prominent time after time. Pressing hard Runcorn caused Faulkner to mark under his posts. Houghton received and tried a drop which was only a little wide. From a rush St. Helens got over the line Houghton failing to drop upon it. Briers placed right under the posts. Foulkes failed to,convert from the easy position. Pressing irresistibly Rtlocorn got a chance but Brifis saved. Then the game was about equally divided,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RECREATION V. FLETCHER RUSsELL'S.—At RUn.lnro oo Siturd4y. Toe li-creation held the advan- t-ir in the first half, and Wriqht scored a try. In the second hal', after even pky, K)nnioa added a sacond try, and Wright a third, Fin :—Runcorn Recreation, one;goal and three ties ; Fletcher R isell's, nil. WIDNES WHITE STAR V. LATCHFORD RANGERS.—At Widnes oo Siturday. Star kicked off and pres9ed, but Latcotord scored, the kick failing. After good passing th- visit )rs scored ain. Half time Latchford, six points ; Star, nil. Fr m tie restart Holland scored. Fiaal :—Latchford, six points : Star, three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ASSOCIATIOI FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATIOI FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District League.</p>
        <p>Warrington Albion V. Earrestown Rovers.</p>
        <p>At Warrington. Earlestown won the toss and White- field kicked off, the opening exchanges being in favour of the visitlrs. Fielding forced a corner for the Albion with no result. Earlestown had tw3 corners awarded them, but failed to take advantage. From a throw-in E lison scored for the visitors. Morris scored the second goal, while Harrison late in the first half, notched the third point. E,en play followei Earlestowa pressing. Ralf-time—Earlest)wn thr-.e, Albion nil. The second half was in favour of the visitors, full time being called with the score. Earlestown five goals, Albian one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Lancashire League.</head>
        <p>Lancashire League.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN V. 01:ITTI LIVEIIPOOL.—For the first time for a rnenth Eerlestown appeared on the Mesnes enclo- sure in a League encounter their opponents being the present wooden spoonists. The match was played up to the interval on Boxing Day but was then postponed owing t 3 the inclement weather. Saturday opened very prouaising but towards noon the sky became overclouded but, fortunately the rain held off until near the close of the match. Both teams were fully represented ,the visitors ranks including Robson, tbe old Haydeck and New Brighton 'rower player. Tao homester3 won the toss, and electel to kick against the slope but with a slight breeze behind them. Ceesidering the heavy state of the turf the gaerve opened very feet, South Liverpool having slightly the best of the exchanges. After a corner to the. visitors had been safely ne,eotiated, Ellis and Whitehouse initiated a Mee passing move• ment between toe home quintette, White ,ouse eventu- ally swinging the ball across to Leach who was left with an open goat, but his shot looked sting and was easily saved by Bennett. A sudden brook away by the visitors' forwards bothered the home defenders and Morgan was going strongly for goal when ho CVACI pulled up by Cottain. The same player soon afterwards again obtained possessien but in passing to his partner he kicked the Is ather to Heyes.The home back however missed his kick and Morgen pounced upon the ball like lightning ani breaking through the defence he gave Chow no chance ' with a capital shot which ent ired the corner of the net. This put the heal:wens no tneir mettle and thee fairly bombarded the visitors' goal. For a time all efforts at a score were repulsed, although one eff art of Johnson's deserves spcniel mention. He received the ball near the half way lino and drioble i it a short way and tnen let ftv for all no was worth. The ball flew hard and straight at Bennett and now he managed to save it was a wonder to everyone. He did save it however, and a fruitless corner was the only result of the best bit of individual play of the game. Just before the interval Ellis received a pass from El ampsou and trickily eluding the backs he banged the ball into tie net at a terrific rate the cus- todian having no possible chance of scoring. Encouraged by tots success, the home forwards renewed the attack and in clearing a shot from Settle one of the visiting backs dallied too long and Ellis dashing up the ball cannoned off his foot into the net, two goals thus being scored in as many minutes. At the interval the home.sters were therefore leading by two goals to one. The restart was made in a downpour of rain but this temporarily cleared off. A foul close in for the Liver- pudlians looked ominous for the homesters but a huge kick by Eleyes transferred the venue. At this period rain was falling in torrents and after enduring it for five or ten minutes the players made for shelter. After a delay of five minutes the VIM, was recurneel and a splendid piece of work by Eiiii gave Whitehouse a per- fect opening. The lit:le inside right made full use of his opportunity as he banged the ball past Bennett at light- ning speed. Towards the close the visitors' left wing hampered Heyes so much that ha was forced to concede a corner. This wee well placed and after bobbing about ' for a short time ultimately found a resting place in the corner of the net,haeing been headed there by Copeland. / No more scoring was doae and the game therefore re- Suited in a wire far trlesto ccu by three goals to two.. _ _</p>
        <p>Tee score about represents the merits of the respective teams, although with a bit of luck the locals might have popped ces three or four more points. However, the score was sufficient to gain two pint, so perhaps the homegters are satisfied. Chew had very little to do in the Earlest)wn goal, &amp;though he ought te have saved the second goal. The backs were very safe; oi con- sidering the greasy statz of the ball, kicked with wonder- ful accuracy. Of the half backs Johnson was head and shoulders above the other two. He was here, there and everywhere, and never seemed to tire, and was about the best man on the field. Of the forwards Ellis was the pick and as he scored two of the goals he took the eye as being the cleverest forward on the field. The visitors' goal keeper had three times as ma • v shots to stop as Chew, and right well did he do his work ; coming to the rescue of his side time after time when tile backa were beaten. The backs were not near as gool as the home pair and suffered in cmaparison. Ro osm was the pick of the intermediate line against him. Settle and Leach—who are usually so effective—could do little. Morgan was about the bect of the forwards, although Copeland</p>
        <p>played a sterling ame. Altogether it WAS uot a great game, neithlr sith sh-) wing good football, but undoubt- edly the better team won.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Cheetham</p>
        <p>Latchtord regained their matches on Saturday after a nu:oath's rest, having been debarred by the inclement weather. Soon after the start Cheetham scored from a rush, but this proved their only score of the fire, half,as Latchford were attacking vigorously till the whistle sounded, having notched five goals. The second half was more evenly contested, the home team scoring three and the visiters two. Carter was in exceptionally fine form, making some grand runs, and scoring five goals. The others were scored by E Grounds (2), J Pearson (1). The half backs also played "a good game. Final score : r—Latchford, eight ; Cheetham, three.</p>
        <p>Billiard Handicap at ttTe Conservative Club. the final game in the annual billiard handicap was played at the Conservative Club on Tuesday evening,and the prizes were afterwards distributed. The final game was between Mr T Horrabin and Mr J Pennington,and resulted after an interesting game in a win for Mr florrabin,by 200 to 168. Instead of one general handicap, two, one for each room, took place, and Tuesday night's game was the final of the second room handicap. Mr Horrabin won the first prize in both rooms. An excellent committee was formed, Mr James St7ettle acting as hon secre- tary and Mr Robert Daintith as bon treasurer. At the conclusion of the game, a large company sat down to an excellent hot-pot supper. Alderman Sutton presided, and after the toast. of the Queen had been honoured, he distributed the prizes. The following is a list of the prize winners : —First class room: 1. clock, T Ilurrabin, sea,; 2, travelling bag, T Sutton ; 3, dressing case, F Redfern ; 4, din- ner cruet, P Coake ; 5, spirit flank ; 6, cue an d case, P Radford ; 7, butter dish, F Ituklani ; 8 cigarette case, C Oldfield; 9, comb and brush, J tt. Unswortb; 10, card cave, J Mottratn ; 11, walking stick. H Ntackey ; 12, jam dish, J Sampson ; 13, pen knife, J Strettle. Second class room: 1, gold albert, T Horrabin. sen. ; 2, clock. J Pennington •, 3, baro- meter, C Leigh ' • 4, cake knife, W Taylor; 5, travel- ling bag, G H Smith; 6, teapot, T Cooper ; 7, case of carvers, It Straw ;8, cue and case, John Cox. The remaining eight prizes have yet to be played for. The Warrington Quartette Party (Messrs. Scatter- good, Davenport, Whitehouse, and Sherlock), Messrs. Whitehouse, Parr, W. W. Whittle, Jones, Poole, L Aukland, G Smith, and others contri- buted items of a musical character during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By "Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>Warrington had no difficulty in defeating Rochdale Hornets on Saturday, though the visitors brought their best team—with the exception of Field, who is on the injured list. After the performance of the Warrington lads against Oldham, there was a ceneral feeling of con- fiderce that they would have no difficulty in avenging themselves on the Hornets for the reverse sustained so long ago as Septnnber 23rd. Tim team is altogether Playing better football just now. There was good ground for feeling that this match at least was something litre a certainty for the 130 KP Club. As events turned out the men from the valley of the Roach were unable to score at all, while Warrington comfortably compiled ten pcints. As the defeat at Rochdale was only by 7 points to 5, this was a satisfactory paying off of old scores.</p>
        <p>The weather was reasonably good, though a heavy shower fell just tefore the close of the match. One ex- pected a somewhat larger crowd however than the three or four thousand speccators who as3embled. On the " classy " side thcre was a particularly thin sprinkling of onlookers. Those who were there saw some good football during the afternoon, and especially in the earlier stages of the game. They also had a bit of fun at the expense of the players, for two or three fairly large pools at the railway end of the ground caused an amount of floundering and mud larking which seemed just to the taste of these who were safely out of it.</p>
        <p>Like the game with Oldham, the one on Saturday °pence, sensationally, for a score was registered in the first five minutes. Fish was the serer on Saturday,and his try followed as pretty a piece of combination between Burgess, Bate, and Isherwood as one could wish to see. The clever wing man ma le no mistake with his oppor- tunity and planted the ball behind the posts, afterwards kieking an easy goal. This practically settled matters, for the Hornets could make no headway against the speedy and polished play of the home team. Jones added a penalty goal, with a well judged kick, and the teams crossed over with the score standing at T points to nil in favour of Warrington.</p>
        <p>The second half calls for little comment, as the home team practically contented triernselves with keeping the Hornetn out. Jones took advantage of a scrimmage near the line to dash over and raise the total to 10 points. Martin should have credited the visitors with a similar try for he had the bill put in his hands near the line,with no one to face,and yet ho failed to score. The reason appeared to lie in the slushy pool which barred his way,for the ex-Olclhamite certainly appeared to jib at " crooning the water." Had he dropped on the line just here he might have been drowned, so perhaps his ad- mirers will excuse him. The Warrington men chuckled hugely over the matter.</p>
        <p>Three men were accidentally hurt during the game. Kruger oif Rochdale, and Mereweather and Burgess of tVarrington. The first two were able to resume, but the plucky little Warrington ly.lf back, Burgess,strained his knee and had to be taken from the field in a cab. He had played a rattling game, and deserved the en- couraging cheers which sounded as he was borne through the crowd.</p>
        <p>Bate, Burgess, Fish, aid Isherwood were the best men on the home side, and it P 73,9 clearly proved what a strong quartette they are. The lett wing men are playing very well together, and the way Isherwood beat Martin and Rigg for Fish t.) ec9re was a treat. Jones and flockenhall did very fairly, but Hallam was terribly slow in clearing when pressed. Rigg and Kruger were the pick of the Hornets' backs,who as a whole were out- classed. Both sets of forwards worked manfully, and it was only in heeling out that Warrington had any advantage here.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire League table up-to-date is as fol- lows : For.</p>
        <p>Against.</p>
