      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>BURNING FATALITY.</head>
        <p>BURNING FATALITY.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Girl's Sad Death.</p>
        <p>An inquiry, concerning the death of Nellie Berry,aged four years,who died at the Infirmary from the effects of burns received at her grandparents' house,6,School Brow, on Friday morning last, was conducted in the Parade Room at the Warrington Police Station on Monday by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner. The first witness was Jane Berry who stated that the deceased was her granddaughter and lived with her at 6. Sehool,Brow. Her father and m-ther did not live in Warrington. At 6.20. on Friday morning she left her daughter and the deceased asleep in the house and went to work. Her husband went at 5.20, and the daughter had to go at 8.30. About nine o'clock she called at the house to see if the deceased,who was the only one in the house, was up. She was not up. and witness again left the house. She returned at 9 30, and finding the house full of smoke, rushed upstairs and saw the deceased's bedroom full of smoke. A neighbour was brought into the house, but she could not eater the room. The deceased was called and she was found in another room, aD the bottom of a bed, burning. The child was taken to the Infirmary and died at the time stated. The weekly wages of herself, her husband, and her daughter amounted to about £2. The Coroner asked witness if there was no one about who could take care of the child during her absence? Witness: Yes, a neighbour. The Coroner said it was not right to leave a child by itself in the house. _ _</p>
        <p>Witnegs, continuing, said she found a box of matches on a chair in the bedroom, which was generally left there for her daughter, who that morning had forgotten to take them downstairs.</p>
        <p>Sarah Speakman, a neighbour of the last witness, said she we aware that Mrs. Berry went out to work every morning. On Friday morning she heard Mrs. Berry scream and she rushed into the house and went upstairs. She found the bedroom of the deceased full of smoke and subsequently found the deceased lying burning on a bed in another room, which was also full of smoke. Nurse Robinson saifi the deceased was admitted on Friday morning at 10.20 suffering from severe burns all over the body and she died at 11 40 the same morn- ing.</p>
        <p>The Conner, in summing up, said it was a terrible thing to leave a child in bed and no one in the house. It was bid enlugh to leave a child in a kitchen. The grandmother should have asked somebody to take care of the child while she was away. Cases of this kind were continually coming before him, and he was quite satis- fied that the greater portion of them might have been avoided if ordinary care had been taken. The fact remained that the child, who was unable to take care of herself, met with a death that might easily have been avoided. _</p>
        <p>- After a short discussion the jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and expressed the opinion that it wasoa case of great negligence on the part of all con- cerned. The Coroner thoroughly agreed with the jury, and addressing the grandmother remarked that he was deter mined to put a step to these kind of cases. In nearly every case there was some negligence on the parts ef the parents, &amp;:. She took the trout:At to leave her work and go home but did not think of goine upstairs. If she had done so ths accident might have been prevented. He had never heard such a pitiful case since his appoint- ment as a coroner.</p>
        <p>WALL COUNTRY PROPLII should read The Rural World d. weekly, which contains articles or T-14, sea, Cattle. thie Dairy, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs, Gardening, Natural History, Botany or the Farm and Garden, the Nome, Foreign and Colonial Intelligence. Anwers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustrated and is altogether, a most interest- and instructive farming and country paper. A good ins -din for servants requiring situations. The Manager Ine to send a specimen free. The address is 110 and TRLND, London, W.—Advt,</p>
        <p>SYMINGTON'S</p>
