      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>• ra".l.• e</head>
        <p>A • ra".l.• e</p>
        <p>much good in all cases where</p>
        <p>he has tried it.</p>
        <p>(From a r</p>
        <p>Outside the medical profession few persons are better qualified to judge of the merits of any remedy than professional nurses. The nurse is with the patient day and night, and watches carefully every symptom, both favourable and unfavourable. It is the duty of the nurse to watch the effect of the medicines administired and to report to the doctor everything observed. Consequently, we publish the following statement from a graduate male nurse of eleven years' experience with much gratification. "24, John-street, Oxbrd-street, "Southampton, - "February 19th, 1899.</p>
        <p>" Dear Sirs,—l have great pleasure in stating that in my eleven years' experience as a graduate male nurse, in the many cases In s hich I have tried Scott's Emulsion of cod liver oil,in all of them it has done much good. Patients soon gain in weight,and also rega'n health and strength atter taking only a few bottles. It seems to me to be a most valuable preparation in all cases of asthma, bronchitis and consumption,as I think it both strengthens and beals the lungs. I think it always one's duty if he knows a geed thing to let others know it also.—Ycolrs</p>
        <p>says Scott's Emulsion has done</p>
