        <p>STATE OF CRIME IN</p>
        <p>WARRING lON.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable's Report. TCe Chief U.,iitabe of wsrringson (Mr. Luke Talbst ), be + jda'; issued his anew eort upon the work of the pshce :ore, during slis ye .r 1339,t,gether with the crim- inal ststist cal ietures f)r the sale nerioel.lt anpesrs that the number of indictable °fitness cornrritted during the year was 96 as compared with 119 in 1898, thus showing a marked e.ecrease of serious criers witnin the Borough. There wa3 an increase under the head of non-indictable offences of 260 persons. Persons proceeded against (Liter by apprehension or upon summoes as compared with that of last year. Toe differ- ence is chiefly aceonnted for by a greater numher of prosecutions for drunkenness and school offences. In aoreturn indicating the number of habitual criminals at large within the borough and the number of houses of bad character ex:sting on a certain date in the year it is stated that the habitual criminals number 33, 26 being male and 7 femsle. 'The houses of bad character 4. The Chief Constable also gives a return showing the number of houses licensed for the sale of intoxicating liquors for the past ten years with details of licensing proEeeutions during the same period. From this it appars that the numbers of licensed houses in 1899 WAS 87 licalsel victuallers, 40 heerhonse keepe,s and 85 shop keepers total 212. In 1890 there were 87 licensed victuallers, 48 beerhouse keepers and 70 shop keepers, total 205 The number of pnsons charged with drunkenness was 405 in 1899 as compared until 297 in 1898 ; 277 in 1897, 270 in 1896. 376 in 1895, 364 in 1894, 606 in 1893, 680 in 1892, 773 in 1891, and 806 in 1890. From another statement it appears that the number of robberies reported in 1899 was 99 against 116 in 1898. The value of the property stolen in 1899 was £l6O 18a 93 and of this sum £9l 15: 103 was recovered by the police. POLLCE ORGANISATION AND ESTABLISTEMEKT. The force new C3149i5t5 of one chief c mstable, one de- tective superintendent, one chief inspector (who is also chief clerk, drill instructor and store keeper), two patrol inspectors, six patrol sergeants, one court sergeant (who is also warrant officer and process server), one Mayor's sergeant,one bridewell serseant, aEd 50 uniform constables, There are five detective officers,one of whom also sets as assistant hsslsney carriage inspector and Coroner's officer. One of th 3 patrol inspectors is inspec- tor of hackney carriages and is curator of the S- ssions House and bridewell. The total authorised strength of the force is 69, and is in proportion of one constable to every 91304 of the estimated population (63,000) and to every 45.17 scrc s of the Borough. The average strength of police forces in the North, rn District of England is one constable to every 671 of the population. In addition to the authorised stret4;cii there are one sergeant and three constables appointed and maintained at private cost for duty upon the premises of th3ir employers. Tney are under the controt of the Borough Police Authority, and make a total of. 73 men s 3 controlled. Sixty-one mem- bers of the force hold certificates of the St. Joha Ambul ance Association, and have rendered first aid to 48 in jured persons during the year. VARIATIONS AND CONDUCT.</p>
        <p>During the year one constable resigned voluntarily to take other emplyment, and one constable was called upon to resign for misconduct. Several other members of the force woe reported for breathes of discipline, which were chiefly of a trifling nature ; the cases being met with small fines and reprimands. The general con- duct of the borough police, has been good.</p>
        <p>MISCISLIANEOUS INFOIVMATION. The Police have, during the year, specially supervised 101 houses left temporarily unoccupied by their tenants, and in no case has there been any report of a robbery from such premises. The Chief Constable again respect- fully invites persons leaving borne at holiday or other times, to apprise him of tneir intended absence, with a view to the special attention of the police being paid to their reeidences if left unoccupied. There are now in the borough 87 licensed victuallers,4o beerhouse keepers, and 74 shopkeepers licensed for the eale of beer not to be consumed on the premices. There are, in addition, 13 places licensed for the sale of wine. Forty-four places are licensed for music and dancing, and 10 for the per- formance of stage plays. The sum of £69 183 6d has been collected from parents and guardians by the police under magisterial orders, towards the partial mainten- ance of children detained in Reformatory and Industrial Schools. This stem was for warded to H.M. Inspector of Reformatories. There have been issued at the Pence Office, during 1899, 49 pedlars' certificates, in repect of which £ll 12s 9i has been paid to the Borough Treasurer. The same official has further received during the year £lO 133 61 for dog fees, and £36 4s 10.' for sp,cial duties performed by the police, and other similar matters. rota' paid to Boreneh Treasurer in 1899—£58 11 id. The sum of £6B 163 Od in fines and fees has been collected and paid to the Justices Clerk, on account of penalties not pail in court by defendants at the time of the hearing of their cases. There are nine proprietors of cabs and omnibuses in the Borough, and 108 drivers licensed to ply for hire. Six persons are licensed to deal in marine stores ; and 62 hold licenses to sell explosives, During the year the police have executed 253 warrants for apprehension or commitment, and have served 1,336 summonses! Town Council and Corporation Committee Notices to the number of 4,298 have also been delivered by them. IDuring the same period, 14 persons wanted for offences ' committed in other localities have been apprehended by the Warrington Borough Police, and handed over to the proper authorities. Five hundred and thirty-four persons have been con- veyed from Warrington to H.M. Prisons during the year. Nine Convicts, on ticket-of-leave, have reported them- selves to the police during the year ; four subsequently left the district, and one continues to report. Two licenses have expired, whilst one license holder has been committed to prison for failing to report, and one has been committed on indictment for felony. Eight runaway horses have been stopped by members of the Force at personal ri&amp;.. During the year lost pro- perty to the value of £75 has been found or recovered by the police. Under the Rabies Act, the police have seized 162 dogs, of which 106 were restored to their owners, 53 were destroyed, and 3 escaped from custody. Captured dogs have been detained im suitable covered kennels in the Corporation yard. unclaimed animals being destroyed by means of a satisfactory apparatus on the Police preentses. One hundred and fourteen cases of sudden, premature or v,olent death have been investigated by the Police and reported to the coroner. In 63 cases there were noiaquests. In 51 eases inquests were held. and verdicts eturned as follows, viz :-34 " Accidental death," 2" Death from natural causes," 5 " Suicide whilst temporarily insane." 7 " Found drowned," 1 " Death by misadventure," 1 "Excessive drinking," and 1 "Manslaughter." Lost children to the number of 209 havebeen restored by the police to their parents or guardians. Sixty-three horses, cattle and sheep were found straying, and handed over to their owners. 13 fires occurred in the borough during the year, doing damage to the amount of about £4,305. The police upon night duty have found 396 shops, warehouses, and private houses insecure through doors, windows, etc., being left open or unfastened. The occupiers of the premises in such cases were apprised and cautioned. Notifications concerning robberies or loss of property to the number of 1,374 have been received from other Police Forces, and have been circulated and inquired into by the Warrington Police in this district. The gross cost of the police establishment for the year 1899 was £6,580 is 61., halt of which is repayable by the Lancashire County Council. During 1898 the cost was £6,501 13s 41. The Police Superannuation Fund amounts so £11,981 18s 01., the total income from all sources during the year being £917 17s 3d., and the charges upon it amounting to £554 7e 31. Upon the pension list are 1 ex-chief constable, 2 ex-chief inspectors 1 ex-inspector, and 1 ex-constable. One pensioner (ex-Inspector Bowen) died during the year. Sir Herbert Croft, Bart. (H. M. Inspector of Constabulary), inspected the force on the 28t1a of February, arid pronounced it efficient as to phys:que, numbers, discipline, and equipment. He expressed satistacti. n with the method and accuracy of keeping the police books and records. In conclusion, the Chief Constable tenders his thanks to the Chairman and the members of the Watch Committee ; the Town Clerk and officials of the various Corporation departments ; etni to Mr. G.F.F. White,the deputy clerk to the justiees.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARC</p>
