      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>NEW HOTEL AT HOWLEY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW HOTEL AT HOWLEY.</p>
        <p>Final Grant Made.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Warrington Police Court, before Mr. T. Birchall (presiding) and other justices, Mr. J. Longland, solicitor; made an application on behalf of Alderman W. Boltov, for the final grant of the license to the new hotel at Howley, for which a provisional grant was made in August, 1898. He stated that tbe hotel was now ready for occuratioa, but Mr. Bolton did not propose to commence selling until Tuesday. Two houses of his were now closed, " The Crow "at Market-Gate, and "The Rose" in Dial-street. and an off-licensed house in Lord Nelson-qtreet would be finally closed that night. Alderman Wright: I suppose we must understand that the new house is built in accordance with the plans? Mr. Longland said an inspection had been made en behalf of the police. The Chief Cmstable stated that there had been a slight deviation from the plans. There was no stabling pro- vided, and a partition had been put np in the vaults for the convenience of the outdoor customers which was a distinct improvement. Mr. Longland explained that it had been proposed to erect stables in one corner of the yard, but it was found the manure heap would be too near some cottages. The stables would now be built in another part of the yard. , The application was granted,and temporary authority to sell (until the next transfer day) sa as granted to Joseph Reed,the holder of the off-license in Lord Nelson- street.</p>
