      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>War and the Call to Prayer.</head>
        <p>War and the Call to Prayer.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Many years ago, when Lord Palmerston w.s in Powrr, a northern city waP sorely affl:cted by an alarm- ing outbreak of cholera. Death was slaying its victims on every hand, and in the universal terror in-mired by its ravages it occurred to the good men who ministered to the SOWN of the people that a day of national fasting and humiliation might prove effectual in arresting the plague. And acting upon this idea a clerical deputation waited upon his lordship and prayed him to appoint a day for the purpose. The answer of the astute states- man was characteristic and to the point. In his blandrst phrases he informed the deputation that he thought it would be time enough to invoke the aid of the Almighty when they had thoroughly cleansed the city clove and made the sanitary conditions of the people what they ought to</p>
        <p>Row I think a line of argument somewhat similar to his lordship's might be fitly applied to the pious people who are suggesting a day of public prayer on account of the war now raging in South Africa. The bare mention of such a day leads one to ask the grave qaestinn, what have our Christ ia.n instructors done in the past to dis- courage and put down strife and blond-shedding among mankind ?—The awful records of Wet,rlon. India, the Crimea,the Soudan, and other scenes of modern carnage carried oa by men bearing the Christian name forbid one to believe that their labours in the cause of peene and righteousness were either great or effectual. And what are our priests and pastors now doing to stop the terrible war going on in Africa.not between savages but between members of Christian churches? Never at any previous period in the history of Christendom were preachers so numerous and so costly as they are to- day; and never was the civilized world in in such a state of preparedness for war as it is at the present msancnt. It would seem, indred, as qua ,n in all countries were getting ready far a slaughter ie the near future that will astonish even the powers of dark- noes themselves. In our own land the demon of war rides rampent over everything ; men, women and even children of all classes clamouring for vengeance upon a people they have never ven and of whose history most of them are entirely ignorant. My by day ship loads of armed men leave our shores with the object of killing and maiming fellow creatures six thousand miles away ; while excited and cheering crowds line the splays and wharves of our seaports to bid them God-speed on their awful mission And all this goes on with the apvroval and by the authority of the Head of the State Church and her christian counsellors, at a cost, probably, of four millions sterling a month. While, sad to relate, concurrently with this vast developreent of militarism and extravagance thousands of the poor and worn out toilers whose labours centributed so largely to the nation's wealth are left to pine and die under the miserable shelter of the work- house, and millions of our fellow subjects in India are perishing thrsmeh starvation. If I could be- lieve that this deplorable state of things was the natural outcome of the preaching of the Gospel of Christ I should be compelled to regard that gospel as an enemy and not as a friend of mankind. But where is the man, lay or clerical, who will venture to associate the heavenly cos- el of peace with the horror. of the battlefield ? Both In the pulpit end out of it one hears a good deal of pious talk abut !mit and righteous wars, but I have never yet heard of any one pho attempted to justify any war on the principle of Jesus, our exemplar in all things. No. No. The Gospel of Christ is one of love, and not of hatred ; of mercy and not of vengeance. There is not a sentence, not even a line in the whole history of the founder of Christianity and His apostles that can even be wrested to cnuntenence the shedding of human blood under any circumstances whatever. On the contrary, pure unselfish love to Gcel and man is the spirit which breathes throughout the whole of that divine revelation. It teaches men to live day by day in dependence upon God, to deal kindly and jesly with each other, to love their enemies, and never to render evil for evil. The preaching of this gospel involved the Lord and His disciples in poverty, nerseeation and death. And as the gospel and human nature remain the same as they were at the beginning, it follows that he who would fully and faithfully declare Christ's meseaee, without fear or favour, must be prepared to bear loss and suffering for his Master's sake.</p>
        <p>Bu'l the go.rpel. an now preached, produces very different results. The preacher; aro mlrch roteemed for their labours, their sermonn afforl no ground for offence or rer-peutirm ; and so the world and the Church move cn qnietly together to their mutual advantage. Several °awes mi2bt b.% assigned for this pleasant sta'e of things. but 1 must csonfine mynelf to ens only. It seems that both Churchmen and N-noonformista are agreed that "It is lawful for Christian men, at the command of the magistrate. to wear weapons and nerve in the wars." (Articles of Religion. No. xxxvii.) In my view, as expressed above, this dogma is nbnolutelv Anti-christian besides being a at contradiction of the divine and unrepealed law. "Thou shalt not kill." But it is very popular because it panders to two leading principles of our Poor human nature—oafishness and combativeness. motives as about opposed to Christianity as midnight is to noon. When our pr-achers cease to inculcate this warrior-doctrine, and devote their efforts to promote peace an good-will among their fellow-men they may expect God's blessing upon their worlr. 13,t to invoke that blessing on the horrors and carnage of the battle- field is ansureily not the duty of a mini3ter of Christ.— Yours truly, lONA.</p>
        <p>March 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>•....... INQUESTS AT RUNCORN. The Missing Man Catterall.</head>
        <p>•....... INQUESTS AT RUNCORN. The Missing Man Catterall.</p>
        <p>Coroner Dobson, held an inquest at the P.uncorn Police Court, on Monday afceracon, on the bedy ns James Catterall, who hag been miseicg from his home 71,Yerk- street, since the 7ch of January, and whose body was picked up in the Bridgewater Canal, it Saturday, in a very advaneed stage of decomposition. Mrs. Jane Ingleefield, 31. Parker-street, Runcorn, widow,swore the body just vievred by the jury was that of her cousin James Catterall, whom she last saw on the 7th of January last. She was deceased'a houpekeeper, from the time his wife died in August, 1899. Since that oeceurrence, her cousin had been in low sririts. On the 7ch of January, Catterall had hie meals throughout the bay in his usual manner, and left the house at 6 o'clock in the evening since which time she had not seen him Witnesi, though she could not Rea much of her cousin in the features of the deceased, was sure it was him from the clothes and the letters "J.C." tatoeed on his left arm. He was 67 years of age and a shipwright. John Cewley, 49, Surrey-street, Runcorn, testified to being a lock-keeper, in the employ of the Bridgewater Canal (M.S.C.Co.), and had charge of No. 9 lock on that waterway. About 9.30 on Saturday, immediately after paining% flat through the lock he erticed that the gates would not come together. and thought a fender had got between. He felt with a " shaft," and as a result ultimately brought deceased's body to the surface. He handed the body ovcr to Constable Bowyer. The jure, of which Mr. William Shaw, missionary, was the foreman, returned a verdict of " Found drowned." Sudden Death of a Labourer.</p>
        <p>At the Police Court, Runcirn, Coroner Dobson held another inquiry, immQdiately following the one given above,on this occasion till subject being TO3MaS Simpson labourer, of Halton-road. Halton, who died rather sod- denly at five o'cls7,ck on Monday morning.—Mr. Shaw, again acted as foreman, and the same jury agreed to sit. George Simpson, nephew of the deceased gave evi- dence of identification. as uncle was a labac.rer, aged 48 years, and unmarried. On Sunday last, he got up as usual and had breakfast. About half-past twelve he went out to the " Halfway House," where be had a glass of porter, and upon coming back, be complaitpd of be- ing unwell, and of having severe pains across the chest. Subsequently he went to bed and had some milk and tea. He, however' died at 5 o'cl,.:ck on Monday morn- ing.---Questioned by the Coroner as to why a doctor was not sent for? Witness replied that deceAsed's mother and Isimself did not think he was that bad, and had done all they could for him. Ddceasfd when asked by them how be was getting on had not desired a doctor. , Dr. J. B. Delaney, Runcorn, said he had examined the deceased's body externally, and there was nothing of note. He also made a pot-mortem (xamination. On opening the thorax he found rho lungs adherent to the chest walls, especially on the left side. The right apex of the lung was also adherent and completely collapsed. Deceased had a vory fat heart, and there was a super- linens deposit of fat around nap heart. His (minim vns that deceased died from " pleuritis (inflammat'on of the lining of the lungs) and failure of the heart." Witness should like to add, also, that the liquor deceased bad had that day, had nothing whatever to do with his !death. The juiy returned a verdict in accordance with the tn edical ovidence. Sold</p>
