      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>RELIEF OF LADYSMITH BY</p>
        <p>LORD DUNDONALD-</p>
        <p>The following telegram has beea posted at the War Office :</p>
        <p>From Sir Redvera Buller to Secretary for War. Lyttelton'a Headquarters, March 1, 9-5 a.m.</p>
        <p>Dundenald,with Natal Carabineers and composite regiment, entered Ladysmith last night. The coun- try between me and Ladysmith is reported clear of the enemy. lam moving on Nelthorpe.</p>
        <p>Queen Congratulates Generals Buller and White.</p>
        <p>When Her Niajesty received the news of the re- lief of Lldysmith she ordered a telegram of con- gratulation tl General Buller and General White to be devatched.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Relieved Garrison.</head>
        <p>The Relieved Garrison.</p>
        <p>Sir George White is believed to have with him at Ladysmith a force of about 8,000 men, the greater numberof whom were landed in Natal from India before the outbreak of hostilities. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>FREE STATE.</head>
        <p>THE FREE STATE.</p>
        <p>British Said to Have Reached Bloemfontein. The Press Association says a report is prevalent in London that the British have reached Bloemfon- tein.</p>
        <p>The news emanated from a generally reliable Fay. feeling very bad. It is all right singing and play- source, but no official telegram to this effect has vinz iderhsaovfetthheoQuguhete7i' bwuatsi! synolud ihearsd osfe esti lc nnyesbs s I been issued by the War Office, never saw such a sight. I will write again soon. Sail from here t'-morrow. Remember ug to the Mayor, Dr. Re-occu patio n of Ooles berg. Adamq, Mr J Crosiield, Mr Hulse, Mr Baldry. and all the Message from Lord Roberts. boys. We had a parade at 10-30, when the Colonel gave us a knife and several other things." • The folio win has been posted at the War Office from Lord R)berts to the Secretary of State for The Embarkation of the Volunteer War: Scene on Board.</p>
        <p>Paardeberz, 8,50 pm., February 28.</p>
        <p>The following extracts are taken from a letter which was received on Sunday morning by Mr. Thomas Arm- strong, bandmaster of the Ist V.B. (P W.V) South Lan- cashire IV giment, from his niece :—.Aftlr describin the measures wh;ch were taken to obtain admission to the s.s. Avondale Casqe the writer says : "The Avondale CasVe is a splendid boat, not the ordinary troopship, so th it will be more comf 4rtah'e I d 4resay. There were a few ordinary nassengers on bard, perhaps correspon- dents. There ty,re not a great many peonle to see the Warrington Volunteers off, I shonli 14.4 y la3tween two tin d three hundred, but there may have heel more. The place being so extensive makes .folks look scarce. All the friends were aristocratic looking and such swells. They mostly came in carriages, go that shows the volun- teers come of good society. It will be a splendid education for them. I shall never forget the glorious sight as the huge pale blue ship moved from the harbobr with one thousand and thirty animated forms on board, some in khaki and just a few in red coats to relieve the scene ; the rays of sunshine on the beautiful seater, and a cornet playing "Soldiers of the Queen," " Au Revoir" " Auld lang syne " and the National Anthem. They on hoard, and we in return waved handkerchieFs until the ship was out of eight. They left the clack about 3 pm." • Warrington Men with Buller's Forces "An Awful Sensation."</p>
        <p>General Clements reports that on hearing - Coles- berg had been evacuated he sent a force to occupy Colesbarg Junction, and rode into Colesberg, where he received an enthusiastic welcome, secured a certain amount of ammunition, and arrested several rebe's. He then returned to Rensburz. He reported the railway line clear and working to Lanewelew - man's Siding. Will report to-morrow as to few culverts which have been blown up. Colesberg and Colesberg Junction are held by our troops..</p>
        <p>Brabant's Operations. Heavy Losses.</p>
        <p>The report is confirmed that Brabant has recap- tured Jamestown, and adds that tbd Boer losses were 50 killed, 123 wounded, and 300 :missing. Enemy disheartened.</p>
        <p>Private James Makin, lst Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, writing from Frere Camp, Natal, January 26th, to his mother,who lives at 23, Gee-street.Warrine- ton, says :—"I have the pleasure of writing to you these few tines to let you know that I landed quite safe• after a pleasant sea voyage. The firat place we stopped at was Madeira for coal We left again on Sunday, January Bth, ablut mid-day, and arrived in Canetown on Saturday. January 20th. Then we left for Diu•ban on January 22nd, arrived on Wednesday, January 24th, and disembarked on Thursday, caught the train for Pietetmaritzbur, and arrived into camp the same even log at seven o'clock. We only had a quarter of an hour's walk from the station to the camp. We stopped there one day and night, and then left for Frere, where we have only been two daye and nights, as we had to leave the folowirig morning for Spearman'a Camp. We have to walk 25 MilPB—that is where our regiment is— and that is the main fighting line to get into Lady- smith. Three of our officers and a number of men bn- longing to C and D companies, were killed or wounded, and General Woodgate, commanding the Lancashire Bailments, was severely wounded. It is an awful sergation to hear the cannons roaring and the bullets flying about in all directiona,but by the time you receive my letter I shall be in the thickest of the fight, and I hope I shall come out safe, But if I have to di:, nut here 1 shall die a British soldier's death, and not a coward's. We will let the Boors see what the Warrineton lads can do. I have no mere te say at present, rattly give my best I• veto my brother and sister,and not forgetting vont-salt—l remain your true and loving son, JAMES MAKIN. Treatment of Soldiers on Board Ship. "Almost Semi-Starved."</p>
        <p>Writing from Naauwonor', South Africa. Private J. R 1470746, of the 2nd Gloucester Regiment sends us the following :—" I will just eive you a little disry as re- gards the living we got on board H. M. s.s. Cymric en route to South Africa. The food supplied to us was something scandalous and almost unfit to eat anti very scarce at that. We embarked at Liverpool at the Canada Dock and all the trnops were in high spirits when they saw the kind of s:ip we were going out in. We were treated very well while we lay in the dock and also the following day, taut afterwards it told a different tale. We simply got bully beef and biscuits for days and then by way of a change we had biscuits and salt junk. We wee %Imo,* semi-etarved aid were glad when wel•eached our journey's end. Occasionally we had breadfand then it was'nt the best. We made a complaint about it as it was almost sour at times, but as usual without satisfac- tion, and we had to make the best of it. As you may guess the greater portion of the bread went through the port holes. We had a few luxuries dur ng our three werks voyage. We had salt butter three timee, also fresh meat as they called it, three times,but that was'nt all. What we could never make out was this, we could not get fresh meat or bread given to us, but early in the morning you could buy bread at vo-ar own (miens" and at night-time you could get fresh potatoes and fresh meat by paying for the same at the rate of 4(1, per plate. So you can see plaiely there most be something radically wrong somewhere and to make it worse we were charged 41 per diem "messing money"—antually paying for our own rations and net receiving the same. It stands to reason the parties that sold the suppers, breal,etc..knew something about our rations and I myself think it is a disgraceful and acandalous way to treat soldiers en, route to a foreign clime and I think such a disgraceful state of affairs on board this ship should be ousted out. We don't mind biscuits and bully beef now we are out here; we would almost put up with anything. but I think things should be looked after better en board a ship.—l remain your obedient servant, Private JR. Lyons, 2nd Gloucester Regiment, Field Force, South Africa."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Relief of Ladysmith• Rejoicings at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Relief of Ladysmith• Rejoicings at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The glad tidings of the relief of Ladysmith reached Runcorn shortly after ten o'clock and was hailed with great enthusiasm by all classes of the community. By direction of the Rev. Canon Mait- land Wood, the bells of the Parish Church sent forth a joyous peal at intervals throughout the day, the steam buzz..rs of the varions works sent forth their discordant notes for a prolonged period and there were salvos of fog signals on the railway while somewhere in the vicinity cannons were fired. With a rapidity that reminded one of a trans- formation scene the town was gay with flags,banners and bunting of every shape and hue Scarcely a house that did not display some token of jubilation At the various schools the children were made ac- quainted with the news and then fresh young voices gave forth ringing cheer after ringing cheer. and the patriotic songs "Soldiers of the Queen," and "Rule Brittania ' were sung. In the afternoon the whole of the schools were given a holiday. As if by common consent the principal tradesmen quickly "shut up shop," one adding to the formal announcement of" chased" the explana- tion "Too pleased to work." At the meeting of the Urban District Council the members mutually con- gratulated each other and the Chairman observed that General Buller deserved all the success that had been gained and onght to have the credit for it. He hoped be would get it. (Hear, hear.) The Death of Lieutenant 114‘Corquodale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>"Joined in the Evening, Shot at Dawn."</head>
        <p>"Joined in the Evening, Shot at Dawn."</p>
        <p>Mr. Winston Churchill. the special correspondent of the " Morning Post " writing from Ventel-'s Spruit Camp, on January 29, thus descrilies the pathetic death of the young Lieutenant M'Corquodale :—I will only relate one other incident—a miserable one ; the (lay before the attack on Spion Kop I had chanced to ride across the pontoon bridge. I heard my name called an 4 saw the cheery' fac'. of a boy I had known at Harrow, a smart, elean-looking young gentleman, quite the ronab material for irrenlar horse. He had just arrive, had yuphed his way to the front. Ilene&amp; so he said "to get a j ,b." This morning they told me that an unauthorised PreFs correspondent had been found among the killed up the summit ; at least they thought at first that it was a Press correspondent, for no one seemed to know him. A man bad been found leaning forward on his rifle—Jea.-. A broken pair of field glasses shattered by the same shell that had killed tbe'r owner bore the name " MacCorquodale." The name and the face flew together in my mind. It was the last joined subaltern of Thorneycroft's Mounted Infan- try. joined in the evening, shot at dawn. uoor, gallant young Enfriishman ; he had soon " got his job." The great sacrifice had been requi..ed of the Queen's latest recruit.</p>
        <p>A Runcorn Soldier's Chum Missing.</p>
        <p>Private F Bradbury, D Company, South Lancashire Regiment, writes t., his parents at Runcorn from Hat- tings Camp, under dare January 30th :—" Our divisi has been in action for tte last ten days, and we hav:l lost fully 1,000 officers, non-commissioned officers, and men, either killed, wounded, or missing, Private Spencer that chum of mine, being among the missing. Our brigade made a night march to Solon lin), getting there about 4 a.m. on the 24th completely surprising the enemy, who rushed from the positizn which we charged with fired bayonets. But the enemy who was about 1,000 strong, sun rallied, and then the guns began. The enemy had three big guns, and they soon began to shell us with deadly effect. There were men of curs with legs and arms blown riff, and our attillery could do no good because we could net get the guns up the hill, it was so steep ; in fact we had to take the ammunition off the mules' backs and carry it up the hills ourselves, S) you can judge for yourself what the hill was like. We lost 500 offieeis, non-commissionei officers, and men on that day alone,two officers and 19 men belonging to my company. I can tell you that the 24th day of January will be a day neve to be forgotten by the men who are lucky eneugh to get back hr me. General Buller inspected us yesterday, and congratulated uson our splendid behaviour and bravery, telling us that our division had practically given him the key to Lads smith, which we hope t relieve by the timeyou receive this letter " It be remembered we published Private Spencer's last letter the other week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>An Earlestown Man's Experiences. "Officer Nearly Dropped us into a]</head>
        <p>An Earlestown Man's Experiences. "Officer Nearly Dropped us into a]</p>
        <p>Boer Trap."</p>
        <p>Private A Jenkinson, of Eirlestown, now one of the Rifle Brigade with General Buller. writes as follows to a friend, Mr. Thomas Bugle. 11. Ilaydock-street. Earles- town :—Frere Camp, 21st January. Dear Chum,-1 would have written you earlier only I heard that there was a large number of your fellows coming out. So I thought rerhaps you might be amongst them, but if you are not you have not much to be sorry for. I can assure you. We are getting knocked about ever ,since we landed in this country. We are right up at the front, and have been for three week, and are at present stationed at Frere, ten mil( s from the Boer stronghold— Colons°. They are very strong in this place, and every morning we look out and have a "pop "at them. On the 15th January we made a general advance on Colenso marching out of our camp at 7.30 a.m halting at Chieveley Camp to try'and get a bit of bu'ly beef and a biscuit down, which we called dinner. While we were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Spion Kop Battle. Corpl. Floyd's (Ist S.L.R.) Terrible Experiences. _ _</head>
        <p>The Spion Kop Battle. Corpl. Floyd's (Ist S.L.R.) Terrible Experiences. _ _</p>
        <p>Copy of letter from Corp'. A. Floyd Ist South Lance Fhire Regiment. Fair VitrA, South Africa, Sunday January 28, 1900 My dear frieads,—l take the pleasure of answering your welcome letter as lam in a very anxious state of mind at present as wo have been starianed on a hill for ten days watching the Bosra and we have had a lot of fighting and I must say it was horrible to see the pOO- - cowling down on stretchers some with leza and arms off and shattered. We got to officersd and 'three men. with 20 wounded and 15 missiog and we con- sider we are very lucky. General Warren got a lot If provisions into Ladysmith for General White. The Bier losses must have been terrible for when we got into their trenches they were filled with dead. Oh such a terrible sight you could never see in a lifetime. Sergeant Jack Nolan (Mounted Infantry) had a very narrow escape as his company did the charge up an almost nerpqraicular hill. On our right flank the Boers had a "Buck•ur"gurt, and it did awful execution amongst the Lancashire Fusiliers whcse Colonel was taken prisoner and one General was shot. I don't kne w 'hether he was killed or not. His name is Woodgate. Jack McNab is sending me some cigarett,s, which I have thanked him for and I must thank you for the writing paver or I would not have been able to write to you. I have not had a letter from home yet, and it seems an age since I left Preston, causei I suppose by anxiety, and having no Proper:restln fact we have not slept in a tent for 14 dsys and have had nothing to cover u.-=. You say Harry com- plains about not being ordered to the Front, believe me, if he only knew what hariships we were undergoing I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 2. 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 2. 1900.</p>
        <p>enjoying this fine meal our artillery were banging away' at the Boers as also were the naval brigade. As soon as we had finished with the bully teef we got orders to , advance, the idea being for U 3 to make an attack on the front position, while oor main body got round the left flank, and we had the 21st Slots on our right. Altoget- her we coverkl nearly t bree miles of front. so von m.v euesd what s finel,ne it was. So on we marched, think ing to draw the Boers out,but they wer not having any for they appeared to realize that their beet plan was to lock after our main body on their fi mit., and who were troubling them above a little. While this was going on our commanding officer nearly dropped us into ,a trap, for there were a large number of Boers just stealing down on us. The artirery men were the first tl notice the sly Beers, and promptly gave them a surprise in the form of leddite shell, which kept them quiet for a bit ; but only for a bite for they replied with a shell as we were closing on our right. It went over us and nearly upset our naval guns. The Beers were displea e when their shells were ineffective, and tiry bangel away at us at random. Our navy then started. and I (could see it meant" bis." The artillery, too, gave them "sees," and they very quickly had the &amp;yrs in their trenches in one volume of smoke frem the lyddite, the shelling lasting until 4.50 p.m. Then we got theprder to retire, but before getting clear the Boers sent us another shell, but, which, fortunately, buried itself in the hill- side, se away we w-sta!*, leaving the artillery and the navy to keep the Beers back and kelp up the she ling. ' which they did until dark, Tom, this was our first' brush with the enemy, and a mighty stiff one it was Just before closing the letter I thought I would leave it open until I came off duty, and this morning I have got some more news. We have just nabbed 25 prisoners from the front, and a nice lot they are. Most of them are our own countrymen—well Irishmen. You may be .nrprised at this, hut it is the truth. The clowns are fighting against U 9 daily, but they wi I get • Home Rule' shortly when our relief column lands in Ladysmith, which won't be very lone now,asWP have captured some Boer positions at Colense. Our main body is also doing very well, so it is twenty to one that you will hear of the relief of the beleaguered garrison before you read this letter; certainly before it reaches you we shall be in the thick of it." Warrington Ambulance Men En Route. "Feeling very Bad." Captain Wiring, of the Warrington Ambulance Corps, has received the following from Mr J G Buckley, one of the members of the corps who volunteered for service in South• Africa :—" Queenstown, February 24th. 1900. Dear Captain,—Just a few lieos as promised. We</p>
        <p>arrived he.Te this morning about 10.50. Out I am sorry to</p>
        <p>clumsy he would be glad that they are not fer the war as it i terrible to face death and not be prepared for the after. I have prayed every night and morning since I sAtne to this place. Oh I wiAo I had taken notice of you instead of being so headstrong and foolish. Would to God I could be like you, I am praying all day long but it seems as if lam lost when I try to pray. When I saw the polr fellows getting buried I wondered how many were saved. sonic dying with a curse on their lips. Oh it was terrible to think about the poor fellows. It it awful thinking you might be cut off by a single or a etra7 bullet. Last Saturday Is tw all the fighting and whEr. our artillery fired snella into the Her position you could see men being blown to atoms when the shell bursr,ed, and then yon would see the Boers roe. Last Wedne,day nigh ; we made a night attack on the Boers' peati.,n, an i when they heard the order " Fix Bayonets," they scampered off like lightning. They g don't like bayonets and c.not face cold steel. Every- thing is at present very quiet after the heavy fightine. and I can% t-ll you any more news this time an mu,t cmiclude with lo?st love, sour affectionate friend, ALP FLOYD</p>
        <p>'Ho'd the Fort for I am coming," said the Hello, Quick as lightning came tin Question. " Ain't you coming slow."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Attack on Spion Kop. Graphic Description by a Warrington Sergeant. "Dying Like Rotten Sheep."</head>
        <p>The Attack on Spion Kop. Graphic Description by a Warrington Sergeant. "Dying Like Rotten Sheep."</p>
        <p>The following graphic acclunt of the attack on Spion Kop hao been received by Mr. Atherton, from a War- rington reaervist lately employed by the Pearspn and Knowles C :al and Iron Co. at Dallam Forge. The writer is a sergeant in the lit Battalion South Lancashire Regiment :</p>
        <p>Two miles from Dewdrop, South of the Tugela River, January 29th, 1900</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Ath,rton,—ln this letter I wiil give you a short account of our doings from the time of crossing the Tugela Iti ger. We starte 1 trom Springfield at night (about seven o'clock), and reached the lueela at nine the following morning. Our engineers sous had a pontoon bridge thrown across, and the divaion was well on the other side betore the enemy dropee I a atoll amongst us. Un- fortunately it killed one of the Dewing, but that Was not much consi tering we expected a great struggle before we got across. Our artillery soon made them pick their feet up. Then we commencsd getting the convoy over. It extended over seven miles To give you a faint idea of the length of t, convoy, I have only to tell you that each waggon is drawn by 16 bullocks, and when travelling, I should say each waggon covers at least 20 yards of ground. Our division nunibered between 30,000 and 40,000 troops when we left Spring- field, thee they were split up into three armies. We were under the command of General Sir Charles Warren, with General Woodgete as our Brigadier. We (the Lan- cashire Brigade) halted at Fair View, in front of the centre of tne enemy's position. They hold a range of hills for thsir position which, it is said by our generals, knocks Sebastopol into a cocked hat. In front of it is a plain about two miles deep, and I think, if we had attacked it from the front, that none of U 3 would lease got within 1,000 yards from their position before being killed. It is simply appalling to look at these hills. Our regiment was detailed as escort to the artillery. Thelrish Brigade under General Hart, with par-, of the Lancashire Brigade, commenced the battle by attacking on the left. The artillery (on oar hills) Commenced Vying them their physic, which was replied to by the Bo r artillery. We got into the trenches, and very soon shells were bursting all around us. Ours was a chronic job, doing nothing, and no doubt you will agree with me when I tell you that being escort to the artillere, we have to lie low, and not move, so as to be ready to resist any attack made on the guns. Shells after shells were bursting but we got out of it lucky (about 16 wounded.) The brigade attacking the left ultimately drove the enemy from their position, but it was not in my opinion. worth th , sacri- fice, until I hsard what General Buller's idea was in sending out to make this flank attack. His idea was to make the enemy to extend their front so that he would he able to push into Ladysmith from Uhieveley. - This fighting was carried on from Sunday until Wednesday, January 24th. Then the order came at dusk for the Lancashire Fuel- Hers, Royal Lancasteis, a secticie of Engineers, and two companies of our regiment to prepare for the road. I belonged to one of the two companies selected from our regiment. It was pitch dark when we started, with General Woseigate in command. On, on, on we marched in silence, not knowing where we were going ; but sus- pecting something desperate was about to happen. We knew we were approaching the enemy's lines, when a dog, belonging, I nelieve, to the Lancasters, gave a growl, and commenced howling. Somebody hit it on the head with the butt end of et --rifle, and then cut its tbro it. We lay flat on the ground, not knowing whether we had alarmed the Boers. In about five minutes we crept along as slowly as pos- sible for fear ef disturbing these Dutch fiends, until we were at the foot of a hill called Spion Kop. Then we began to real as what was expected from us. We were to attack Spion Kop. It is the highest hill around the district, commanding every other position. It took us two hours and a half to get up to the top. I believe. it is between six and seven thousand feet above sea level Providence favoured us while we were asceeding it, as we were enveloped in a thick fog. The Boar sentry shouted out tbe challenge, but he got his answer by Weing a bayonet driven through his ribs. Then we heard tmllets whizzing past us. "Fix bayonets and charge' was our order. We rushed along the flat top, yelling like devillemly to find that the Boers had skipped leaving their tackle behind them. This would be about 3.30 a.m. We gave a good ringing cheer, as we thought we had captured this position without loss of life. I had a walk round the hill to see what it was like. It has a round, flat top about 200 yards diameter, with a gradual slope to the Boer position, but steep and rocky leading to our position. The two sides have a slope down for about 20 yards, then the sides are perpendicular. Our men started to make. trenches. Myself with 20 men, were sent to throw out a lice of skirmishers to the right front, and an officer and twenty men to form a similar line to the left front. We bad scarcely got into position,when we were greeted with a shower of bullets, which for the moment com- pletely staggered us. I gave the order to my men to keep under cover until we could find out where they • were coming from; but it was no use, they were coming as thick as hail stones. We retired to the trenches (poor ones, as they had not had time to finish them) one by one, creeping along the ground. Up to this time not a shot had been fired by the British, as the fog was too thick. We were waiting to charge the devils. At 530 a.m. the fog began to lift, we could see them in trenches, but we had no guns to root them out. Now and then ore would show himself, and generally when they did show they soon got a ticket for eternity. The fog had completely lifted at 6 0 a.m.,and from that hour the top of Splon Kep was hell on earth. There we were perched on the top of a fiat hill without a gun to assist us. Shrapnel' shell after shell was bursting all around us,and our men where dying like rotten sheep, and we had nothing but our rifles to help us, and nothing but rocks to aim at. The trenches wore soon full of dying and wounded men. Oh, it was horrible, men with pieces of shell in their sides, all ablaze others with their legs shattered, some with their beads nearly blown off. At about 9 a.m. our General:wasshot through the right eye, and his Brigade Major killed alongside him. It would be toe sickening to relate individual eases. Enough to say that I saw sights that day which will never be blotted from my memory. Another thing that tended te add to our misery was, we had no water. It was a blazing het day. Men were lying baking in the sun w ,untied and crying for water, and we had none to give them. We had the hospital (wi h large white flags with the red cross on to denote that it was a hospital), where we dragged our wounded, but they dropped sbell after shell right in the midst of it. For 17 long hours we had to face the music. At about three o'clock in the afternoon we were reinforced by the Middlesex, and they were being mowed down like corn. It was not infantry we wanted,hut artillery, and the hill was too steep to get guns up. Colonel Thorneycroft took command late in the afterncon, and at night he asked me if I would vo'unteer to accompany him to every trench, so that he could give the order to retire on a certain point. Of course I said "Yes, sir," but I can assure you before we got half way round we came to the conclusion that it was a warm job. I don't know how we got out of it safe. At that time I did not care a pin whether I was shot or not, I was so tired and . jaded, as I (in fact all of us) had had nothing to eat or `drink all day that I felt as if death would be a relief, but thank God I canoe out all safe. The retirement was conducted in a marvellous manner. When we came to the river we drank enough water to last us a week, I should think It was hard work to get men to leave it. By' Jove! I don't waist another day like that. Fiehting is all serene when you have 'euns to help you, but it'd a bit off when yen haven't. We want another go at them, and I think we shall have it before long. Including myself, there were five Pearson and Knowles' men on the hill that day. Two of them used to work for H . • . R . . . , named R . . . and L . . . , another named B . . . worked for Mr. J . . . . sheeting mill wagons,and K . who worked in T . . . Y . • . 's gang. K . . . came to me after it was over, and says: " Sergeant, it's mere comfortable unloading plates than here, isn't it ?" I agreed with him. He says he intends to come back to Denim if you will have him. The object General Buller had in view was that whilst we were engaging the enemy around Spion Kop he was to push a convoy into Ladysmith. Whether he achieved his object I can't say, as we are kept completely in the dark. We don't know as much as you in England Give my best wishes to Mr T . . . and J . also to Mr G . . . and Mr D . . . , and kindly, show this letter to R . . .in the office, who will con- vey my regards to the office stiff. If Ladysmith is re- lieved I expect we will go round to tbe Free States. as I firmly believe all the forces two European Powers could put in the field would not be muneessful by frontal attacks against the Boers, south of Majuba. Their positions art impregnable—With best wishes, yeure sincerely, --</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A MEC ing of this Board was held in the Board Room at the Workhouso on Wednesday morning. Mr. A. J. Jolley presided and there wc.re also present :—Mrs Clay, Mrs E relaters, Itrar W /1 Drummond, Messrs 'l' ii Ash- croft, El Hibbert, William Hickman, A Ja!ley, G Pilling, Forsha aa,T B Malkin, H Thornton, S Davies, J Clarke, J Ball, William Houghton, A Breaks, T Halton, J John- son, with J C Sutten (clerk), S Sutton (assistant c!erk), C U Ward (workhouse master),A Wright (superintendent Padgate schools). APPOINTMENT OF A NEW SUPERINTENDENT NURSE. RETIREMENT OF MISS WARD, The House Committee reported that the tender of Mr F G Rennie, for repairs and diverting drains of the Workhouse for the SUM of £44 10i was accepted. Four caodidates for theiorosoectivessperintendent asa- 8 rost were interviewed, and upon a vote it was agrred that Emily Davoll should be appointed. She asked for a few days in which to consider her answer. This was granted and upon a vote it was Wither agreed that in the event of her declining, Nurse A. M. M. Emerson should be appointed. At a meeting held on the 23rd Fabruary it was recommended haat Ann Langley be retired as from March 26th, and that the Committee be empowered to engage six assistant nurses in addition to the present staff, to commence duty at the opening of the new infirmary. The resignation of Miss Ward as schoolmis- tress was tendered te take effect from March 31st. Toe CHAIRMAN, in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the committee, said the lady who had been appointed to siacceed Miss Brown as superintendent had had a large exper:eoce and came to them with high testimonials, and he hoped her appointment would be couspicususly successful. With regard to the resigna- tion of Miss Ward (daughter of the matron) he frankly recogniz A thar, although he did not agree with the ap- pointment at the time, her work had been for the bene- fit of tho institution. The work she had done had been of great use, and he was sorry personally that she was about to leave them. Mr. Jolley also alluded to Mrs. Langley,the assistant to the matron and bore testimeny to her long and faithful services. Faithful services,whether the officer was Ugh or low, ought to be recognised, and he had pleasure in doing so in Mrs. Langley's case, If r-ver there was a wall-earned pension it was hers. Allud- ing to the six assistant-nurses to be engaged, the Chair- *lap pointed out that the present accommodation in the hospital was quite inadequate and that a number of c.ses were being dealtlwith in the houses that ought to be in the hospital.</p>
        <p>Mr. HicrArr seconded, and the minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>It was also decided, on the motion of rhe CRAIRDIAN, seconded by Mr. ALFRED JOLLEY, that Miss Daeod be appointed superintendent nune when Daces Brown leaves.</p>
        <p>INFIRMARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Infirmary Committee, held oe the 10r,h February, the plan prepared by the architects for the laying out of the land of the new Infirmary with new roads, walks and ground for the planting of shrubs was approved, and iestruetions Were given to obtain estimates for the necessary work in respect of the roads, walks, sett paving to boi!er and ermine houses and the formation of airing yards, in the ratter case obtsiciin alternative estimates for finishing with cinders or with asphalt. It was decided that the digging of the land where grass was to b) sown should be left for the present as possibly this might be done with lab err from the workhouse. It was decided that these estimates should be obtained and put before the Committee at a meeting to be held at the architects' office on Tuesdae, the 27tti February at 114a.m. It was moved by Mrs Clay seconded by Mr:A J Jolley and resolved that the Board of Guardians be recommended to empower the Infirmary Committee to arrlnge for the public opening of the new Infirmary buildings.</p>
        <p>Mr 130IIGHT0N, in moving the confirmation of the tninutes,said th architects seemed more or Itss saneuine that they wouli b 3 able to open the institution in M4y, although of course, they would not commit themselves to a definite statement on the matter. ' Mr HICKMAN seconded. Mr. THORNTON : Can anyone give the approximate amount spent on the new infirmary P The CHAIRMAN: I cannot tell exactly. _ .</p>
        <p>The ()Lena said the Board had borrowed £32,000 and as be plinted out when he submitted the estimates that left a deficiency Of Fomethinc like £2,000 or £3,000. They then calculated £l,OOO for furnishing but he was afraid that the coat of furnishing would be nearer £2,000. The Board would have to move slowly if they intended to keep the poor rate to sixpence in the £. (floar,hear.) he hoped that the balance] in favour of the Board wGuld enable them to pay for all that was required for the new infirmary without increasing the loan. The minutes were Urn passed. TflE CENTRAL POOR LW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr PILLING mooed the appointment ot the Chairman and the senior vice-chairman a 4 delegates to the Central Poor Law Conference to no held in London this month. He said the experience gained at the conference was very much to their advantage. _</p>
        <p>Mr HicxsiAN seconded, - aud the resolution was carried. THE BOARD'S SUBSCRIPTION TO THE NURSING ASSOCIATION. PROPOSED INCREASE REJECTED.</p>
        <p>The Rev W a Drummond in accordance with notice of motion, moved that subject to the approval of the Local Government Board the annual subscription of £lO to the Warrington Nursing Association be increased to £l5. In doing so be said it would be to the advantage of the Board to increase the subscription. There were some items of expenditure which really were not expenses because they saved the money in other ways. The Warrington District Nursing Association had already done a most admirable work among the very poor, and that work was only limited by the smallness of their staff. It had beets found necessary to increase the staff and in another month the nurses would migrate to a larger home where there would be increased expenditure. Et considerable number of the very poor were visited by the nurses, and by their skill the nurses shortened the duration of the illness and obviated the necessity of the Guardians giving help to the patients. The nurses had saved many lives in Warrington. One of he doctors told him of a case which be had practic- allv given up, and where recovery would have been im- possible but for the services of the nurses. By contribut- ing to the Nursing Association they were really contri- buting medical relief to the very poor in the best form possible, and saving money in other directions. War- rington was of course only part of the Union and many Guardians might feel that this was an institution from which Warringtoti; would only benefit. That was true but on the other hind he should be the very first to advocate that they should support nurses in any other part of the Union. If an alsociation were established on the same lines in any other township he would be only too glad to eupport them because he thought it was a work which was thoroughly in line with the best methods of poor law relief. Mr. ASHCROF Z; seconded the proposition. There was an impression in the town that the institution was not intended for the very poor but for the middle-class. That was entirely a mistake-</p>
        <p>_ Mr. CLARKE moved an amendment that the subscrip- tion be n,,t increased, and said be did so on account of the small area c)vered by the nursing association. It was not an institution which benefitted in any way the people of Newton, Earlestown, or Haydock. He was willing to admit that in the town where they exercisel their powers they were very useful and did very useful and good work. But the institution was not to be compared to the Manchester Eye Institution and the other institutionel receiving subscriptions from the Union, because they recoil? d patients from any part of the Union.</p>
        <p>Mr HOUGHTON said he followed Mr Clarke in opposi- tion to the proposrd inerease of subscription on the ground that the whole distriat had to contribute, and did not receive the benefit. Mr Drummond bad told them that the work of the Nursing Association was in- creasing, but as long as the committee could get money together they would find something to do with it. It was probable that they would be asked for a further subscription if they granted the proposed increase. He considered that they gave the institution a handsome subscription at present, and be hoped Mr Drummond would see his way to withdraw the proposition. He intended to bring forward a drastic resolution dealing with subscriptions to charitable institutions. Mr FORSHAW : How many institutions do we contri- bute to ? The CLERK : We have the consent of the Local Government Board to contribute to institutions at Bux- ton, Liverpool, ani Manchester.</p>
        <p>NINIr. HOT:ORTON But in the case of the Warrington Institution there is no benefit to the country districts. . The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said it was necessary that there should be a limited area for the Nursing Associa- tion. It was very desirable that there should be a Dis- trict Nursing Association iu each township, and that the Board should subccribe to them. On a vote being taken the resolutiol was lost, only four voting for it and eight against. VACCINATION RETURNS.</p>
        <p>The Cr„Rsz reZci; return showing the number of per- sons in the U.ion who had been vaccinated and the number of conscientious ohjetorg since the Act came, into force. There were 2,048 successful vaccinations and 28 conscientious objections. THE SERVING OF CHILDREN WITH DRINK.</p>
        <p>A-Cornaluniestion ws read from the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope and Temperance Union asking the Board to petition Parliam9nt in the following terms : .1 That your petitioners re;ard the practice Of permit- ting children to be slryed with intoxicating Num in public-houies as one fraaght with serious moral danger to the childrea thus served and pray your honourable house to pass a Bill prohibiting the sale of intoxicating liquors to children blow the age of 16 years." The CHAIRMAN sail it had always been the practice of the Board to abstain from tahing any action of that kind.</p>
        <p>Mr. THORNTON did not sel why they should abstain from taking action in a matter of that kind. He thought</p>
      </div>
