        <p>it was a very reasonable request that was made by the Band of Hope Union. They were suf- fering from the evils of intempi.rance. At the present mement thee wore spending large sums of tlie retereeteere' ineney in cenerquetice of the drink evi ,nd they ouelni, to do all le their powe^ to prevent the sale of driak ¶o persons antler eixteen pears of age. It wa.s a reeommendttion whieh c enmended itself to mete. people. It wis deplorthie to see children entering public-houses egein and again. He only read the other day of a man who on going to church saw six bears under fourteen sears of ace drunk. He thought as a Bo ad of Guardians they ought to do what they could to stop drunkenness amongst young people. They were t,eme c riled upon to speed the zatepayere' money on an Infirmary for the use of ;mere poor people who had been brought there throueli drink. He would move that the Board supp )rt the pet tion. The R-v. ISO 13. DRUMMOND seconded the proposition, and said it was a prop -r• thing to do. Be was not in favour of a great dell ef ml i c illaneous businein being treught hetore the Beard. There vres riot the slightest doubt but tnat towns like IV Arrington were every year manufacturing an enormous number of paupers by the ease with which children were allowed to enter public-heuses. Anyone wno had studied the subject would know that a great deal of tire mischief crime and misery was brought on by our carelessness in this matter. They had hone.; a great deal of our colonies lately and it would be a good thing is they could follow their example and pass a law prohibiting the sale of drink to children. He did not see why this country should be eo conserva- tive in the matter. They ought not to allow their chil- dren to be exposed to such a temptati .n. At the present time in the lower parts of cities ani towns all s elite of ilinnucetteets were held out to children to enter public hews's. 'This was net a question of whether one was a teetoteler or not. It was a question of whether they ^eddied to protect child life from one of the greate-it dangers. Mr HIBBERT pointed out that licensed victuallers were liable to be brought before the magistrates if they served childree under 14 years of age. Mr HOUGHTON objeete.: to the Board entering into matters with wnich it had nothing to du. On a vote being taken Mr Thorntoiee resolution was lost, and the petition was allowed to lie on the table. This was all the business. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Electric Lighting Question. The usual monthly ineenne of the above Cottacil toot i place at the Tewn H4ll, Rune.orn, on Mt:wily moreing. Mr. F Wiswall presided and there were present : Mesnis. Pritchard, Kirktiam, Greco, Wilson, Gatley, Jones, ltnison, Posreat. Deai., Liehthurn, Taylor, Handley, Bishrown, Lake (law clerk), Marshall (secretary), and ' Wilding (surveyor). THE INFLUENZA EPIDEMIC. The Medical Officer (Dr. McDodgali) repotted that during February there were 47 deaths registered ; four were inquest cases and of the remaining 43 14 were due to bronchitis a.nd 5 ti influenza. Tee deaths showed a death i ate of 29 6 per 1000 ; but exclusive of the in- quest cases the death rate was 27 per 1,000. Twenty nine births were registered during tee mouth. Th. re were 18 deaths anl 44 oirebs registered during the corresponding period last year. Mr Dean said that toe Council mieht congratulate themselves that there were no deaths from jute :tiou. diseese and the town had gone through the epidemic of luflueeza very lightly. Ins one Ler..cseire town the death rate from infl ienz a was 81. per 1000. The Chairman arid this was Bedford Leigh. Mr. Biebrown inquired whether iufluenze was on the increase. The Medical Officer replied that it was not a notiflible disease. The wave of influenza paneing over the country, had been very severe but it Was not infer- enz t but imprudenee that was the cause ef the mortality. The five cases in Runcorn reported under the head of influeoze were ready due to tne secondary cause pnea- rnonia. From his own experience he knew the disease was on the decline. In fact there was hardly any itifeenous disease in the town. (Rear, hear.) Toe Highway Committee's minutes were passed on the motion or Mr. Tay: or, seconded by Mr. Wilson. HEALTH COMMITTEE This committee rec imarenoed that. the tender of Mr. J. Dobson tor the supply of provender for the next six months be accepted ; and that legal proceedings he taken aeainst Samuel Worrell, of 4, Arthur-street, to re- cover the expenses in respect of the nursing anti main- tenaece of hie wife and two children in the hospital. Mr. Dean in moving these minutes said it would appear that the committee were dealing harshly with Worrell, but this was net so. Toe cost to the ratepayers foe his family in the hospital was something like £4O and the committee were only asking Worrell to pay £lO. 'The minute was altered accordingly. Mr. Lightburn secoeded the minutes, which were passed. EXTENSION OF THE WATER MAIN. 'The Water Committee recommended that the Water Maia be continued down Queen's-road. Mr. Pritchard moved this minute and said the work was not extensive and would complete the circulation ' between Green way-road and the Holloway. Mr. Kirkham seconded the motion, which was carried, TECHNICAL INSTRUCT/ON COMMITTEE. This Cernnaittee reported teat a letter had been received from the Society for University Extension in Liverpool and District asking the Council to elect a re- presentative upon the Council of the Society :—The Committee found that it was not at present feasible to establish University Extension Lectures in Runcorn, and therefore considered that there was no advantage in being represented on the Council. It was recommended 1 that the aforesaid Society be informed that it was not proposed to elect a representive at present. Mr. Wilson moved these minutes, and Mr. Handley in seconding, said the Library Committee were using their best endeavours; to get a coarse of Gilchrist lectures, and there was a likelihood they would succeed. '1 The minutes were approved. THE PURCKASE OF NEW BOOKi. The Library Committee recommended that 93 velumes of new hooks be parchased as per list submitted. Mr Imison suggested that the history of the Jubilee in Cheshire about to be published, should he purchased. Mr Handley said he did not know whether the funds of the committee would admit of this being done but be should have pleasure in presenting a copy. FINANCE. Oa the recommendation of the Finance Committee the offer of the trustees of the Loyal William the tourth Lodge of Oddfellows to lend the sum of £290 for five years at 3 per cent. interest, payable half-yearly, was accepted, and the Seal of the Council affixed to the deed. The money is required for: £lOO for strong room, £l9O for water mains extension. The Committee further recommended that £5OO be lent to the Bangor Corporation at three per cent. Mr Imison moved and Mr Pritchard seconded the minutes. Carried. THE OVERSEERS. Mr. Lightterrn proposed the re-appointment of Messrs. Imison and Taylor as overseers. Mr. Bisbrown see)nded and Messrs. Handley and Wilson supported, all speaking in highly eulogistic terms of the services and character of the two gentlemen. Mr. Headley suggested a. revival of the vestry meeting, bet Mr. Lake remarked that the powers of the vestry were now vested in the Council. The appointments were made unanimously. Mr. Imison acknowledged the complimentary remarks and the Council's confidence and said the overseers would inquire into the subject of the vestry meeting,in which the ratepayers took no interest in the past. Mr. Taylor also replied. THE EXPLOSIVE ACTS. The Clerk read a letter from the Home Office announc- ing that the Council's power under this Act would not be uedertaken by the County Council. THE FORTHCOMING ELECTION. The Law Clerk announced that the annual election for District Councillors was fixed for Saturday. March 31st. The retiring members are—Messrs J W Lightburn, J Wilson, G Hewitt, HJ S Taylor, and W THE POST OFFICE LAMP. Mr. Imison drew attention to the unsatisfactory way in sybice the Post Office lamp was lighted,and the Chair- man promised to personally den with the subject. The Council th a went into Committee.</p>
